      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>His Majesty's Autumn Arrangements.—The King has consented to hold the grand review of Scottish Volun- teers at Edinburgh, on September 18th. Only a short stay of a fe.v hoars will be made in that city, the King proceeding from the station to Holyrood, where be will breakfast, and, after the review, the journey to the North will he resumed. His Majesty will stay about two or three weeks on Deeside, during which, visits will be paid to Lori and Lady Barton at Glenctrisich. and to Sir Allan Mackenzie, of Glenminck. The King has a round of other visits to pay during the Autumn, and on his ieturn from Marienbad, will stay with Lord and Lady Savile at Rufford Abbey, in Nottinghamshire for the Doncaster race week. Liter on. his Majssty is to go on a visit to the Earl and Countess of Londes- borough, at Londesborough Park, and also to Lord and Lady Alington, at Crichel in Dorsetshere. The Royal Visit to India —When the Prince and Princess of Wales visited Australia, they travelled on the liner "Ophir" but their Royal flighnesses are to make the journey to India in the first-class battleship "Renown." One would think that the large and modern ocean liner would be the more comfortable, and less costly to transform into a Royal yacht, for the time being, but the "Renown" is considered a good sea- going ship, and the most extensive alterations are being carried out in her for the Royal voyagers. Though her big guns remain, the smaller ones have been removed, and when the final arrangements are made, she will be more like a palatial yacht, than a warship. The decks are being cleared as much *l4 possible, for dances and receptions, whilst the dining and drawing room, boudoirs, and sleeping apartments are being fitted up in the most elaborate manner, and decorated in white and gold. All the most np-to-date arrangements are being introduced and nothing will be omitted to make the "Renown "a most luxurious pleasure ship. The Ex-Empress of France.—The Ex-Empress E. genie of France is not far short of eighty, having been born in 1826, and those who are always with her cannot disguise from themselves the fact that she is showing all the signs of advanced age. Up to now she has enjoyed excellent health, in spite of the grief and sorrows which first began to fall upon her five and thirty years ago. She has only recently returned to Farn- borough after her yachting cruise on board the Thistle. For many summers past, the Ex-Empress has gone on a yachting cruise to the Mediterranean, but this year she has been to the North Sea and the Baltic, when, for the first time since the fall of her Empire she set foot on German soil. When on board her yacht, the Ex-Empress always has a hedgehog with her, which she regards as a protection against evils and accidents. Of late years much of her time has been taken up in writing her memoirs, which cannot fail to be deeply interesting, and will contain many, as yet, unpublished letters from her husband, and her ill-fated son. A Much-Prized Hat.—A reward of £SO for the recovery of a straw hat which has been lost or stolen, appears at first sight to be a little ridiculous, yet it seems such is the sum which the proprietor of a fashion- able cafe at Marienbad is prepared to give for the restoration of his missing headgear. It was only a straw hat, but it was made entirely of straws through which none others than Crowned Heads of Europe had refreshed themselvea with drinks at his establishment. It had taken him many years to collect a sufficient number of Royal straws, but finally the cherished object was attained, and the historic hat produced. It is now supposed to have been stolen by a waiter, and unless the very handsome reward offered proves effectual in its purpose, it looks as if the owner will have to begin collecting the much-prized strawsall over again. Modish Fabrics.—Autumn is to bring us a crop of grey materials, not distinct greys, but those having a green, blue, or brown tinge. For dressy wear, double cashmere—a lovely fabric, so soft and pliable that it would go through the proverbial ring—will be greatly seen, but for more useful knock-about costumes, loosely woven hopsack, with a "basket work" surface is likely to find much favor. Skirts are being designed in the new circular shape, and the bolero is still the most popular form of coat. About Collars. —We have become so accustomed to the comfortable flat collar, that the news which hails from Paris and Vienna to the effect that high Napoleonic collars are to appear on all the fashionable coats this coming autumn and winter, will not be altogether wel- come. But in this matter as in the majority of others, there are compensations. Firstly, the high Napoleonic collar protects the neck delightfully ; secondly it is less muffling, and objectionable,from a health point of view, than fur or feather boas ; thirdly and lastly it is the most becoming collar imaginable. As autumn advances it will be carried out in velvet, of a somewhat vivid shade, and richly embroidered. Hints on Washing Linen.—When washing a navy blue linen gown, or wrap, soap should never be used. A teaspoonful of ammonia, and two grated raw potatoes, the skin having first been removed—should be added to about one gallon of tepid water, and the linen wa3hed in this concoction. Next it must be rinsed in cold blue water, and then dried, and finally ironed on the wrong aide. A bag of bran should always be placed in the rinsing water when brown linen is washed, and the addition of a little hay is desirable when rinsing buff linen. Flower Names.—A decade or so ago it was the custom among the highly born to bestow upon their daughters, the name of a flower or plant, the coirequence being that at the moment we have quite a number of Violet, Ivy, Daisy, and Rosemary debutantes. And this name flower or plant plays an important part in the owner's life. Should it, for example. be Violet, then the modest blossom is embroidered upon the child's christening robe, and little bonnett ; at her confirmation a profu- sion of violets surround her ; when her engagement is announced baskets of violets are sent her by relatives and friends ; and at her wedding the violet again plays an important part. Many of the presents take some device suggestive of the violet, and every garment in her trousseau shews the monogram surrounded by a wreath of violets. In illness violets arrive from sympathising friends, indeed from the cradle to the grave the name flower is always greatly in evidence. Doubtful Cider.—Cider is steadily increasinc in popularity as a summer drink, but the sale would be enormously larger than it is, if it were not for the wide variation in quality. When cider is asked for, one never knows, except it is of some well-known brand or bottlers, what it will be like. It may be something approaching either to vinegar, or sugar and water, cud it is this uncertainty which prevents a great many people from asking for it. The cider business has been left very much to chance, and there is no uniformity concerning the kind of fruit employed, or its condition at the time of manufacture. The Rev. S. Baring-Gould tells of a farmer who sold all his cider to a firm of champagne dealers, and when orders were not forth- coming from that quarter, he did not make any. The same casual and indifferent methods apply to the great majority of cider-makers, but if the industry were organised, and pushed on business-like lines,there would probably be as large a demand for cider in the summer time as there is for the white wines of France. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>BRASS BAND CONTEST.</head>
        <p>BRASS BAND CONTEST.</p>
        <p>The brass band contest organized by the Runcorn Cycle Club in aid of the Cottage Hospital took place in the field adjoining the hospital on Saturday afternoon. The attendance of the public numbered about 500. The entrants were the Weston Point Works Band, Runcorn Amateur Brass Band, Runcorn Volunteer Band, St. Edward's Band, Runcorn, and Frodsharn Vonnteer Band. The test piece was the glee "Garland." The adjudi- cator, Mr. J. H. Harper, of Widnes, heard the bands without seeing them, and giving his awards as follows : —First (silver cup and £2), Runcorn Volunteers (con- ductor. Sergeant F. J. Walker) ; second (£2), Weston Point Works Band (conductor,Mr. Horace W. Handley); third (£l),Frodsharn Volunteers (conductor,Mr. Thomas Knight, Runcorn). Three gold centre mecills forsoloists were won as follows :—Solo trombone, Lance-Corporal Sadler, Rune arrl Volunteers; solo cornet, W.Macdonald, Weston Point Band ; and solo euphonium, C. Tudor, Prodsham Volunteers. Mr. W. Handley presided, and the prizes were distributed by Miss Wallace, matron of the Hospital. The winning bands afterwards plaFed for dancing on the Holloway recreation field, which was eat by Mr. W. Shaw, sen.</p>
        <p>".COMMERCIA.L FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp'i Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in En3land and Wales during the week ending Friday, August 11th, was 154, viz. : —New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, Si; deeds of arrangement registered, 70. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, (17; deeds of arrangement 82—total 149; being an increase of 5. The total number of commercial failures recorded during the 33 weeks of the present year is 5,736 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 33 weeks of last year was z),667 showing an increase of 69.</p>
      </div>
