      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR AI7USTRZD. The Examiner, WOMAN'S REALM. ISCIENCE NOTES ANDI THE FREE CHURCHES. GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR AI7USTRZD. The Examiner, WOMAN'S REALM. ISCIENCE NOTES ANDI THE FREE CHURCHES. GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>IA NEW EXPLOSIVE. A quite extraordinary combination of rimits is claimed in France for a new explosive, con- sisting of a mixture of powdered aluminium and I nitrate of ammonium. It is not liable to spon- taneous decomposition, or premature explosion by shock or friction, burns only with difficulty, not affected by frost or dampness, and the gases from its explosion are harmless. It can, be ex- ploded readily by an ordinary detonator.</p>
        <p>MINERALS ON THE PACIFIC COAST- A new and important sourc.6 of iron ore supply is said to have been found on the western coasts of America. It is believed that the black sands of the Pacific coast constitute the world's most valuable mineral source. Be- sides gold, platinum, chrornitc, monazite, zircon, topaz, garnet, rutile, and a great number of of other rare minerals, these sands are known to contain quantities qf magnetic iron ore.</p>
        <p>HOLIDAYS AND WORK. The holiday season is now drawing to a close, and soon the Free Churches will 190 making their plans for a vigorous „campaign in the Autumn. For the present, however, attention is more concentrated on our seaside and health resorts than to the work of cities and towns. At practically all the great seaside resorts, the Free Churches have combined for special efforts during the summer months. United Open Air services are held with considerable success, and every endeavour is made to reach the.class of people who are not usually in attemlance at places of worship. In addition to work +..f this kiud the holidays are now being utilised for gathering of workers.</p>
        <p>REV. F. B. MEYER'S CONFERENCES. • The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., has just con- ducted a series of Conferences at well-known resorts, starting at Morecambe. At this popular restirt Mr. Meyer presiried over five or six meetings. The principal items in the pro- gramme included a sermon by himself, an Early- Morning Praver-Meeting, a Conference over tea, a Free Church Demonstration, and a Talk to Mini:. term and Lay Preachers. This latter has been one of the great features of Mr. Meyer's work. For some two hours he talks in a .frank and brotherly manner to his fellow-n.inisters, giving them most practical hints, both on their own con- duct , of life and upon methods of work. Everywhere Mr. Meyer has gone lie has left a deep impregsion with the ministers. The Con- ference over tea. too, is a feature in which Mr. Meyer excels. Calling the local Secretaries to his side, he.puts them through a cross-examina- tion as to their work, the status of the Council, its finances, operations' &amp;c. This is done with his usual tact and humour, and helps the audience to realise, in a way not otherwise possible, chat Free Church Federation actually means. It can easily be realised that, with such a huge organisation as that possessed by the National Free Church Council it is frequently :necessary' to overhaul the inacinnery, to tighten the loose screws, and 840 that evt ry thing is in the state of high ell-icier cy. Mr. Meyer has done valuable work in this direction, and the Councils _locally are far stronger and bettor equipped as a result of his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME</p>
        <p>WOMEN AS SHOPKEEPERS.</p>
        <p>Women who think of turning shopkeeper might profitably study* the suggestions offered on this subject by Miss Alice Stronach in the Girl's Realm. Miss Stronach describes some of the most interesting and original ventures in trade. One lady, an artist and author,. has opened in the West-End a combined fancy shop and library, and she also makes a speciality of designing shop signs—for the signboard is quite coming back into fashion. Another runs a circulating library and a business in antiques in a fashionable Highland centre. Then there is a practically minded woman who has a charming and attractive restaurant in the artists' quarter of Chelsea, and she proposes to meet a "felt want" by trying to sell some of her customers' pictures. Miss Stronach advises that tea shops should now be opened rather in country towns than in London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHARMING COSTUME.</head>
        <p>A CHARMING COSTUME.</p>
        <p>in our sketch we have portrayed a charming little costume intended for early autumn wear. It is realised in fine white serge with magpie trimmings of narrow black silk braid and jet buttons. The coat is simple and tight-fitting, finishing below the narrow belt in a small basque. The braid is laid on in strappings over each shoulder, and further lines of it are carried down the fronts and round the basque, while a vest of black velvet, cut in vandvkes, finishes off the</p>
        <p>coat in front. The sleeves are full and short, and end in an ornamental cuff to match the decora- tion on the coat. The skirt is full and long, being gathered in round the waist, whilst the deep hem is made the excuse for a grouped band of the black silk braid. The economically minded could easily reverse this suggested scheme, and fashion the costume of hlack or navy blue serge with strappings of creard serge, which could also be relied on to give a smart effect.</p>
