      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Miss Deborah Volar, who so successfully sue• ceeded Miss Isabel Jay as " Veronique," at the Apollo Theatre, London, is only twenty-one. She studied singing with Mr. H. J. Wood and Mr. Richard Temple. Miss Volar made her debut two years ago as the Princess in "The Country Girl," at Daly's. The Crown Princess of Roumania, writes a resident, heal been little seen since she has taken up her residence at East Cowes, for she lives a very quiet life at Kynance, the villa she has taken for the season, just outside the park of East Cowes Castle. She is devoted to her chil- dren, and quite unhappy when parted from them for the shortest period of time.</p>
        <p>The engagement is announced of Lord Tenter. den to Miss Turner, daughter of Sir Alfred Turner. The future brickgroom is still on the right side of forty, with a house in Bryanston- square, but no country place. His stepmother, Lady Tenterden, is one of our finest bridge players. A number of women of Indiana. U.S.A., are preparing for the establishment of a training school for the special benefit of all who are to enter into service in private households. Ulti- mately it is intended to extend the plan until a similar institution shall have been founded in every State in the Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>GIRL'S PRETTY AUTUMN COSTUME.</head>
        <p>GIRL'S PRETTY AUTUMN COSTUME.</p>
        <p>Our illustration shews a pretty autumn cos- tume for a girl of fifteen or sixteen. The mate- rial chosen for its achievement is a' rough serge of a rather bright shade of navy blue. The neat little coat is of the bolero form, and can be worn either open or closed. Two shaped, stitched straps outline the neck of the coat, and finish in pointed tabs on the front, each point being adorned by three pretty but- tons. The fronli of the coat consist of two pieces of serge laia one over the other and firmly stitched down; the back is similarly treated. A -• - - -</p>
        <p>Lady Rose Mdlyneux left all her own money, amounting to about two thousand a-year, to her sister, Lady Gertrude Crawford, says the Onlooker. The money for the Abb4ystead estate, which was only hers for her lifetime, goes back to the Earl of Sefton.</p>
        <p>little stitched band and fancy button make a neat finish to the leg-of-mutton sleeve. The skirt is plain to the knees, where it is cut into a succession of tabs with wide spaces between, each tab bearing three buttons on its pointed end. From beneath each tab come four or five little kilted pleats, the intervening spaces being filled by wide box-pleats. . The belt of patent leather, cravat, and the scarf twisted round the crown of the boat-shaped hat are all of a vivid scarlet, a very pretty contrast with the blue of the costume. A CONGO FASHION NOTE.</p>
        <p>The native ladies in the Congo Free State have been made happy by the introduction of an entirely new and becoming garment. Leather pouches used for carrying the mails be- tween Belgium and the Congo are being much much worn by the dusky belles, the prevailing fashion being to cut out the end of the bag and fasten it around the waist by straps. The Brussels postal authorities have just discovered this interesting solution of the mysterious dis- appearance of the bags.</p>
        <p>feft)' • ~.;,.,1,21v , , • 47,2- 47411 .• \ , p ' g --A. - . : '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>- - - - PARFAIT OF PEACHES.—Stone half-a-dozen large ripe peaches, blanch the kernels, add them to a gill of water arid I.lb. of loaf sugar, which boil to a syrup. Whisk the syrup with the whites of two eggs in an egg-bowl over a sauce- pin of boiling water. Pound the peaches with a spoonful of apricot jam and rub them through a hair-sieve; add them gradually to the syrup. Then whisk it till it is cold, whisk in a gill of stiffly-whipped cream, and turn it in the freezer till it is nicely frozen. Serve it at once in tall glasses with wafers on a separate plates</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FIRST THERMOMETER.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST THERMOMETER.</p>
        <p>According to the Abbe Mollett, the first ther- mometer was invented by a peasant named Drebbel, of North Holland. Drebbel's ther- mometer was composed of a vertical glass tube, ending at top in a bulb, while the lower end was plunged into a vessel filled with water or some coloured fluid. When the bulb warmed the ex- panded air within it drove back the water. When the air cooled again the external pressure caused the liquid to rise again in the tube. The mem- bers of the Accademia del Cimento soon sub- stituted for this hardly practicable apparatus the more convenient instrument we still use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>DANGERS OF CHEAP LEATHER.</head>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF CHEAP LEATHER.</p>
        <p>The danger attending the use and wearing of adulterated leather is not perhaps fully realised. A large amount of the cheap leather is weighted with glucose and barium., especially the latter. so that when the weight test is applied, such adulterated leather may pass as first-quality material. Leather so treated, however, has the peculiar quality of absorbing moisture freely and retaining it to an extreme degree. The resu;t is that a boot made of this chemically-treated material is in actuality never dry. Even in the driest weather the perspiration of the feet is sufficient to render the footwear dangerous, as such natural moisture acts upon the inner sole and collects there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>VITRIFIED QUARTZ.</head>
        <p>VITRIFIED QUARTZ.</p>
        <p>Vitrified quartz, a valuable material capable of withstanding a great heat or sudden variations of temperature, was originally prepared by heat- ing quartz fragments to I,COOdeg. Cent., and plunging them into cold -water. The white enamel-like product thus obtained can be heated suddenly in the oxy-hydrogen flame without splintering, and may be fused into rods from which tubes and vessels may be moulded. Vitrified quartz vessels are not attacked by bod- ing water, hot dilute acids, or concentrated sul- phuric acid at 100 deg. Cent. Phosphoric acid at 400 deg., however, has a marked corrosive action, and barium silicate is produced by long contact with baryta water at 18deg. Cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DEATHS AT DIFFERENT HOURS.</head>
        <p>DEATHS AT DIFFERENT HOURS.</p>
        <p>In an investigation covering over 3,000 cases in relation to the hour of death said a well- known physician, who has been himself a student of the question, it has been ascertained that the greater number die between 5 and 6 a.m., when the death-rate is over 40 per cent. of the ave- rage; the next during the hour before midnight, when the rule is about 25 per cent, in excess. A third hour of excess is from 9 to 10 a.m., when the rate is about 18 per cent: in excess. On the other hand, the death-rate,between 10 to 3 p.m. is 16 per cent. below the average, the hour be- fore noon being the most fatal. From 3 until 7 p.m. the deaths rise to 5 .per cont. above! the average, and then fall from that hour to 11 p.m. From 9 to 11 p.m. there is a minimum of 6 per cent, below the mean average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>NEW THEORY OF NERVES.</head>
        <p>NEW THEORY OF NERVES.</p>
        <p>The study of nerves, their connections and activities, has been begun in earnest only with- in the past few years, :lint what has been learnt seems to lead to as many surprises as has any other branch of science. Only here and there, says Science Siftings, is there now an investiga- tor in this branch, but these have already found out that all nervous action is spent upon the muscles. That all are in one way or another connected with them, that each particular nerve cell has a specific function and substitution seems no more possible among them than can the eye be substituted for hearing or for tasting. At present work is being carried on to deter- mine the functions of various parts of the brain, especially for the effects of use and disuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ELECTRIC SUNSTROKE.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC SUNSTROKE.</p>
        <p>"Electric sunstroke" is an effect produced by electric light in certain respects resembling sun- burn and sunstroke, although the results are nothing like so serious. • The electric arc em- ployed in the reducing furnace is of such in- tensely concentrated heat that, while steci will melt in it like tallow, a thermometer a dozen yards away from it is hardly affected. But people standing at this distance become affected with a sense of burning pain on the head,face, and neck, resembling some of the effects ,of ac- tual sunstroke. Their skin becomes bronzed to a coppery tint, temporary blindness in naturat light supervenes, with pain in the eyes, followed by headache and insomnia. Afterwards the skin peels off just an in the case of sunburn. The ex- planation of these effects is still to seek, but they would certainly seem to show that in ordinary sunstroke light plays a part as great as, if not greater, than heat,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MINE EXPLOSIONS.</head>
        <p>MINE EXPLOSIONS.</p>
        <p>There is a peculiarity often manifested in re- gard to the evidences of violence at different points, says Mines and Minerals. Too often it is assumed that a fresh body of firedamp or a keg of powder has been exploded at such points. The greatest violence is generally manifested at the point developing the greatest resistance, when that resistance gives way under the pressure. The expanding air of an explosive blast may cushion from one point in the .mine to another. A temporary cushioning of the blast by a .con- tracted passageway, or other heavy obstruction, may have the effect of transferring the pressure from the starting point, where destructive vio- lence is usually manifest, to a point where the obstruction was met. This cushioning effect may be so complete that between these two points there is perhaps little sign of the passage of the blast in that direction. There is seldom any evidence of violence manifested inby or toward the face from the initial point of an ex- plosion, owing to the cushioning of the air in this direction.</p>
        <p>PROBLEMS OF BRAIN ACTION. What is the nature of exhaustion? What is the rate of recuperation, the source of energy and of automatic activity, what happens in sleep, in the hypnotic state, in disease, insanity, and- in unconsciousness? All this will be inquired into. Dr. 'Hall has said that the nerves are the most wonderful things in the world, and we know so little about them—mind and thinking, condi- tioned by their presence and activity, on the one hand, and all expressions of them through muscular action as exhibited by motions and emotions. There are many reasons for expect- ing most important disclosures from this direc- tion, which may make needful many changes in common belief ill educational theories and efforts, of responsibility in crime and the proper management of defectives of all sorts. It is not unlikely as great changes as took place during the last century in the beliefs on many impor- tant subjects will be required for the work cf the twentieth century.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>DUST INFECTION.</head>
        <p>DUST INFECTION.</p>
        <p>Robert Hessler includes under dust disease the vague and ill-defined symptom-complexes that are variously referred to or misdiagnosed as "colds," "biliousness," " sick headache," and so on, or as a typical case of influenza, rheumatism, gastritis, neurasthenia, and the like. Cases of dust disease are to be especially looked for under and separated from recognised diseases in which "rheumatic and gouty dia- thesis" are concerned, as well as in cases of neurasthenia and hysteria, and in cases in which there are frequent alimentary or respiratory tract disturbances. This affection is most com- mon in crowded cities. It is not found among Arctic explorers and weather observers in hir't mountains. When these men return home, b( . ever, they are attacked again. The eases may be divided into groups, depending upon the part of the body or the organ chiefly affected. If the existence 'of this affection is kept in mind, comments Health, many cases with ob- scure symptoms will be cleared up. There are no marked or recognisable pathological leAions in dust disease. • Mucus may p?ay an important part. The causative factor is infected dust. The cause of their discomfort should be pointed out to the afflicted. In some cases no medication will avail, the only relief coming, from the change to a better atmosphere. 'he writer emphasises the importance of recognising the influence of a dust-contaminated atmosphere in the productioD of ill-health and disease in general.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DOUGLAS AND BLACKPOOL.</head>
        <p>DOUGLAS AND BLACKPOOL.</p>
        <p>Last year Mr. Meyer conducted Conferences at :Scarborough and Bisekpool. This year, in addition to that held at Morecambe, he is visit- ing Douglas (Isle of Man) and Blackpool. On each of these visits Mr Meyer is accompanied by Rev. Thomas Law. the Secretary of the Federa- tion. l'he Revs. J. E. Battenbnry and J. G Stevenson are also rendering assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MIDLAND CONVENTION.</head>
        <p>THE MIDLAND CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>In the Sluing of 'this year Dr. Horton, the President of the National Conned, commenced a series of Convention's which have already provoked Considerable attention. His visits to such centres as Plymouth, Llandrindod Wells, Sunderland, Lowestoft,. have made deep impressions. After a brief interval for holidays Dr. Horton is commencing again with a Conven- tion at Cheltenham. This centre has been fixed as being the most convenient fcr the Midland towns, and representatives will he present from all the Councils around. The meetings continuo for three days, and include Early Morning Prayer Meetings, 'Morning Conferences, Afternoon Rsnibles to places of interest in the n&amp;ghbourbood, Conferences over tea, and Evening Demonstrations. September the opening day, and on the Tuesday fol- lowing Rev. It J. Camphell, M.A.. will ire ich what is known as the Convention Sermon. At the reat Free Church Demonstration, to be on the Wednesday. the sp.s.kers will in- clude Rev. Thomas Law, Rev. F. B. Meyer, and Mr. J. Compton Rickett, M.P.; while the Rev. Silas K. Hocking, Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P., and Mr. H. R. Mansfield, M.P., are expected to speak on the Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DR. HORTON'S JUBILEE.</head>
        <p>DR. HORTON'S JUBILEE.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that on tho opening clay of the Cheltenham convention Dr. Horton will celebrate his Jubilee, having been born in London fifty years ago on September 18. Dr. Horton has had a distinguished career, and possesses a wide influence. For twenty years he has ministered to the Congregational Church at Hampstead, but his fame has been extended greatly, not only by his personal visits to other places, but by the books he has found time to write. • Of the works which he has recently issued, one of the most notable has been "The Open Secret," which is intended to be a Free Church Manual of Devotion. This has already had a large circulation, although it has only been on the market for some months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>METHODIST UNION.</head>
        <p>METHODIST UNION.</p>
        <p>There seems to be every prospect now of con- siderable progress being made in the scheme for Methodist reunion. The Annual ConferencHs of the Methodist bodies, which have just held their meetings, have carriO the matter a long step forward. Within a short time we should see the accomplishment of the union between the, Methodist New Connexion, the Bible Christians, and the United Methodist Free Church. These should form a strong and united body. Of those who worked for the consummation of this end there were none more prominent than the late Rev. F. W. Bourne, whose death comes as a sad blow to all Methodists. Mr. Bourne's saintly life and sterling qualities endeared him to all who were privileged ;to come into touch with him, and his memory will long remain amongst a wide circle of friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BRIGHTON PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>A BRIGHTON PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Amongst the new Methodist Presidents, the choice of the Bible Christians is a happy one. The Rev. S. B. Lane has for many years been acting as secretary of that Connexion. His elevation to the highest office in his denomina- tion has come as a reward to long service. Mr. Lane is a man of many activities. Amongst some of the most important of his labours have been those In connection with the Free Church Council Movement. He is Secretary of the Brighton Free Church Council and the Sussex Federation of Councils. In this capacity he had the undertaking of the local arrangements when the National Free Church Council held its meetings at Brighton some time ago, and his energy. tact, and ability commended him to all attending the gatherings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>BOURNEMOUTH ENTERPRISE.</head>
        <p>THE BOURNEMOUTH ENTERPRISE.</p>
        <p>The Free Churches of Bournemouth have united in the founding of a Free Church Girls' Guild Home, which should prove a valuable addition to that pleasant town. The Home was recently opened by the Mayor and Mayoress, and is intended to serve as a centre for the girl-life of the Free Churches of Bourne- mouth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WORK FOR YOUNG MEN.</head>
        <p>WORK FOR YOUNG MEN.</p>
        <p>Among the many departments of service in connection with the National Free Church Council, one which is receiving considerable attention just now is the Young Free Church- men's Auxiliary, which was commenced last Autumn with the intention of banding together the young men of the Free Churches for mutual help and active service. Although, of course, little can be done during the summer, two new branches of the Auxiliary are reported, and it is anticipated that during the Autumn renewed stimulus will be put into the arrange- ments, and an ambitious programme of work entered upon.</p>
        <p>The body of Mr. George Y. Hattersley, a member of a well-known Flixton family, was found on Sunday afternoon lyingover a gate in Boat-lane, Main. In the right hand was a revolver, and death had been caused by a bullet wound in the right temple. An inquest was held at Irlam on Monday afternoon, and Dr. Jago, who had been attending Mr. Battersley, said that his patient's condition was such that at any time he might become temporarily insane. fheCoroner said it seemed evident that Mr. Hattersley .had been suffering from some delusion as to his position. The jury without hesitation returned a verdict of suicide during tempor- ary insanity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>• .1' • • ' – , . ,er .11 • • *1441. •</head>
