      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>W. HO GKINSO</head>
        <p>W. HO GKINSO</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>of matron of the new hospital. They had decided to hold a special meeting for the selection of a candidate. The committee would ask two or three applicants for the post of caretaker to attend before the I.loard. The report of the committee WAS approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Fortnightly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present : Messrs. S. Woodward (presiding), C. Roylance, H. Eyes, J. Bolshaw' A. Broom, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, T. Guest, S. Woodward, NV. Harrison, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, J. Spruce, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, R. Billington, J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwis, Admiral Jones Parry, G. F. Ashton (cleric), NV. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Diggle (surveyor), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical officer.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY AT PRESTON ON Tle HILL.</head>
        <p>WATER SUPPLY AT PRESTON ON Tle HILL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that Messrs. Timmins and Sons of Runcorn wrote intimating that the cost of supplying water to 15 cottages in Preston on th' Hill (12 Ship Canal Company's and 3 North Staffs. Railway Com- pany's) would be £147. The Clerk explained that the Ship Canal Company asked the Council to meet them in supplying the houses. The question was referred to the Preston on th' Hill Parish Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DEFECTIVE WELL.</head>
        <p>A DEFECTIVE WELL.</p>
        <p>• The County Council wrote stating that the sample of water transmitted by the District Council to the County analyst for analysis on the 24th February was certified by the County analyst to be a very bad water and not tit for use, and requested the Council to close the well from which the sample was taken. The letter was referred to the Morley Parish Council. MOTOR TRAFFIC. . .</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a circular letter for the Local Govern. ment Board enclosing a copy of general order varying the provisions of sub-section 4 and Section 28 of the Highways Act 1878 so as to authorise, subject to the conditions contained in the order, the use of the high- ways by locomotives having driving wheels shod with wooden blocks. The circular stated that representa- tions had been made to the Board to permit of a further variation an as to authorise the use of the highway by locomotives the driving wheels of which were fitted with the contrivance known as the " pedrail." The Board had issued an order covering the purposes of the suggestions.</p>
        <p>A PPOINTM ENTS. Arr. Woodward reported that the isolation hospital committee had received 69 applications for the position</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>OUTBREAK 01' SCARLET FEVER.</head>
        <p>OUTBREAK 01' SCARLET FEVER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported that during the month five cases of scarlet fever had occurred in Stockton Heath. There had been no further cases notified and he did not think any more cases would be reported.</p>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST A CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. On Tuesday, at the Runcorn County Court, Charles Woodier, aged three and a half years, sued by his father and next friend, James Woodier, King-street, the Run- corn Co-operative and Provident Society for £2.5 damages for injuries received through being knocked down and run over on December 28th last, by one of the defendant's horses and milk floats, which it was plleged was negligently driven by the society's servant. Mr. Browne. of Warrington, appeared for the plaintiff, , and Mr.J. W.Knowles, Widnes defended. Mr. Browne said the float was being driven along Church-street, a very narrow thoroughfare, and while the driver had his head turned round from tho direction in which he was driving the cart knocked down the child. The wheel went over its stomach, and the child was seriously injured. Dr. M'Dougall was in attendance until May of this year.—A verdict was returned against the ociety for twenty guineas and costs.</p>
        <p>James Arrowsmith, of Brunswick-street, Leigh, a blacksmith, died on Sunday night, in his 101st year. He had had excellent health until a few weeks ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>‘.3 'fr"t k • eat</head>
        <p>‘.3 'fr"t k • eat</p>
        <p>SEE HOW</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
