      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>SPLENDID NEW SERIAL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MITE 'LOSE.</head>
        <p>THE MITE 'LOSE.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. MR. lAN FRASEN was not an early riser. :Inless he had someth.ng particular to do. 'Me usually went to bed very late; and. :besides, he found .it more economical to stay in bed, which cost nothing, than to keep up the position in the world he claimed or his 3wn. He bad lived his whole life in great straits and greater expectations. Canny to .a degree . in matters of actual fact, he was at the, same time an optimist in his imagin- ative flights; and, above 'all,. he had tile utmost self-confidence and self-assurance. He was never at a loss for anything but ready money; and his one set object in life was to land ultimately at the very top of the tree. Poor without being particularly proud, he bad never been hampered in minor matters • by any false feelings of self-respect, and he / had no compunction in violating all the ten commandments, severally or simultaneously, -so long only as he did not break the final injunction, bred by twentieth century morals, -of not being found out. He was one of those men who from birth judge themselves to have a grievance against God- and man, and are not content to do their duty in .the sphere ?to which they are called. lan Fraser belonged to. the.,poor branch cf a wealthy family, and he had never forgiven fate this slight. He had a great lust both for money and for power, and all his life he had not seen his way to gratify it in either respect.' From the day of his birth onwards his career had been one prolonged struggle to get the better of his acquaintances without their being aware of the fact. It had been- the same at school, at Oxford, and on the fringe of the diplomatic service: and now it was the same when he found him:sett, having received his rouge from the -last-named quietly and unostentatiously two years before, trying to eke out an exiguous income in town on the edge of an irreproachable set, where, by means of family traditions, Siniiinited cheek, and imperviousness to snubs, .he had landed himself. • Still he was not ill. pleased with himself pn the morning of the Duchess of Ilainp- stead's dance, as he shaved himself in his tiny 'bed-room in Chapel Street, Mayfair. Things :of late had begun to look up with him. A year ago a 'great idea had entered . his head, -and he had s'et himself to work it out with all the enthusiasm of an adventurer and th,:. earnestness of a Scot. It • had at first ap- peared a forlorn hope, but it had turned out beyond his expectations, and at the inonimi -everything looked extraordinarily promising --at any rate, for himself. Whether the ulti- mate object were ever attained or not, he thought he saw his way by a little judicious care, to feathering his nest most ilown:ly for life ; and he was not at all above putting his coadjutators in the cart, if the pi lieh came. lan Frazer had a rugged, determined face,. typically Scotch, yet, withal handsome in its roughness. His hair, WC:11 short in military fashion, was dark, while his mous- tache, heavy and cut straight above his, lip, was a dull red. His nose was big, but wail formed, and his eyes a light steel-blue. • His chin was large to clumsiness and solidly square. It was a face which attracted and repelled, according to its expression, but ,could not fail to insist upon a second glance. By his bed-side were the remains of a .frugal meal, a couple of morning papers, and .a pile of cigarette ends, and the watch on its 'stand pointed to noon. " Lunch with Gordon at one," he muttered. '" and then I shall hear all about it. What a fearful hubbub it has. made." He went on dressing quickly, humming and talking tc himself. " I can't make up my mind whether or not 'to take up the - secretaryship old Struan offered me so unexpectedly last night," he said half aloud, as he tied his tie with ali due care. "Of course it's a very good thins to be secretary to the Secretary of State for Scotland ; but I question whether it won't 'pay me better at present to keep out of any public connection in case of failure I've got to look at both sides of the question and con- sult the best interests of Mr. lan Frazer. • I think, on the whole, I'll keep my connection 'with these good people very quiet, but very intimate, and await events a bit. I can tell old Struan that I think it better at the moment to devote all my energies to the Work of the League I" By this time he .had got into an immaculate frock coat—it was part of his profession as a free lance to dress above reproach—and with,. his connections he could • always dress on -credit. 'With a last look round, he left the room and started for the chambers of Mr. Ellis Gordon in the Temple. Later in the afternoon the pair sauntered into the Unattached together. The smoking room was crowded, and the atmosphere heavy with cigar smoke. Around the lire- place a little knot of men stood talking excitedly. It was obvious that they Nv ere discussing the one topic of the day. Frazer and Gordon walked across the room and joined them. " It's all very well for you . -chaps to talk of a political 'Jack the Ripper,'" . went on the man who was speaking, "but I believe there's a lot more behind it than you think. Personally, I shouldn't be surprised if • Russia were working the whole thing, or Something like that," he concluded vaguely, brushing back his sparse hair from an unin- tellectual forehead. "'Russia be hanged, my dear Fitzßoy," laughed a bumptious young M.P. with a . bull-dog face."l like the way all you tellows spend your time building up theories without foundation and making bricks Without a scrap of straw. Commend; me to a comfortable homicidal maniac with a down on successful public men." "You'll have to be careful then, Whit- tington," interrupted a soldierly looking man of about thirty-five; "it may be your turn next. You know you are getting rather famous yourself." Whittington laughed a little 'nervously, zot quite knowing how to take the remark. "Don't you get too - sarcastic, my. dear Atherley," he replied, "or I shall begin to 'be afraid that ,you will be sticking me your- -self."</p>
        <p>"Might do worse," grimly chuckled Sir Alan Atherley, who was a major in the Lye •Guards, "might do worse, I assure. you." The ambiguity of this remark puzzled Whittington even more than the other, and he subsided to think it over. Frazer seeing his opportunity broke into the conversational ring. "Have you fellows seen the latest theory In the papers? " he asked. "No ; what's that ? " came from several mouths at once " That these murders have a connection With each other, and are the beginning of an attempted revolution ! Did you ever heat anything so absurd ? " Everyone laughed consumedly at the idea. "Fancy a revolution in dear old staid twentieth century England ! " said one of the listeners; and everybody laughed again. The conversation was interrupted by the -shouting of a paper boy in Piccadilly. He was yelling something indistinct, but it was Obviously causing great excitement in the Street. An unceremonious rush was made to the Window; and one or two of the members ran out of the room to buy the latest. Hallo," exclaimed Atherley, stroking his moustache with his usual sang fraid,•" there's I3ranksea buying a paper at the corner. He'll bring the news in with him." The eyes of the group were instantly turned upon the figure in question. He was a tall, dark man of six-and-twenty, with black hair and a bad-tempered face. He was a favourite with no one; but his position as heir to the Marquess of Swanage made into a person of importance. His most toticeable characteristic • was a low, heavy forehead, beneath which his .thick black e1.,,- 0s joined above the bridge of the nose, Civlng him naturally a very sullen alpear.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS.</p>
        <p>ance. Altogether, with his large moustache brushed off a thick-lipped mouth, and a per- petual dark bluish hue on his chin and cheeks, he gave the impression of being a very hairy man. "They say he is the latest of Lady Bina Belsize's scalps," said Whittington, who had been one of the earliest, and had the brag- gadocio to propose. " Refused with thanks, like the rest, I believe," rejoined Atherley, smiling a trifle contemptuously at the youngest member of the House. "1 wonder what she wants ?" went on tht. impervious Whittington, regardless of the thrust. "A future King or a Prince of the blood royal at least, I should think." "Not a cockahoop little politician," mut- tered Atherley under his breath, raising smile on the face of his nearest 'neighbour. He, himself, was more bitten than he would have cared to own; but though a baronet and a major in the Household Cavalry, he was sensible of his• own limitations. Meanwhile the rejected Branksea, oblivious of the anxious spectators, .had slowly opened his pink paper and was conning it anxiously. As he was reading intently, two men came up behind him, and the younger smacked him on the back. Branksea turned round with a frown. " Hallo, Belsize," he said, seeing who it was, and relaxing his scowl as far as nature would allow, "How are you ? " Fit as a flea," answered the other. "Do you know Lord Ben Nevis? If not, let pie introduce you. He's been out of England for years, ever since we were quite kids ; so I don't suppose you have met him." The two men shook hands, and the trit; ascended the teps of the club. The Earl of Ben Nevis was a wiry, athletic man of three-and-thirty. He was not par- ticularly good-looking, but he had an open, pleasant face, with a fine frank smile. He had blue eyes, hair inclined to be auburn, a rather large nose, and a small moustache. He was only about five foot ten in height. but his figure was admirably proportioned and his frock coat showed it off well. " Very kind of your. mother to send ma a card for to-night," he said to Belsize, ae they entered the club. "It was very un- expected, as I didn't think that she would either remember me as a boy, or know that I was back." _ "01', I told her this morning that you were back, and she remembered you at once," answered the other, stretching a point. "It will seem quite strange to be in a London ball-room again, after all these years," said Ben Nevis thoughtfully. "All the girls one used to, dance with will now be matrons with considerable families and sons almost ready for Eton ! " - " There are always plenty of girls," said Charlie Belsize, cheerfully. "A new lot comes on every year as regularly as clockwork." "What's the latest, Branksea ? " asked -Atherley, and a chorus of voices, as they entered the smoking-room. "Oh, they've found the cabman who drove old Morny to the Carlton," replied Branksea, curtly. " That's all.' " Where (I,d he drive him from ? " asked Whittington, eagerly. " Oh. somewhere just near the radius up Hampstead way," vouchsafed the other— Pond Street or somewhere." "I wonder what the devil he was doing up there ? " said Whittington, asking a vague general question no one seemed able or in. dined to answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CHAPTER VI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>THE Duchess of 'Hampstead's dance toi the opening of Parliament was a magnificent affair. Her entertainments were always on a princely scale, and done in such a way as to suggest not only unlimited money, but the most perfect taste. To begin ivith; in such a house as the mansion in Belgrave Square, however many the guests, there could never be an Unpleasant crowding or an unseemly squash, as only too usual in London. And, in addition, the duchess was very exclusive in ner selection of guests, preferring to give offence in certain quarters by withholding an invitation rathe than to ask any one she considered super- fluous or outside the pale. Thus her recep- tions always were a guarantee of sufficient elbow-room and a select circle.</p>
        <p>The house itself was admirably adapted for entertaining. • Immediately inside the front doors was the outer hall with cloak rooms on either side, and beyond the large square inner hall of marble, in the centre of which played a fountain. This hall rose through the entire of the height house to a dome, which lighted the centre of the building. On each floor a wide gallery ran completely round the four sides of the house, and all the doors opened on the galleries. A great double staircase of marble led from the inner hall to the drawing-room floor, and the Duchess of Hampstead, standing Opposite to the entrance of the chief drawing- room, received her guests in the centre of the gallery. They were escorted by a long line of powdered footmen, and the names were passed from one to another to the head butler, who announced them from the top of the right hand staircase, by which all entrances were made, the left being reserved for exits, exploration and supper couples. This marble" staircase was the only one visible to the eye; and the upper floors wer reached by lifts unostentatiously let in at the corners of the galleries. Altogether, the London home of the Hampsteads was one of the most magnificent mansions in town, and more like a palace than a private house. The whole hall with the staircases and the galleries was a gay mass of orchids from the famous orchid houses at Southsea Castle, and the scheme of decoration was carried right up to the top of the dome. Rows of electric lights st regular intervals all round the galleries graduated up to the cluster in the dome, lent a superb air of brilliance to the scene. The chief drawing room at the head of the staircase and the two side drawing - rooms were reserved for dancing ; while the front drawing room over the entrance- hall was set out for bridge. The conservatories, which ran round three sides of the house, and the galleries, gave ample room for sitting out; and supper was served in the large and ,small dining-rooms. The Duchess of Hampstead, supported by the Duke, who was in one of his languid moods after an exciting day, and Lady Bina, looked superb in a dress of black lace and blazed with diamonds. With a high diamond crown on her head and diamond ornaments all over her dress and arms, and her famous ropes of pearls which hung from her neck to her waist, she looked every inch a queen and felt in her own arrogant way that she could, without fear, challenge comparison. By half-past nine the guests had begun tc arrive ; and by ten the rooms were filling up. In view of the arrival of the royal party, noted for their punctuality, at 10.30, practically all the guests had arrived ten minutes before that time, and all were awaiting the presence of their Majesties for the commencement of the great ball. Lord Belsize brought a party of men who had been dining with him, and in the enthu- siasm of his new friendship presented Ben Nevis at once to his sister, insisting upon his being given a dance later on in the evening. Branksea, who came with them, cast jealous eyes upon the new intruder and refrained, to Bina's surprise and relief, from asking for one for himself. Naturally her programme was very lull, but on such an occasion most of her engagements were entirely " duty" dances. The whole scene seemed very strange to Ben Nevis after his long absence from civilisation and his previous spell of active service, and he was very glad to find himself under Charlie's friendly wing. That young gentleman, with his usual exuberanne was here, there. and</p>
        <p>everywhere, and seemed bent on Introducing his new friend to everybody. "Come and be introduced to my Aunt Grizelle," he said, at length. " She's a regular topper, and you'll get on with her splendidly.' Hardly had the introduction to Lady Grizelle Belsize been completed when a murmur went round the room and a sudden hush fell on the whole party. "Halo," said Charlie, "the King and Queen, by Jove! I must be off and join the guv'nor and mater. Ta, ta." And off he hastened, leaving Ben Nevis under the wing of his aunt. .</p>
        <p>They watched the entry of the royal parfy in silence. Then, as 'the band struck up, Ben Nevis turned to Lady Grizelle, white-haired but well-preserved woman of fifty who was one of the most popular people in London. . '</p>
        <p>- "-I can't tell you how strang% I feel being back in town again after so ldfig," he said. smiling into her face in the way which made many peoplo reconsider their decision as to his looks. "I was such a youngster then. In fact.l had only just joined the loth Hussars when the Boer War broke out."</p>
        <p>" Yes, but I remember you," answered Lady Grizelle. " I remember you perfectly well, and, what is more, your mother and / were always particular friends." "Then, perhaps," . went on Ben Nevis, "you'll help to, put me into the way of things a bit. You see, for instance, Queen Victoria was on the throne when I left England, and lots of things have happened since then."</p>
        <p>"Lots' indeed," admitted Lady Grizelle' with a sigh. "</p>
        <p>"Tell me who the people axe," he con- tinued. "For instance, who is that portly, red-faced man *Ali white whiskers ? "</p>
        <p>"That is the Marquess of Swanage, the father of young Branksea, who came in with Charlie and you. He stands very well with the Government, and everyone. was surprised at his being left out of the Cabinet. The slight, elderly and rather effeminate man he is talking to is the Earl of Fulford, the Premier."</p>
        <p>" Oh, I remember him," said ~Ben Nevis. "He used to lead the House of Commons as Mr. Braithwaite, didn't he ? "</p>
        <p>" Yes ;. and that's the present leader of the House who has just•joined them, Mr. Ossory, Member for Whitstable. • He is sup- posed to be the strongest member of the Cabinet, now that ,poor Henry Mornington is dead and gone." ' " What an awful affair 1" said Ben Nevis, thoughtfully. "Everybody seems to be talking of it and nothing else, even at a dance."</p>
        <p>"Yes, but it's a little out of place, don't you think ? " asked Lady Grizelle with a troubled look in her eyes.</p>
        <p>I accept your. correction, answered the other bowing. ." You are right. Tell me who are those three men over there ? "_</p>
        <p>"An interesting 'little group," said Lady Grizelle, putting up her 'starers." "That is Mr. Colgate, the Home Secretary, neareSt to us, net to him is Lord Oddingly, the Secretary for War, and that slight, cynical- looking than is Mr. Reginald Carruthers, editor of the ' Morning News '." "The Cabinet seem out in force," said Ben Nevis, smiling. "They always are at this house," answered Lady Grizelle, "and you must remember that to-night, as ?recedes the opening of Parliament, is a serm-political one." "Of course, I realise that. Who is that thin-lipped man with the big chin and scanty red hair ? He's with Mr. lan Frazer."</p>
        <p>"Oh, that," answered Laily Grizelle, looking up at the pair indi,..ated, "is the Earl of Struan, the head of the Robertson clan. He is Secretary of State for Scotland. Do you know that man Frazer ? " she added quickly. "I can't say that I know him," said Ben Nevis, "or that I like him: but I have met him at the club with Charlie Belsize and other men."</p>
        <p>"I don't like him at all," said Lady Grizelle with decision, "and there is some- thing about him I don't undrestand. I certainly can't imagine why they have recently taken to asking him to this house." Ben Nevis spent some little time with Lady Grizelle, talking over the people and learning how the world of London had gone round during his long absence. He took a great liking to her with her hearty straight- forward manner and downright criticisms, and she appeared to reciprocate it cordially. As he was leaving her she invited him to dinner.</p>
        <p>"Not to:inorrow night, but the night after," she said, nodding pleasantly to him. " It won't be a party, just Bina and perhaps Charlie, and we'll go to a theatre afterwards." Ben Nevis accepted with obvious• pleasure and then was carried off into the crowd by Belsize, who had returned, intent upon sup- plying him with partners. Later in the evening, after supper, his dance with Lady Sabina came round, and he sought her out eagerly. After a couple of turns round the room she stopped. "Do you mind if we sit the rest out ? " she asked. "I must own to being tired. These big parties, with all the receiving and the duty dances, are horribly tiring." "I shall be delighted," acquiesced Ben Nevis, and Bina led him to a cool corner of the conservatory. He sat down beside her looking closely at her for the first time. Never in his life it seemed to him had he seen anyone so beau- tiful, and he began to understand what he had heard flippantly described at the club as the "Bina craze." No wonder, he thought, they all want her, and no wonder she refuses them all.</p>
        <p>. !To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MEN AND WOMAN ALIKE</head>
        <p>MEN AND WOMAN ALIKE</p>
        <p>Praise Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>• "Let me confess I did not tell any wife I was taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, for I had an idea that pills so go(id for ladies would be of little use to men. I was mistaken; the pills are not an "old woman's" remedy. They are a wonderful tonic and strength- giving medicine, as good for men as for women; I have proved this." The speaker--Mr. Hazlehurst, 8, Isaac. street, Toxteth, Liverpool—continued : " I had to be out in beat or rain, and my meals were taken when work allowed. I fell into a low state of health, and grew so 'seedy ' and unfit for work that at last I gave up the My appe- ite vanished. I 'pt badly at night felt tired out the morning. I :as utterly wretched iad hopeless through ly weak state. But Jere was a wonder- ful change in me when I took Williams' Pink Pllis for Pale People on the advice of a friend. After a week I- felt brighter and better in every way. I steadily im- proved, and now can eat a hearty meal with a relish. I sleep well and awake refreshed, and ain capable of deal- ing with plenty of Work. My recovery is entirely due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills."</p>
        <p>Pale anemic women and weak hopeless men find renewed health in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, which make new rich blood, besides imparting "tone" to the nervous system. They have cured countless cases of Anwmia, Indigestion, Bile, Eczema, Kidney Disease, Rheurnhtism, Sciatica, Paralysis and Locomotor Ataxy. Of all dealers, also post free for 2s. 9d. (six boxes for 13s. 9d.) from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn- viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>Sir Wilfrid Lawson,M.P.,-presided at a Band of Hope meeting at Aspatria on Sunday. He said that if he had made the world he thought he would have made it perfect - without need of improvement—(laughter),— but they must take things us they were, and the world wanted putting right. it would never be put right as long as there was strong drink among the people. Many years ago people used to think that drink was a good thing. He .helieved that many people thought the Baffle of Waterloo, would never have been won if the soldiers had uot drunk beer.—(Latighter.)</p>
        <p>ELASTIC SToCKINGs. KNEE CAPS, &amp;c.—For Varicos Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Leg.. Sprains, &amp;;.% They are porous, light in texture, and in- expensive, and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking. —Price 4s. Gd., 7s. Gd. and 10s. Gd. each. Postage free. WHITE &amp;. WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument and Trusii Makers, 93, Renshaw Street, Liverpool.—Adr.t.</p>
