      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXTENSIONS AT DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>EXTENSIONS AT DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>Scheme Approved. "RECKLESS FXPENDITURE "</p>
        <p>The members of the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday approved of a scheme for the extensions and alterations at the Workhouse at Dutton. Mr. T. Baxter presided over the meeting, at which there were also resent :—.Messrs. .1. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, A. Broome, H. %L. Davies, C. E. Liniker, J. H. Dtvies, .1. Smith, T. Wright, T. Guest, S. %Voodward, W. Harrison, J. L .lon-s, E. Bretherton, J. Spruce. .1. M. Morris, S.Taylor P. Collins, J. Douglas, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, R. Billington, C. E. Linaker, On., J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwis, Admiral Jones-Parry, G. P. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), %V. R. de Prez, T. Ccok and Pearson (relieving olice:s), and J. Cannah (workhouse master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXTENSION scrimir. ArpßovEn.</head>
        <p>EXTENSION scrimir. ArpßovEn.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker presented the report of a committee : appointed to consider plans for the alteration j and extension of the Workhouse. At the last meeting of the Board, he explained, the plans for the new maternity ward and the Nurses' Heine were referred back for reconsideration and reduction. The cost of the maternity ward had been reduced from 1;101 to .£3OO and the cost of the7nurses' home from £1032 to £6OO. The nurses' home would be little better than a cottage. It contained two sitting rooms 11 feet by eleven feet and 14 feet by 13 feet. and a room which could be used as a kitchen or scullery. There were three bedrooms and a bathroom. The committee were hardly unanimous in the recom- mendation. in his opinion they would get better value for their money by sticking to the original plans, and he still adhered to the opinion he expressed at the last meeting of the Board that the building originally pro- posed could he erected for £BO9. He moved the adoption of the report of the committee. I Mr. Spruce seconded. Admiral Jones Parry : What has become of the servant's MOTU ? I Mr. Linaker : There are three bedrooms and one of the three can be used for the servant. Tr. Hallows I don't see any earthly reason why we should spend £609 upona cottage. A gentleman will build a house with every convenience for ititnself and his family for about £OOO. Why we should spend £3OO upon two of our servants I cannot see unless we are putting up an folaborate build- ing. If we elaborate in this case it means going deeper and deeper. If we are going to provide a place like this for the nurses will the master and matron be content with the eccemmodation which is provided for them? They will not be and we cannot expect that they should be. Plenty of builders will put up a cottage like this for ...C2OO. NTr. Linaker did not say the building would cost £6OO. That is the architects' estimate and I contend it is excessive. The building ought not to cost any- thing like £llOll. Mr. Hallows : If we give the nurses a little larger salaries we would induce tham to stay. Mr. .1. H. Davies supported the proposal of the corn- ; mittee. He did not think the hnilding would cost £OOO i Mr. Baxter did not see that it was necessary for the nurses to have a servant. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows : Hear, hear. If wo give a servant to two of our servants surely we should give a servant to our master. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce : It is customary to have a servant at a Nurses' Home. Mr. ‘Voodward said he was bound to express the opinion that they would not get value for their money as they would in the original scheme, and moreover he did not think the second building would he so adapt- able to the requirements of the institution as the first building. He thought something between the two schemes could he decided upon at a reasonable cost. Mr. Tanker : I think the two schemes are too extremes. I think it wonld be better if we could get something between the two. Mr. Taylor : What necessity is there to spend this money!? We have two nurses to provide for:and I would like to ask if any member of the Board would spend £1.059 for the accommodation of two women. A more reckless way of spending money I never heard of. Con- tinuing he said he supported the recommendation of the committee, for the erection of the smaller build- ingr Mr. 'Hallows: The place could be put up for £250 and lam bound to ask where the £6OO comes in. I pronose that the plans be sent back for reconsideration. Mr. J. H. Davies thought the Board were in the dark as to what the building would cost and would be so until they obtained tenders. He felt certain that tenders would be sent in for less than £6OO. The report of the committee was approved. REDUCTION IN PAUPERISM. The Clerk read a return from Mr. Dansey of pauperism and expenditure. The number of paupers in the Union in January 1872. was 110 G, and the expenditure £2.702, a proportion of Is. 1011., per head of the population. The number in .Tanuary of this year was 760 and the expenditure £2.551. a cost of Is. 31d. per head. -It would be perceived, said the clerk, that whereas the rate of pauperism in the Union was five per cent, worse than the average of the whole district in 1872, it, was now five per cent, better and the cost per head of the population was 3:d. less than the average cost of the whole district. In 1872 there were only 14 out of 41 unions in the district with a greater per centage of pauperism than the Runcorn Union whereas now there were 31 and the cost was greater in 34 districts. Mr. Linaker asked if any return was given as to the . cost per head for each pauper. That he contended wa.s the!only proper criterion end he was inclined to think that when they had such a return they would find they were not so economical as they fancied they were.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL. I Mr. Wright reported that the reePipts since the last meeting or the Board had been £3,437 16s. Rd, nn,lthat after meeting all accounts they had a balance in hand of £l,OlB Ns. lid.</p>
        <p>VOTE OF THANKS. The Board cordially expressed its thanks to Mrs. Jones-Parry for her kindness in giving one of the girls in the Workhouse a six weeks' holiday.</p>
        <p>APPOINTIt ENT.</p>
        <p>The Ninster reported that Miss Florence Parr had been appointed a nurse at the Workhouse, sod Mr. Thomas Jackson empty as boys' industrial trainer.</p>
        <p>The nppointrnents were confirmed.</p>
        <p>HAMPER-MAKER AND THE CHEMICAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday, Thomas Chantler. skip and hamper maker. Frodsham, applied for an administration order. He stated that he wils sixty-five years of age, and his indebtedness had arisen through loss of business. His debts totalled £32, and creditors to the extent of .Cl 4. objected to the order.— Mr. Linaker, for the objectin7 creditors, said the objec- tion fwas not to the order, 'but to the composition of 10s. in the pound. He had goods of more value than his debts.—The applicant said four years ago the United Alkali Company. Widnes, took their trade of hamper making from him, and he had done nothing since. - The applicant agreed to raise the composition to 15s, in the pound, and his Honour made the order, the payments to be made at 10s. per month.</p>
