        <p>EXAMINER OFFICE, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Lord CultzoN's resigns- LORD CURZON. tion has surprised nobody and the only wonder is that it did not come about sooner. It is clear now that he did not accept the apparent com- promise betwoen himself and Lord KrTcuaNER on the question of military organisation, but sought to re-establish his own views by appointing as Military Member of the Viceregal Council a dis- tinguished officer who would back him up, and run counter to the Commander-in-Chid. It is impossible to attach any other meaning to the nomination of Sir EPMUND Bsnuow. In accordance with Lord KITCHENER'S scheme, adopted in :principle by the India Office, the system of dual military authority was to end, and the Military Member of the Viceroy's Council was to give his attention chiefly to the Supply Depart- msnt, and not to questions of high policy. Sir EDMUND BARROW has had no experience of the kind of service here indicated, but is a very able soldier q kite unsuited to the subordinate position the new scheme would require him to occupy. It is only too plain that the Viceroy counted on General BARROW'S help to oppose the Corn- , mander-in•Chief, and make the fundamental change in the system more apparent than real. This, it will be seen, has nothing to do with the principle of civil control, to which Lord KITCHENER'S ideas are quite erroneously sup- posed in some quarters to be hostile. After all, the Indian Viceroy remains the head of the Indian Government, military as well as civil, sub- ject to the authority of the Government at home. In any dispute between the Viceroy and the Commander-in-Chief, grave enough to call for the intervention of the India Office, the ultimate decision would be a civil and not a military decision. It is therefore altogether misleading to talk about Lord KITCHENER as if he were seoking to get the Government of India under the heel of militarism. All he demands is that there shall be one Commander-in-Chief of the Indian Army, and not two. That-view has been supported by the Home Government, backed by an expert military Committee, comprising Lord ROBERTS and Sir GEORGE WHITE, whose knowledge of the Indian Army and its needs is not to be surpassed.</p>
        <p>If Lord CURZON had resigned in the first instance, when the Imperial Government definitely adopted Lord KITCHENER'S views, an unfortunate episode would have ended sooner. But he chose a course which was really an attempt to override the authorities at home. There is not much to be said for the tact and temper of BRODRICK'S despatches, either in this case or in the earlier dispute about Thibet ; but Mr. BnonrucK does at least occupy a strong constitutional position in the later controversy, whereas Lord CURZON takes a line which is really too autocratic to be tolerated. This may be said without any disparagement of his services. He has been a very successful administrator, and has left upon India and her people a stronger impress of individuality than any other Viceroy of recent times. His ' successor is a man of whom there is not very much to be said. It is easy to be a blameless Governor-General of Canada without exhibit- ing any remarkable qualities. In .a self- governing Colony. , Lord MINT° has been</p>
        <p>no more than a figure-head. It is when he comes to administer a more or less benevolent despotism in India that Lord MINTO must satisfy a real test of character. Lord CußzoN, we pre- sume, has a future in our party politics. Probably he will return home not in the best of humour with the rest of the Unionist leaders. It is far from unlikely that he will be no more in accor.l with their domestic pllicy than he is with Mr. BRODRICS'S views about India. It would be piquant to find Lord CURZON publicly disowning the economic views of the Prime Minister and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. But even if he should range him- self together with those illustrious persons he will not do their cause much good.</p>
        <p>For the second time the TEEN Wonstrouto Workhouse ext EXTENSION. scheme which created so much heated discuseion a few months ago has been defeated. On Wednesday Mr. Hickman, the vigorous leader of the opposition, succeeded in carrying an amend- ment that the erection of the new infirm block be not proceeded with at present. It was certainly bad policy on the part of the supporters of the scheme to again endeavour to commit the Board to an expenditure of £8,430 at this stage, when barely six months ago the majority expressed themselves so unmistakably in favour of a postpone- ment for at least a year. At present the need for additional accommodation appears to ne less urgent than before, which is perhaps not to be wondered at in the summer months. There is still a pronounced difference of opinion as to the necessity for a new block, the opposition being convinced that the difficulty could be met by a full utilizatiion of available buildings. When those who are most closely in touch with the working of the institution advance such widely different views, it is not easy for the outsider to make up his mind on the point. Bo that as it may, we believe the majority of the ratepayers will share the opinion that the time is inopportune to involve them in the projected expenditure, and the firm attitude of the opposition will have their support.</p>
        <p>Another importan ANOTHER PROBLEM FOE question is occupy- THE GUARDIANS. ing the attention of the Warrington Board of Guardians. The House Committee unanimously recommend the appointment of a resident medical officer to have charge of the medical work at the hospital and to undertake the training of the nurses, hut at the Board meet- ing on Wednesday the opinion appeared to be that the matter had not received full corsideration in all its bearings, and it was referred back to the committee. Although in the committee's resolu- tion the increased number of inmates was stated as the ground for the suggested change, the train- ing of the nurses is apparently the chief reason which has influenced the committee. There was a lack of evidence that the care of the patients necessitates the creation of the new office and a doctor in local practice should be able to give ample attention to that work. If, however, the training of probationer nurses is to be seri- ously entered noon, a resident medical officer ' becomes absolutely necessary. The queation there- fore resolves itself into whether the patients or the nurses shall receive prior consideration. At present the probationers cannot obtain that training which is necessary to ensure their progress in the profession, and there is a danger that hafore long the Board will be unable to obtain suitable persons to enter their service under these conditions. If in place of the probationers fully-trained nurses hr.ve to be engaged, the extra expenditure will be quite as large as the salary of a resident medical officer. Which-ever alternative is adopted the ratepayers seem likely to be saddled with an increased expense very soon.</p>
      </div>
