      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PLEASURE IN WORK.</head>
        <p>PLEASURE IN WORK.</p>
        <p>The Happy "Hands " of Warrington.</p>
        <p>A NOBLE EXAMPLE FOR EMPLOYERS. Just as this is an age of the centralisation of industry of immense commercial establishments, and huge aggre- gations of workpeople, so it is also a time when the social welfare, of the workers is to a large extent in the hands of the captains of industry. The measure of happiness of an artisan's life is largely what his employer likes to make it. Too often, it is to be feared, the workers are regarded merely as "hands," to be engaged or dismissed by the score; livings life of precarious labour in which interest in their employers is limited strictly to the amount of weekly wages they receive, to their probable length of service, and the security lof their tenure. Per contra, social reformers never tire of holding up for emulation the examples of Port Sunlight and Bourn- ville. As solutions of the housing problem those colonies are certainly excellent, but the opportunity is not given to all employers to lay out virgin estates in proximity to their works. Messrs. Crosfleld have realized the impossibility of transforming Warrington into a garden city, yet their record of social progress among their employils is probably unique. An interest is shown in the workpeople which is positively paternal. The girls in certain departments are encouraged to sing part-songs at their works. To e prevent dysentery the drinking of cold water during hot weather is prohibited, oatmeal-water and tea being provided free as a substitute, an arrangement which has resulted in a striking diminution of sickness. Each girl who leaves the works to be married receives from the firm a wedding present varying in value according to length of service. The" too old at forty" theory is disbelieved ; 00 the other hand, inducements to long service are held out. The completion of 25 years' service is rewarded with&amp; present of £5, and four men who have been at the , works for 50 years have been given £2O apiece. Neither ' boys nor girls are taken into employment until they have passel the sixth standard at school, and every is taught to swim bran instructor from the works at, the cost of the firm. The younger girls are compelled , to attend evening classes In the domestic arts, and the end of the cookery course they enter a cakemaking competition. Instruction in laundry work is also given during working hours. Courses of lectures on technical subjects are arranged. Electricity is demonstrated by a teacher from the Man- chester School of Technology, and the working of motor cars is theoretically and practically considered. Last winter 3,000 attendances were made at a series of : 21 dances, the music being supplied by Ithe works band, which, by the way, he ided the junior section at last year's national contest at the Crystal Palace. Indoor, and outdoor recreations of many kinds are promoted, , the former leading up to an annual eisteddfod, and t. ' latter to an athletic sports meeting. During working hours, too, this same fatherly inter: is centred on the staff, and it is something gained in a town like Warrington to find first consideration given to scrupulous cleanliness. Anyone seen to spit is liable to instant dismissal; girls working where dust is likely to occur are furnished with special uniforms and hoods. Fines for black smoke nuisance</p>
        <p>are now unknown at Crosfield's ; formerly they were frequent. Moreover the suppression of this nuisance has been, accomplished at a saving of a thousand tons of coal per week. A special department, indeed, deals solely with water purification, prevention of smoke, and economy of fuel. Shunters wear scarlet coats as a precaution against accidents. The system of profit-sharing is being extended, and the metric system is now in almost general use throughout the place. These form but an outline of the several schemes adopted to secure the amenities of the establishment, and who shall say the object has not been worth the effort ?—" Daily bispatch."</p>
        <p>It is always interest- WARRINGTON'S FINANCIAL ing to know how National 1.0. of Oddfellows —Warrington POSITION, the r at ep a y ers' District. money is expended The annual gathering of the Manchester District and the annual abstract of the accounts of the and Warrington District took place last Saturday at Corporation, which the Borough Treasurer has Warrington. The visitors to the number of thirty- this week issued, furnishes a mass of information one arrived at the Central Station at 1.50 being on the point. As usual the volume is a very accompanied by their District officers, viz., P.P.G.M. bulky one and although the study of figures is not Wm. J. Aldster, D.S., P.P.G.M, James Myers, D.Treas- as a rule very inviting, both ratepayers and mein- Bro. Albert Cooke Grand Master and Bro. Thos. bers of the Corporation should find much in the Hodgson D.G.M. They were met at the station by abstract to merit their thoughtful attention. the officers and brethren of Warrington, conveyances Our indebtedness continues to increase and were boarded and a drive to Daresbury and Stretton on much enjoyed, returning for . tea to March 31st it stood at £1,236,632 9s. 9d. as against 7 the was very on Hotel, Bridge-street. Great credit £1,208,761 15s. 2d. last year. As a set off against is due to Mr. Lee of the hotel for the this large sum we have valuable assets and properties which are stated to be worth £1,634,602 excellent knife and fork tea he provided, and the catering generally excelled all former meetings- 12s. 6d, leaving a margin on the right side of £397,970 2s. 9d. The ratepayers will be most con- While removing the cloth, &amp;c., several of the visitorg had a stroll to view the different alterations made by cerned with the progress of the industrial concerns the Corporation. At 7.30 the Warrington Grand of the Corporation—gas works, water works, Master Bro. William Pennington took the chair for electricity and tramway undertakings. The gas the usual social and was supported by Councillor T. works still maintains its position as the Wright, D.G.M., Thomas Dickenson, D.S., and the most profitable source of revenue for after officers of Manchester. The Chairman said he was meeting interest, annuities and sinking fund, over delighted at having the honour to preside especially £lO,OOO was devoted to the relief of rates. prob. after their lovely drive and splendid tea. He was ably the mourner will be disposed to ask why he sure all had enjoyed both (hear, hear) and he should not obtain the benefit of this profit in the hoped they would have an equally pleasant evening. shape of a reduced charge for gas. He would feel Bro. A. Cooke on behalf of the visitors said they were direct advantage. The waterworks and much obliged for their kind hospitality. He also re; a more electricity departments show a satisfactory earning marked how pleased they were when they knew their capacity, while the tramways are stated to have of districtileosredcretar7rillad hoped eiltect,evdoulniontht -cl ebeExtehceulailss made a net profit during the year of 2451. Tnis l sum has gone to the reduction of the previous time in fact they feirt he was fitted for higher honours years' loss, and there is now a deficiency of £242. (Hear, hear), The other officers and past officers also We notice, however, that the department has made short speeches as the time past all two rapidly. During the evening the following contributed songs nothing in hand for reserve or renewals, and we &amp;c., Bros. Falshaw, Beever, Meachin, A. Richardson, -, must again express disapproval of this insecure (ireen, Wright, Dickenson, and P. G. Blackshaw policy. It would be sounder finance to institute such a fund and allow for depreciation in tha usual gave several selections on a gramophone. It should be mentioned that a remarkable coincidence happened way. There may come a day when the up-keep of during the drive. This was that two of the oldest plant out of revenue will seriously aff