      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>I Notice. The Warrington " Examiner " is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica- tions to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington WASHING AND WRWGING MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.— ADVT. Index to Inside Pages. PAGE. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Science Notes. Athletic Notes : Cricket and Bowling. ' 3. "The White Rose." Cricket and Bowling Matches. Dutton Workhouse Extensions. 6. Board of Guardians. Proposed Resident Medical Officer. Police News. Rev. J. Yonge and the Cotton Crisis. 7. " Dunberry Bells," by Bernard Capes. Warrington Baker's Bankruptcy. A receiving order has been made at the Warrington County Court on the debtor's own petition, against Frederick Bancroft, residing at St. Mary's-street, and carrying on business as a baker at Napier-street, and formerly at Cockhedge-lane. He has been adjudicated bankrupt. Will of the Late Rey. Richard Greenall. The will of the Rev. Richard Greenall, of The Rectory, Grappenhall, who died on July sth last, aged 74 years, has been proved at £5,301 is. 6d. gross, and at :£4,887 14s. sd. net. He has bequeathed i• 200 to his son, Mr. Arthur Richard Greenall, and the residue of his estate to his two daughters in equal shares. - LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE'CLOCK,BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Died on the Way to the Police Station. While wahting along Arpley Meadows, at War- rington on Thursday morning, a workman named George Longshaw found a man named William Peacock (30), carrier, Walton, lying bleeding from the throat. A blood-stained razor was lying near. The man was at once conveyed to the Police Station, but died on the way. • KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Files &amp;c., large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ADvT. Warrington Bridge Widening. This (Friday) afternoon a deputation from tbe Warrington Corporation consisting of the Mayor, Alderman Smethurst, Alderman Pierpoint, Councillors Wilkinson, Benson and Lorl waited upon Admiral Sir George Nares in Liverpool, to submit arguments and evidence in favour of the scheme for widening War- rington Budge by cantilevers. The meeting took place at the London and North-Western Hotel at 3.30. Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>By the kind permission of Lieut. Colonel Edelsten and officei s.The Volunteer band will play the following programme on Sunday next at 3.15 p.m. March "Crusader," Sousa, ; overture " Victory," Round ; selection " Maritana," Wallace ; Eup solo " Jupiter," Dawson; Mr. Finch soloist; selection, "Idue foscari," Verdi ; fantasia. "The Sabbath," Newton ; March, " The Pearl," Holloway ; "God Save the King."• J. T. Richardson, (bandmaster.) UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt., Fire in Wilderspool-road. At about half-past ten on Saturday night a fire was discovgred in the attic above the shop of Mr. Thomas Massey, tripe dresser, Wilderspool-road. The fire brigade turned out, under Superintendent Turner, and soon extinguished the flames. The damage was slight and is covered by insurance. It is supposed that the fire was caused by an accumulation of soot in the chimney becoming ignited by the heat from a chip potato range, and setting fire to the spars. ASK foe Cu,s•wmaittat's LITG:I-04.4.5s MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Fire at Lower Walton The Wilderspool Fire Brigade were called out at ' about one o'clock on Tuesday morning, to a fire on a farm belonging to Mr. Henry Dodd, at Lower Walton. A large wooden shed, containing about 50 tons of this year's hay, was found to be well ablaze when the brigade arrived, under the superintendence of Mr. Thomas lace, and as it was absolutely impossible to save anything, the fire was allowed to burn out. The damage is, estimated at £2OO, and is covered by insurance. Homing. The Bath young birds' race in connection with the Sankey Bridges Homing Society was flown on Saturday, Mr. S. Taylor liberating 100 birds, belong- ing to 18 members, at 10.30. There was a strong north-west wind. Result : —'l'Roughsedge, velocity, 1,092; Lucas, 1,080 ' • Hayward, 1,071 ; Itoughsedge, 1,069 ; Boyle, 1,067(2); Summerfield,l,o6o; Honitram, 1,054; Boyle, 1,048 ; Hartland, 1,043 ; Greaves, 1,940 ; Lee, 1,030; Wilcock, 1,018 ; Ward, 973 ; Dixon, 969 (6) ; Cope, 946 ; Summerfield, 939; Wheat- ley, 934. '"i‘Tins special. Best average young birds' races; Lucas, 905 ; Boyle and Barlow, 902 ; and Summerfield. 894. H. M. ASHTON'S Lan Tomo takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Choir Trip. The members of the Church of the Sacred Heart choir and friends had their annual outing on Saturday when they visited New Brighton. After a short stay lon the sands at New Brighton the party entered the tower, where a substantial knife and fork tea was provided. After tea the party separated to join in the various amusements, the ballroom proving the chief attraction to the younger members. A start was made for home by the 9.30 boat, and from Liver- pool by the 10.50 train, Warrington being reached at twenty minutes past eleven. Everyone was delighted with the splendid arrangements made and carried out without a hitch by Mr. John Gibbins, the choir- master. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. Oddfellowship. On Saturday a presentation of a framed certificate of merit was made by the members of the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfellows to Past Grand Herbert Sykes, as a mark of appreciation for the able manner in which he had carried out the duties connected with the different offices which he has held. The presenta- tion was made by the Grand Master of the Lodge (P.G. Thomas Reddish) in the absence of the Deputy Grand Master of the district (Bro. T. P. Bradshaw), and who alluded to the excellent manner in which Bro. Sykes had acquitted himself. P.P.G.M. Nor- bury, P.P.G.F. Freeman, and N.G.W. King supported the remarks made by Bro. Reddish. P.G. Sykes thanked the officers, and said he would always do his best for the society. MAIL CARTS and Bassinettes. the latest designs. J. S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —ADvT. Annoying Lady Cyclists. At Crewe, on Wednesday, James Rowlands, of Warrington, was charged with unseemly behaviour towards several lady teachers who were cycling near Crewe. Superintendent Pearson said the prisoner was the fireman of the train in the great railway disaster at Chelford. For weeks he lay between life and death. He had a silver plate on his skull. The accident, he believed, had caused him to go wrong at periods. The prisoner was remanded in custody. On Thursday Rowlands was again brought before the Bench.—Dr. Lawrence, surgeon to the London and North-Western Railway, said, hearing of the ease, he felt it his duty to speak on prisoner's behalf. In 1894 prisoner was firing the Manchester to London express, which was wrecked at Chelford, and a great number of people were killed. Prisoner was thrown from the engine and shockingly injured. For months he lay between life and death, a large piece of bone pressing on his brain. This had to be cut away. When dis- charged from hospital he told him if ever he took alcohol he would go mad. Prisoner now had a silver- plated skull.—The magistrates said the doctor's evidence certainly mitigated the offence, but decency and morals must be protected. Sentence of six weeks imprisonment was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 26, 1905. WARRINGTON POLICE SPORTS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 26, 1905. WARRINGTON POLICE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>1' Revision Courts. Mr. J. T. Foard, barrister, will attend at Warring- ton on September Bth, 9th and 11th, for the purpose of revising the list of persons in the borough entitled to vote at local and Parliamentary elections. Mr. Clement Ephinstone Lloyd will attend at Grappenhall at 11.15 on September 16th, and at Stockton Heath at 2.15 on the same day for the purpose of revising the voters' i list of Cheshire County electors resident n the two districts. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Watch Repairer's Breach of Trust. At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, Ernest Allen, watch repairer, Cheshires Line, was charged with stealing a gold hunter watch, value £l2, the property of Thomas Doraville, by converting it to his own use and remanded. Yesterday he was again brought before the magistrates and also charged with similarly stealing a watch value £2O, the property of Harry White, Old Ball Inn, Winwick-street. —Prisoner denied any felonious intent. He said he pawned the watches to tide over a temporary difficulty. He was sent to gaol for six weeks with hard labour on each charge—three months in all.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. A Terror to His Family. At Warrington on Wednesday, John M‘Adatn. Oliver-street, was committed for six in )nths for ill- treating his five children. Mr. Browne, who appeared on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. stated that the defendant was continually drinking, and the children were in terror of him. He badly assaulted one ''(4l4 and threatened to put a knife through her, and said he would kill the mother and the eldest. He was like a madman in drink, and said if he was imprisoned he would afterwards get a revolver and murder all the family. Defendant's daughter and son said that for some time their lives had been full of misery on account of their father's conduct. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Rev. F. Slater's Last Request The Rev. Francis Slater, M.A., of 8, Abbey-road, Oxford, late vicar of St. Chad's, Over, formerly vicar of St. James', Latchford, and prior to that curate of Wigan, Coatham, and of Bury, who dieg on the 22nd May Last. aged 60 years, left estate of the gross value of £5,638 15s. 6d., of which the net personalty has been sworn at :C2,055 198. sd. The will is written in a small manuscript exercise book, and therein the testator describes himself as a priest of the Anglican branch of the Holy Catholic Church. He desired that he should be buried in the churchyard of Disley, Cheshire, and directed that should he die abroad hts heart should bø cut out and placed on his wife's coffin. He bequeathed a silver cross to the Rev. I Arthur Venables Calvery Hordern, chaplain to the forces, and subject to numerous specific bequests, he left the residue of his estate as to one-fourth each to his sons Cuthbert and Gilbert, and his daughters Hilda and Majorie, directing, however, that his daughter Hilda should pay to his daughter Majorie during spinsterhood, or until she should join a sisterhood, an annuity of £5O.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL MEETING. - - The Warrington police are ever te the fore in efforts on behalf of deserving charities and it is little wonder that they always have the willing support of the townspeople in the promotion of an annual athletic festival in aid of the Infirmary, District Nursing Association, and Police Athletic Chub. It is gratifying to be able to record that Thursday's sports -which were the seventh of their kind-were eminently successful. For one thing the promoters were exceedingly fortunate in their choice of date,ancll the sports were catried out in delightful sunshine. The ` men in blue" are second to none in ticket-selling, and the splendid gate which lined the enclosure was testimony to their work in this direction. About 4,000 people were present and they were thoroughly pleased with the I programme submitted. Sergeant Evans, the inde- fatigable secretary, and his colleagues had gone to great trouble to arrange an attractive series of events, and the public appreciation, which was strikingly manifest, must have been very pleasing to them. There was a record entry of 400, and some of the best known athletes in the district put in an appearance. As usual, the chief interest centred in the bicycle races which were rsplendidly contested, some exciting finishes being witnessed. T. Sherratt, the international rider, heavily handicapped as he was, rode brilliantly in the two mile race and finished first amid cheers. C. Berry, a local favourite, was another popular winner, although on this occasion he had to be content with third place. There were several nasty spills, no fewer than seven competitors being thrown in the one mile final, but no one was seriously hurt. The foot races produced some excellent running, and one of the most acceptable victories to the crowd was that of W. Warren, of Warrington, who is to be com- plimented upon a fine performance in securing first prize in the 100 yards race. The conclud- ing item of the afternoon-the one mile handicap-was certainly one of the best. It was chiefly notable ft)/ the remarkably good racing of F. F. Crawford, of Marylebone C.C. Although he was virtually scratch man he gradually overhauled his opponents and was second to breast the tape. The programme was agreeably varied by a military tourna- ment by men of the Bth and 40th Regimental District under the direction of Sergeant-Instructor Stamford. The soldiers were frequently applauded for their skill in bayonet exercises, movements on the parallel bars, bayonet fighting, vaulting horse, fencing, free gymnastics, and cockade fighting. The Blue Coat School Band, under the conductorship of Mr. Armstrong, played some selections in creditable style. .The arrangements could not have been better carried out, as is evidenced by the fact that the *hole of the nineteen events had been decided by six o'clock. Praise is du; to the following officials for the assistance they rendered : -President: Mr. L. Talbot, Chief Constable; Vice-President : Detective Superintendent Monaghan. Judges: Alder- man D. Tinnion, J.P., Messrs. E. Warren, J. Rabbitt, D. Wyllie and Pickavance, N.C.U. ; Referee: Mr. Thomas Locker; Marksmen : Messrs. A. naoth, E. Crabtree, Birchen, W. Carter, P.C. Robinson, F. Whittaker and J. Beardsall ; Stewards : Inspectors Taylor and Rowbottom, Sergeants Williamson and Barlow,Detectives Meacock and Clough, P.C.'s Newey, Hayes, Stewart, Tolley and Wyse • Clerks of the Course : Chief Inspector Phillips and P.C.'s Hodgkiss and Dennett ; Starter: Mr. T. Wainwright, Salford Harriers ; Secretary: Sergeant W. Evans ; Assistaut Secretary : Sergeant S. Smith.. 100 YARDS FLAT RACE HANDICAP. (CONFINED TO WARRINGTON P3L ICE.) The members of our local force who entered for this ; race showed themselves to be very fleet of foot and the delinquent who attempted to escape from one of these sprinting P.C's should stand a very poor change indeed. P.C. Sweeney was an easy winner but had a big start which helped hint considerably. The second prize went to P.C. Roberts. the third to P.C. Houghton, and the I fourth to P.C. Woodland. The following were the heat !winners :-Ist heat, 1, P.C, Sweeney, 12 yards ; 2, P.C. Roberts, G ; 2nd heat, 1, P.C. Woodland, 5; 2, P.C. Wood 11; 3rd heat, 1, P.C. Berry' 5 ; 2, P.C. Houghton, 0. The time recorded was 11 3-ssecs. Prizes : Ist, walnut bracket chiming clock, value £2 10s. ; 2nd, basket of spoons and cutlery, value £1 15s. ; 3rd, silver- plated Queen Anne teapot, value £1 ; 4th, field glasses, value 12s. 6d. 100 YARDS FLAT RACE HANDICAP (OPEN). There was a splendid entry and some keen racing in this event. The final witnessed a hard struggle, but a supreme effort at the close placed W. Warren of Sankey Harriers and A. C. first. The spectators were much pleased with this triumph of a local athlete. Only about a foot separated Warren from Sydney R. Thompson, Pembroke H., while the third prize went to W. W. Challiner, Prescot A. Co- Th., time occupied was 10 secs. Winners of heats : Sydney It. Thompson, Pembroke H., 10 yards; W. H. Hunter, St. Helens, 5 ; Alfred Glover, Sutton, ; B. Sager, Sefton H., ; R. Lightburn, Blacspool, 41; W. Warren, Sankey, 81 ; W. W. Challiner, Prescot, 8; George McLeavy, Sefton H., 81 : H. Spann, Wigan, 81 : Finnegan, Manchester, 19? ; G. B. Gillett. St. Anne's F.C. 8 ; James Allen, Warrington, 81 : E. Bickerton, Salford fi., 71; E. R. Whitelegge, Manchester, 8 ; F. Maguire, Warrington, 9. The prizes were : Ist, onyx and bronze clock and side , ornaments, value 27 ; 2nd, oak spirit frame, value £2 ; 3rd, silver-plated oak salad bowl, value LI. ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP (OPEN). The final in this race was spoiled by a series of spills. One of the competitors attempted to "cut in" at the corner with disastrous results, six riders being thrown from their machines. Fortunately none were badly hurt. In the next lap another cyclist in taking the same corner collided with a post which marked out the track and fell to the ground. Only three com- petitors were left to finish the race and the prizes were awarded as follows :-1, R. Jolley. Liver- pool; 2, N. H. Stephens, Warrington; 3, Alfred Roberts, Oldham. The time was 2 min. 30 secs. T. Sherratt turned out but made no great effort with the result that he did not win his heat. The heats resulted as follows :-Ist heat, 1, R. Jolley, Liverpool, 93, 2, A.T. Daintith, Warrington, 170 ; 2nd heat, 1, Alfred Roberts, Oldham, 110, 2. Levi Yates, Preston,l6o ; 3rd heat, I,N. M. Stephens, Warrington, 120, 2 T. Waring, Warrington, 185; 4th heat, 1, Johh Waimea, Neston, 120, 2, A. E. Holoird, Warrington, 135 ; sth heat, I, William Eccles- ton,Hindley 180, 2,H. Howard,W.P.R.C.,lBs.Prizes:-lst, marble clock and bronze ornaments, value £5 ; 2nd, silver-plated fern pot, value £2 ; silver-plated cake basket, value £l. 220 YARDS FLAT RACE HANDICAP (OPEN TO POLICE). There was only a small entry for this event but the final was keenly fought, resulting in a victory for Alex Gleave, St. Helens. The winner of the second award was R. Bissey, Manchester, while the third Wa..4 secured by James Ashton, of Liverpool. Winners of heats ; let heat, 1, J. Ashton, Liverpool, 17, 2, A. Cleave, St. Helens. 9,3, Alfred Ardron, Manchester. 14 ; 2nd heat, 1, R. Bissey. Manchester,l4l, 2. Robert Lund,Liverpool, 7,3, Edward Jordan, Liverpool 11. Time 23 1-5 secs. The prizes were : Ist, silver-plated tea urn, value £4 ; I 2nd, silver-plated cheese dish, value £2 ; 3rd, silver- plated egg frame, value £l. 100 YARDS WARRINGTON POLICE RESCUE RACE. This was a new feature and excited considerable interest. Each competitor had to start from scratch, run 50 yards, then pick up a dummy man (weight about ten pounds) who was supposed to be suffocated, and carry him the remaining distance. The "patient" had to be lifted and carried in the way provided in St. John Ambulance rules. P.C. Houghton was an easy winner. P.C. Whitby made it splendid effort hut experienced hard luck in stumbling and had to he content witb second place. The third prize went to P.C. Berry. 800 YARDS FLAT RACE (OPEN). This event was contested with great spirit, there being a capital entry. F. F. Crawford, the well-known athlete of Marylebone C.C., was a much fancied competitor. Although almost scratch (28 yards) be speedily forced his way ahead and finished up third. The winner of the first prize was Arthur Astley. Salford H. (30), and the second went to G. W. Ince, Sefton H. (31). The time was 1 min. 56 2-5 secs. Prizes Ist, combination case of silver plate and cutlery, &amp;c., value £5; 2nd, silver- plated inkstand and flower vase, value £2; 3rd, silver- plated flower stand, £l. 220 YARDS FLAT RACE (OYEN). This race attracted a large entry and there was an exciting finish. J. Ansdell, of Liverpool, was the first to breast the tape, being two yards before J. Barnard, Pembroke H. About a yard and a half separated the last-named from M. J. Kite. Warrington,who was third. The time recorded was 22 2-5 secs. The winners of heats were :-H. Fleetwood, Sefton H., 1; W. Challiner, Prescot, 114 ; J. F. Cowl, Marske-by-Sea, 12 ; J.insdell, Liverpool, 15 : John Moran, Warrington, 6 ; Morris J. Kite, Warrington, 13 ; J. Barnard, Pemkhroko 11., is; G. E. Gillett, St. Anne's, F.C.,1a4 Alfred Glover,Satton, 16. Prizes: Ist, silver-plated Georgian tea and coffee service with oak-mounted tray, value £5; 2nd, silver- plated cut glass six bottled cruet, value £2 : 3rd, cut glass silver-mounted biscuit barrel, value El. TWO MILES BICYCLE HANDICAP (OP EN). From the spectators' point of view thus was perhaps 1 the most interesting event of the afternoon. T. ' Sherratt, of Bamfurlong, although he had to give the competitors a very long start, raced in grand style. He won his heat, with ease and in the final he gradually forced his way to the front and carried off the first prize his victory being loudly acclaimed by the crowd He was two lengths before J. Hancock, of Neston, who came in second, while the third prize went to C. Berry, the popular local rider, who also acquitted himself well. The following were the winners of heats :-Ist heat, 1, T. Sherratt, Bamfurlone. 30 ; 2, T. Barnett. Birkenhead, 283 : 2nd heat., I, N. \V. Stephens. Warrington, 180 ; 2, J. Hancock, Neston, 175 ; 3rd heat, 1, C. Berry, War-</p>
        <p>Frington, 145; 2, R. Jolley, Liverpool, 215; 4th heat, 1, J. Houghton, Warrington, 105, 2, J. Dobson, Bury, 210; sth heat 1, A. E. Howard, Warrington, 210; 2, T. Astill, Warrington, 235. The time was Stains. 20secs. Prizes; Ist, inlaid rosewood bracket quarter chiming clock, value £5 ; 2nd, silver-plated dessert service, value .£2 ; 3rd, silver-plated sardine dish, value £l,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NEXT LIBERAL REGIME.</head>
        <p>THE NEXT LIBERAL REGIME.</p>
        <p>A Forecast.</p>
        <p>So much debate. Parliamentary and otherwise, has' waged with regard to dissolution and the chances of succession that it is interesting to note what a shrewd and far-sighted member has to say of the prospects as nearly as close knowledge of the men and Cabinet con- ditions can assit him. This a" Retiring M.P." does for us in the September nnmber of the " Pall Mall Magazine "as follovc:s.:_—__</p>
        <p>THE CABINET. OFFICE. MINISTERS. Prime Minister (in the Lords) ) First Lord of the Treasury</p>
        <p>- -- ---- - Sir H. 'Camptell-Bannerman Lord Chancellor Mr. Haldane.</p>
        <p>Lord President Lord Tweedmouth. President of Board of Education</p>
        <p>Lord Reay. Lord Privy Seal Lord Carrington. Chancellor of the Exchequer Mr. Asquith. First Lord of the Admiralty Sir Charles Dilke. Home Secretary Mr. Lloyd-George Foreign Secretary (in the Lords) Sir E. Grey.</p>
        <p>War Secretary Lord Elgin. Colonial Secretary Mr. Morley. Indian Secretary Mr. Bryce. Secretary for Scotland • Lord Aberdeen. President of Board of Trade Mr. J. Burns President of Local Government Board</p>
        <p>Lord E. Fitzmaurice. President of Board of Agriculture Mr. E. Channing. Postmaster-General Mr. H. Gladstone. Chief Secretary for Ireland Sir A. Macdonnell NOT IN THE CABINET. OFFICE. MINISTERS. Chancellor of the Duchy Mr. Robertson.</p>
        <p>First Commissioner of Works Lord Carlisle</p>
        <p>Patronage Secretary Mr. L. V. Harcourt. Three Junior Whip 4 Mr. J. A. Pease,</p>
        <p>Captain Sinclair, Master of Elibank Secretary to the Board of Education Dr. Macnamara. Financial Secretary to the Tretsury Mr. Churchill. Secretary to the Admiralty Mr. McKenna. Civil Lord of the Admiralty Captain Norton. Under Secretary, Home Office Mr. Tennant. Under Secretary, Foreign Office Mr. Emmott. Under Secretary, War Office . Mr. Hobhouse. Under Secretary, Colonial Office, Lord Monson. Under Secretary, India Office Lord Beauchamp. Financial Secretary. War Office Major Seely. Under Secretary, Board of Trade Mr. Lough. Under Secretary, Local Government Board M. Trevelyan. Attorney-General Mr. Lawson Walton. Solicitor-General Mr. Robson.</p>
        <p>Unveiling of Altar Stalls. Special services were held at St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church, Latchford, on Sunday,on the occasion of the unveiling of a handsome gift for the high altar of beautiful carved oak stalls for the altar boys,which had been presented to the church by one who desired to remain anonymous. Solemn High Mass was sung at eleven o'clock by the Rev. H. Hazlehurst, D.D., of War- rington, who was ordained priest a short time ago. He was assisted by the Rev. Fathers D'Arcy and Parker (Birkenhead). The preacher at both services was the Very Rev. Father Bernard, 0.5.F.0. (superior of Pant- asaph Monastery. In the course of his address in the morning the rev, father said there was a wave of irreligion spreading over the country. In these days we seemed wanting in faith and were not ever mindful, as we should be, of the greatness of God. God was not one of the men of history, who passed away like Napoleon, or other prominent figures of the times. He was living now and was with them all the time. It was to Him they must look for grace and peace—Him who was the source of all goodness. Special music was rendered morning and evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>This event confined to the staff of the Royal Court and Royal Theatres, resulted as follows :-1, Jack Gleave, scratch; 2, A. Newnes, 20; 3, S. Pomfret, 30. Time 3 MillS. 33 secs. ONE mir.k BICYCLE HANDICAP. (WARRINGTON POLICE IN UNIFORM.) The appearance of the police cyclists in uniform created some amusement. P.C. Berry, the scratch man, looked a winner throughout and came in first by about 20 yards. The second prize went to P.C. Whitby, (20), .1 and the third to P.C. Hughes (15). Prizes : Ist, spring regulater clock, value £1 55.; 2nd case silver-plated teaspoons, value 155.; 3rd, silver- plated breakfast cruet. value 108.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ONE MILE FLAT RACE.</head>
        <p>ONE MILE FLAT RACE.</p>
        <p>This the concluding event of the sports provided a very interesting contest. F. F. Crawford, of Maryle- bone C.C., who was virtually scratch (47), maintained a capital speed throughout and carried off the second prize. The first went to Ernest Walker, Manchester H. (113) and the third to Charles Dennis, Winnington Park (102). Elliott Harris, the well known footballer, made a very praiseworthy effort, and seemed certain to capture the third prize but was overtaken in the straight in the last lap. The time was 4mins. 18 1-ssecs. Prizes : Ist, combination case, value £5 ; 2nd, ink stand, value £2; 3rd, flower stand, value £l. At the close of the sports the Nrayoress (Mrs. Bolton) distributed the prizes.</p>
      </div>
