      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S FINANCIAL POSITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FINANCIAL POSITION.</p>
        <p>HOW THE RATEPAYERS' MONEY IS SPENT.</p>
        <p>Some Interesting Figures.</p>
        <p>The Borough Treasurer (Mr. John Fairhurst,F.S.A.A.) has this week issued his annual abstract of the accounts of the Corporation of Warrington for the year ended 31st March last. The volume comprises 199 pages and gives a mass of details respecting the expenditure:and income of the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BALANCE SHEET.-WARRINGTON'S INDEBTEDNESS.</head>
        <p>THE BALANCE SHEET.-WARRINGTON'S INDEBTEDNESS.</p>
        <p>Accompanying the accounts is an aggregate balance statement of the liabilities and assets of the Corpora- tion on the 31st March last. The total liabilities are set forth as amounting to £1,236,632 9s. 9d. and the assets to £1,634,692 12s. ed., thus showir'v, a balance of assets amounting to .£397,970 2s. 9d. The balance sheet shows the liabilities and assets in connection with the following funds :</p>
        <p>Property Assets and Liabilities. Outlay. £ s. d. £ S. d. Borough Rate 82,853 19 7 Improvement Rate 30,950 5 1 Sewers Rate Museum &amp; Library 790 15 11 Education Parr Hall Police Superannuat'n 5 12 6 Privatelmprovement Expenses 11,084 13 2 Gas Works Water Works Electricity Tramsways Middleton Scholar-</p>
        <p>ships l,llO 8 5 Fire Engine Renewal --- 474 1 2 Latchford &amp; Cider-</p>
        <p>spool Causeways --- Walker's Gift -</p>
        <p>1,236,632 9 9 1,634,602 12 6 COST OF THE POLICE FORCE.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Watch Department the expen- diture totalled £7,705 12s. Sd. The largest item in the account is that for constables' wages, which absorb £5,720 ss. 21. In addition a sum of £95 12s. Gd. was paid as boot allowance, and £23 be. in lieu of uniform. Clothing and accoutrements cost £293 ISs. Sd., and a year's medical attendance on constables cost £3l 10s. The expenditure on the maintenance of prisoners whilst in the bridewell was £32 I3s. 4d., and there was an additional sum of £95 Gs. 9d. for the conveyance of prisoners. The receipts in this department include the sum of £3,230 3s. 2d., being a contribution from the National Exchequer for one half the charge for pay and clothing of police. His ,Nrajesty's Paymaster General contributes .£lOB Ss. 4d. for the conveyance of prisoners to gaol. The propor- tion of fines and penalties imposed by the Borough justices and handed over to this account was £Ol 25., to , which must be added £lO 18s. for fees. The registration oflinotor vehicles brought in £27 13s. to the funds, and licences for driving motor vehicles £lB. In the Watch Department the expenditure exceeded t'le receipts by £4,125 9s. 9d. THE CEAETER.Y.</p>
        <p>In the Cemetery department, gardeners' and labourers' wages amounted to £474 is. 6d., and there was an expenditure of £6O on painting the chapels, &amp;c., and £27 ss. 9d. on re-casting the bell. The burial fees paid to the clergy during the year were as follows :—Rector of Warrington, £43 15s. 4d. ; Vicar of St. Paul's, £24 12s. ; Vicar of Holy Trinity, £l2 Gs. Gd. ; Vicar of , St. llarnahas' £l2 2s. Scl. : Vicar of St. Peter's, £27 16s. Bd. ; Vicar of St. Ann's. £l7 18s. 2d. ; Vicar of St. James', £l5 9s. 2d. The receipts included £373 7s. for purchased gr tves, and £lB3 4s. for common or unbought graves. Fees charged after first interment azaounted to £239 Gs., and for excavating the fees totalled £275 16s. Gd. The total income during the year was £1,569 2s. 91, and after deducting expanses there is a credit balance of £442 Bs. 11d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TOWN HALL, PARKS AND BATHS.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL, PARKS AND BATHS.</p>
        <p>Electric light and fittings at-the Town Hall have during the year cost £174 ss. 3i, and maintenance and alterations of buildings, furniture and sundry repairs have involved an expenditure of £lO3 14s. Gd. In the parks department £731 4s. 2d. has been disbursed in gardeners', labourers', and park keepers' wages. £42 2s. 101. has been spent on trees, plants and seeds, and £3B Os• 11d, on food for the birds and fishes. A con- tribution of £7O has been made to the Bands Associa- tion. The laying of the public bowling green in Victoria Park cost the department £73 Os. Id, and in addition £97 Ss. 9d. was paid to the Highways depart- ment for work done in connection with the green. On the income side, £23 2s. 4d. was received for the hire of plants. In the baths department the chief item of ,1 expenditure is £228 Hs. 10d. for superintendent's and I assistant's wages and £49 12s. 2d. has been paid to the Highways department for assistance given. The water bill was £175 ss. 2d. while snap and cleaning materials ran away with a sum of .C 44 3s. 103. Receipts from bathersamountecl to 043 2s. 7d. The total income was .£553 Hs. 10d.and as the expenditure was £862 7s. Gd.the baths cost the ratepayers during the year £3OB 12s. Sd.</p>
        <p>RIVERS DEPARTMENT AND GYMNASIUM. The rivers department has spent £195 ss. gd., £lOO being absorbed by the salary of Mr. Schofield Jackson, river surveyor. £GB 14s. •7d. was paid to employes of the highways department for assisting in the survey of the river.</p>
        <p>_ The total exnenditura in the Gymnasium department was £539 16s. 10d. and the income £BlO 45., thus cost- ing the ratepayers .£223 12. 10d. The principal icem of income is .£2OO from the Education department for the instruction of school children. FINANCE DEPARTNIENT.</p>
        <p>The salaries in the Town Clerk's office amounted to .C 1,451 ; Borough treasurers office, £2,243 2s ; Borough Surveyor's office, £1,217 16s. Bd. ; Clerk to Borough Justices' office (including certain expenses) £534 ss. Id. The expenses and subscription re Corporate Treasurer's Institute atuounted to £7 12s. fid. Bank interest ana commission absorbed £635 12s. 7d.</p>
        <p>COUNTY AND OCHER CHARGES. Various'county charges absorbed £1,669 16s.and under the heading of exchequer contribution account there is an expenditure of £7,179 ss. 3d. This includes a payment of £1,598 19s, 53. to the Guardians of the Warrington Union for proportion of salaries of Union officers and medical appliances, while £122 3s. id. was paid to Drs. Peacock and Bennett for services rendered as public vaccinators. On the exchequer contribution account the income is £7787 19s. id. GENERAL PURPOSES.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF THE TRADING INQUIRY AND</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL ACTION.</p>
        <p>lii the General Purposes Department election expenses totalled £IIS 14s. Id. The charges of Parlia- mentary agents in respect of the Cheshire Electricity Bill were £2ss. 103., and of the South Lancshire.Tram- ways Bill £l5 10s. 23. Professional services and expenses of witnesses, re action against Man- chester Ship Canal Company, have involved the rate- payers in an expenditure of £2,731 us. 101 Expenses to London re Associations of Municipal Corporations amounted to £23 15s. 6d.; re rating of ground values £4; re widening of Warrington Bridge Zti Is. 83.; re miscellaneous matters £2O 3s. 7d. For expenses re non-trading inquiry a sum of £334 14s. 93. is set down and in addition the cost of printing reports and evidence was £77 lls. 3d. The sale of the reports realized ,C 7 17s. The total expenditure was £4,325 16s. and the income £153 9s. 6d. The balance overdrawn on March 31st, 1905, was £1,156 Gs. sd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PROFITS ON THE MARKETS.</head>
        <p>PROFITS ON THE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>The markets again prove a proflt-tble source of revenue, the total receipts being £5,359 3s. Id. Of this sum £3,959 Gs. 2d. was received for fair and market tolls. The expenditure amounted to £3,217 I3s. 7d. including £6OO an instalment of the cost of the exten- sion of the markets. The profit made in this depart- ment was £2,140 9s. td., a reduction as compared with last year of £4Ol 18s. sd. The weights and measures department received £3OO 10s. Bd. and expended £2Ol 17s. 51, expenses of deputations totalling £lB 19s. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HOSPITAL AND HEALTH DEPARTMENT.</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL AND HEALTH DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The income of the Hospital Department amounted to £313 Is. 4d. The Newton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council paid .£lOO for the use of the hospital, and the Warrington Rural District Council £95 13s. Sd., a Idecrease in each instance. The infections diseases I hospital expenses amounted to £3,958 7s. Gd., and the small-pox hospital expenses to £352 Ils. Sd. For men's itime in disinfecting an expenditure of £156 9s. Gd. was I incurred. The notification of infectious disease cost the Borough £l6l 65., and expenses in connection with the small-pox expidemic amounted to £lB 9s. Gd. 'During the year horse hire cost .£159 19s. 10d.. COST OF SANITARY WORK.</p>
        <p>In the sanitary works department, the expenses in connection with the Longford manure works amounted to £3,861 IBs. Bd., Mr. Duncan's ex- periments costing £935. The intercepting depots cost during the year .£935 16s. 2d: night-soil collection £7,036 35., and the scavenging department .£3,478 10s. lid. Longford depot extensions involved the rate- payers in an expenditure of £2.042 19s. 3d. THE HIGHWAYS.</p>
        <p>Highway work in Warrington during the year cost £15,179 13s. 3d. The receipts mainly for doing work for other Corporation departments, amounted to £3,936 13s. 7d. A year's lighting of the streets cost £4,397 16s. lid</p>
        <p>BRIDGE•STREET IMPROVENIENT. The purchase of property in Bridge-street has cost £1,762 18s. 10d., and there is a payment of £609 2s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>for professional services. The sale of old materials ha* realised £173 3s.</p>
        <p>RECREATION GROUNDS. The expense of laying out the Liverpool-road recreation ground amounted to £360 10s., and for the purchase of iron fencing Ac., for the Orford-lane recreation ground £l7l 2s. Bd. was spent. THE MUSEUM AND LIBRARY. The expenditure in connection with these institutions was £1,698 19s. Gcl., including ISS 17s. 10d. on books, £72 18s. 2d. on periodicals and £64 Os. 3d, for bind- ing. Excavations at Wilderspool cost £34 rt. The income was £53 15s. 3d., the chief item being £42 2s. lid., fines paid by borrowers.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION ACCOUNTS. Elementary education in the borough has during the Tear cost £33,472 7s. Id. The largest item of the expendi- ture being £25,0i5 17s. 4d. for salaries of teachers. The salaries of officers other than teachers amounted to £1,497 ISd. Books and aparatus cost £1075 IGs. 2d. land fuel,light and cleaning £3,059 us. sd. The amount ! raised from the borough rate for elementary education : was £7,406 I`.:s. 9d. nod Government grants totalled £24,394 13s. 9d. School fees realized £2.473 16s. 51. On higher education the expenditure was £6,774 9s. 9d. including £1,421 15s. 3d. for salaries of teachers at the secondary school and pupil teachers centre and £1,933 us. 10d. for evening school teachers' salaries.Of this sum the ratepayers pro- vided 513,001 Gs. Bd. and grants from the Board of educa- tion amounted to £1,754 19s. 9d. The total fecs received ! from students amounted to £321 14s. 10d. The account for the Middleton scholarship shows that after granting rme scholarship there is a balance to be carried forward of .£llO Ss. sd.</p>
        <p>PARR HALL PROFITS. The profit on the hiring of the Parr Hall during the yetr was exceptionally low, having dropped from £6-4 13s. 10d. in 1904 to £ll 7s. lid.. depite the fact that the payments for the hire of the building show an increase of £l2O Os. 4d. The total receipts for hiring the hall were £617 ss. Gd. Repairs to the building cost. £143 Is. lid.</p>
        <p>POLICE SUPERANNUATION. The police superannuation fund at date amounts to £14,701 2s. 2d. The money is invested in the Warring- ton, Manchester, St. Helens, Bolton and Stockport Cor- porations. Pensions have beau paid during the year amounting to £235 2s. Bd. SIR PETER WALKER'S GIFT. The balance in hand on this account stands at £341 12s. Bd. The only item of expenditure during the year has been £2O for the renovation of a portrait.</p>
        <p>THE GAS WORRS PROFIT. The gas works account shows that/the profit for the 3 ear ended 31st March was £18,94R 19s. 3d. From this must be deducted the amount paid for one year's interest, annuities and sinking fund amounting to i 18,541. 7s. 7d. leaving £10,323 19s. 11d, to be devoted to the relief of the rates.</p>
        <p>THE WATERWORKS. The gross profit on the water undertaking was £l5, 211 13s. 2d. Payments for interest and annuities amounted to £13,225 lie. 9d., and the year ended with a balance in hand of £6,250 Bs. 6d. Well-sinking and adit driving has cost £3,699 13s. The accounts of this department are remarkable for the fact that they are not signed by the auditors. ELECTRICITY DEPARTMENT. The total expenditure on the electricity works to date has been £78,395 18s. 11d., the expenses during the past year amounting to £4,770 18s. 11d. The loans repaid during the year amounted to £2,561 18s sd. The revenue account shows a net profit of £1,543 12s. 9d. The sale of current per meter realized £8,148 12s, 6d. ; public lighting £620 ; current for trams, £3,501 Bs. Gd. THi?. TRAMWAYS. The net profit on the working of the tramways during the year was £l5l. This sum has gone to the reduc- tion of the previous year's loss and there is now a deficiency of £242. The receipts per car mile averaged Sankey 6•7 d., Latchford 10.64 d., Wilders- pool 15.32 d., Cemetery 10•52 d., Longford 5•43 d. The total income from fares was £16,599 is. 3d., and ad- vertising on-cars brought in .£2ll 13s. lid. Interest on capital amounted to £3,415 19s. and loans repaid amounted to £1,455. The working expenses per car mile averaged 6•308 d. and ieceipts 10•025 d.</p>
        <p>ASSESSABLE VALUE AND RATES LEVIED. In a return showing the assessable value and rates levied since the incorporation of the borough it appears that the present assessable value to the improvement rate is .C227,30i as compared n ith .£224,57G last year. The average rate levied for the past nine years has been. Gs. Bd. in the whereas the average for the preceding ten years was ss. 9d. in the 4.</p>
        <p>- AN ECHO OF THE WAR. Particulars of the Transvaal Campaign relief fund are contained in the abstract. Pensions were paid to widows and orphans amounting to £B9 ss. and allow- ances were also made to sick slldiers and dependents to the extent of £9 Is. The amount of the fund at data is £1,253 12s. Ild.of which £1,200 is invested inWarrington ICorporation bonds.</p>
        <p>THE GRAMNIAR SCHOOL. The accounts in connection with this school show that the receipts were £1,831 10s. and the expenditure 4:1,987 15s. 3d. and the year closed with a bitlance owing to the bank of £278 IS$. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WARRINCTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINCTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will he held on Monday, when a light agenda will be presented.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>STAFF CHANGES.</head>
        <p>STAFF CHANGES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Management Committee, it was resolved that the resignation of Mr. John H. Bankes, as assistant master in the Boys' department of the Warrington Parochial school, to take effect on Sep- tember 30th, be accepted, and that a successor be appoin- ted.--It was reported that paragraphs with reference to the applintment of the senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school, were not approved. After careful consideration, it was resolved that Miss Elizabeth Frost be appointed senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school, at a commencing salary in 11,,cordance with the scale (I:125 per annum), as front the Ist August, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION.</head>
        <p>PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>A letter nas submitted to the School Management Committee from the Town Clerk, asking for a meeting to be arranged between members of this committee and a sub-committee of the Gymnasium Committee. It was resolved that Mr. J. D. Buckton, Mr. Henry Roberts, and the Chairman of the Committee (the Rev. Canon Stevenson) be appointed to represent the Committee in the matter.</p>
        <p>SCHOLARSHIPS. A report on the examination recently held for the "Charles Middleon " scholarships was submitted, and, as no candidate had attained the re9uisite standard for the senior scholarships, the Committee decided not to award any of those scholarships this year. It was resolved that "Charles Middleton" scholarships (junior) be awarded to eleven boys in accordance with the scheme approved by the Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>THi7i GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THi7i GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A letter was submitted from the Law Clerk to the Governors of the Grammar School, asking the Com- mittee to receive a deputation from the Governors to discuss the question of the making of an interim grant by the Committee to the school, pending the receipt of the new amending scheme. for the government of the school. The Director was requested to arrange the date for the meeting with the Law Clerk to the Governors.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. At a meeting of the Sites and Banding; Committee it was resolved that the tender of Mr. Harry Fairclongh amounting to £1.85, for the extension of the lavatory accommodation at the Secondary school, be accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>QUESTION OF IDENTITY.</head>
        <p>A QUESTION OF IDENTITY.</p>
        <p>Snail to a Cycling Accident at Warrington,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court. on Thursday, his Honour Judge Bowen Rowland; K.C., resumed the hearing of the action in which Thomas Forster, Peasley Cross Lane, St. Helens, sued James Cook, round-about employee, for damages for injury to his bicycle alleged to have been caused by defendant's negligence.—Mr. Kelly, St. Helens. appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. A. Browne defended.</p>
        <p>The facts of the case as stated by the plaintiff were that on the evening of June 29th 12. e was cycling along Walton New !toad, Warrington, when he was knocked down by a horse and trap driven, he alleged, by the defendant. Plaintiff was riding on his proper side and the only warning he had before being knocked down was that someone shouted "Heigh,up !" He was slightly injured but the bicycle was almost wrecked, and it would cost £7 to repair. Plaintiff had no doubt that defendant was the driver, but said that at the time of the accident defendant had a moustache which had since been shaved off.—Mr. Houghton and Harry Williams, gave similar evidence of identity both men agreed that the man had a moustache on day of the accident. The defence was a total denial of liability. Defend- ant said he was working with his round-a-bouts on a field adjoining the Orford Barracks at the time of the accident and was never near Walton New-road. Plaintiff said the man who collided with him had a moustache, but defendant's moustache was shaved off in the first week in June. Defendant called several witnesses as to his where- abouts on the night of the accident and a barber stated that he shaved defendant's moustache off about the first week in June. His Honour gave judgment for the defendant without. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WORD TO THE WISE.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE WISE.</p>
        <p>The fickle nature of the climate of this country often gives rise to a variety of ailments and complaints, which assuming at first the form of only a slight indisposition, if neglected and unheeded may become the germ of serious diseases, which will undermine the constitution, and finally result in a long and lingering illness, and perhaps terminate fatally. The variable temperature and the changeable weather of the last month or so, makes it incumbent on all of us to exercise wise and prudent precautions to counteract the evil effects which the weather may have had upon our health and comfort. Already we frequently hear such complaints as "No appetite," "These frequent headaches," "This languid feeling," and dozens of other expressions which all point to the urgent need of a good Tonic. Now there are several tonic mixtures offered to the Public, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. This Preparation is acknowledged to be The Best Remedy of The Age for Nervousness, Weakness. Chest Affections, Palpitation of the Heart, Indigestion, Liver Complaint, and Influenza. The numerous cases of marvellous cures effected, and the restoration to perfect health, of those who had long suffered and had vainly tried other remedies, can be accounted for by simply stating the effect which Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has upon the System. Ist. It strikes at the source of the disease, and by removing the causes of disease, the evil effects soon vanish. _</p>
        <p>2nd. It strengthens that part of the system which is weakest, and therefore, most liable to the attacks of colds and all diseases. 3rd. It purifies the blood, and thus gives new life and force to all parts of the body, for dis.ase cannot exist where there is a free circulation of pure blood- 4th. It gives healthy action to the digestive organs and to the liver, thus aiding to keep away Indigestion and the host of different forms of disease which re- sult from it, sth. By removing impurities, strengthening the weak parts of the system, and purifying the blood, the human frame is well fortified to withstand the attacks of disease. It is repelled, and its place taken by health and strength, through the effective action of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. Sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. (id. each, or will be sent carriage free for these prices, direct from the Sole Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Beware of Imitations. See that you get the genuine Preparation with the name," Gwilym Evans "on Label, Stamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON HAWKER IN 'I not:stn.— CHARGE OF RECEIVING 6TOLEN BRASS. At Widnes on Saturday Peter Grady, Margaret-street, Widnes,was charged witn stealing a quantity of valuable brass,and Joseph Sutton,hawker, Turner's-yard, Warring- ton, was charged with receiving it.—lnspector Strick- land, in opening the case, said that owing to numerous complaints of this sort be had placed constables on the Warrington approach road to crotch the Warrington rag and metal gatherers. On Friday Police-constable Howarth stopped Sutton on Cuerdley Bridge, and upon examination of his cart he found the brass produced in court hidden away under the rags. Not being able to give a satisfactory account about it, he was brought to the station, and from a description he gave of Grady the latter was arrested later on.- -Michael Griffen, aged fourteen, Turner-street, War- rington, said he was employed by Sutton, and on Friday morning they came to Widnes hawking rubbing-stones. About elevea o'clock they were in the neighbourhood of Grady's house. Sutton afterwards went to the Cor- poration's tip in Moor-lane. Grady joined them and asked Sutton to bring a bag and fetch some brass. In 3 field close to, Grady unearthed the spoil, and placed it on the cart. Grady asked is. for the lot, but only got ss. 6d.—At the station later Grady, it transpired, had volunteered a statement that he had stolen the lubricator from Golding Davies's works, and also that Sutton, when he bought the stuff, did not know it had been stolen.—A remand was granted until Monday when prisoners were further remanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. H. Thornton, S Wright and W. Roberts. '</p>
        <p>FOO2I3ALL IN THE STREET.—Five boys named James Somerset, 54, Kerfoot-street; James Barton, 38, Kerfoot-street ; John Hewitt, 32. Kerfoot-street ; Joseph Hill, 2, Russell-street, and Thomas Coyn, 2, Side Kerfoot-street ; were summoned for playing football in Russell-street, on August loth.—PC. Lewis proved the case.—Hewitt, who denied the offence, was discharged, and the other !boys were only fined 25.6 d. on promistou not to play football in the streets again. YOUTHFUL GAMBLERS.—'fhroe boys named Joseph Rankin, 82, Pierpoint-street; William Humphreys, 12, Philip-street; and Edward Jones, 6, Philip-street, were summoned for playing with cards for money in a public passage off Friar's-gate on August lOth.—P.C. Holland proved the visa—Humphreys, who made his third appearance, and who said he was only watching the other boys, was ordered to pay the costs of the summons (3s. 6d.) The other two boys, who admitted playing for halfpennies, were fined 2s. Gd. and warned by the Mayor.</p>
        <p>TuisoAr.—Before Dr. Fox (presiding), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and IV. Richardson.</p>
        <p>SCENE IN BUTTERAIARKET-3TREET.—Thomas Davies, last from Bolton, was charged with having been drunk and disorderley in Butterrnarket-street, and with assaulting William Lawson, sen., and William Lawson, jun.—Davies had been lodging with the prosecutor, who keeps a coffee house at 54, Buttermarket-street, and went home to Bolton at the week end. He came back on Monday under the influence of drink. He used abominable language to a customer and to Mrs. Lawson, and the son then endeavoured to put him out. Defendant struck him violently in the face. Mr. Lawson, sen., went to his Eon's assistance and was also struck in the face. Defendant was eventually put into the atreet, where he created a disturbance, a large crowd collecting.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GALE PLAYS HAVOC AT NEWTON. MARQUEE BLOWN DOWN.</head>
        <p>GALE PLAYS HAVOC AT NEWTON. MARQUEE BLOWN DOWN.</p>
        <p>The north-western gale on Saturday morning played great havoc at Newtonle-lVillows Horlcultural Society's Show. The exhibitors were busy arranging their plants flowers, and vegetables, which were particularly num- erous, when about eight o'clock the gale clean swept a marquee to destruction, smashing the poles and causing tremendous damage to the exhibits. Unfortunately the best plants were practically destroyed, and several of the visitors had extremely narrow escapes from injury through the falling poles and spars. At half-past eleven the refreshment marquee barely escaped a similar fate,the broken ringe pole just missing several:occupants . An improvised show was held in the open air under very unfavourable circumstances, and with dire misfortune to the exhibitors, who had made special efforts for the best show of its kind in the district.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Flull, Peter Williams, who gave him- self up to the police confessing that he had murdered Ellen Barrill, was remanded by the magistrates. On Sunday he led the police to a cornfield where the woman's body was. He says that the woman asked him to kill her, and when he refuse 4 she cut her throat. Then he took hold of the razor'and "finished her off."</p>
        <p>oin-E rE 14. ASI \E • T- &lt;, / Ithss F. Jubb, Chapolthorpe Hall, 91 near Wakefield, writes :—" I was / on my feet so much that my left foot began to blister and to be very painful. The second toe .7.•„5. gathered, and got so bad that -/• I could not bear my boot on. 0, I tried all kinds of things P. . without success, and then a V' friend told me to get some g Zara-Buk. I did so, and 1., when I had used it a few /.4i times it gave me relief. In a few days I could wear my boot, and my • foot is now quite better, .0, thanks to Zam-Buk." .2/// , Is RUE: IT IN." 1 . ' . . 'l4lllllll and r 9 per box, (.)f a/4 chrniatt and Zani-Bak Co., Red Cross Street. &amp;C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Successful Warrington Students.</p>
        <p>The results of the Oxford Local Examinations were announced on Monday. Boys were examined at 186 and girls at 213 centres. The total number of candidates examined was 14,222, as compared with 14,149 last year, and 9,732 certificates were awarded as against 9,805 in 1904. The senior candidates numbered 3,646, the juniors 6,960, and the preliminary 3,616. Of these 2,416 seniors passed, 4,861 juniors, and 2,455 preliminary. Appended is a list of the successful local candidates.</p>
        <p>SENIOR CANDIDATES.—Honours List: Framed upon the aggregate work of the candidates, and containing the names of those who satisfied the examiners, and obtained honours and the title of associate in arts : First Class, in order of merit, 72 candidates—M. J. Burgess, Warrington Grammar School, 47th, bracketed with several others. Second Class, in order of merit, 142 candidates—R. Kennon, Warrington Grammar School, first bracketed with others. Third Class, arranged in numerical order under the several centres, 410 candidates—T. B. Weaver, Grammar School. Pass List—First Division, framed upon the aggregate work of the candidates, and containing the :runes of the senior candidates born on or after July Ist, 1885, who satisfied the examiners and obtained the title of Associ- ate of Arts; arranged in numerical order;l,sso candidates —R. S. M. F. M`Murray and C. S. Woods, Warrington Grammar School, and D. Price, Priory School. Respon- sions—The following cand dates have shown sufficient merit to be excused from the examinitions in stated subjects (79 candidates) : R. Kennon and R. S. M. F. 111`Murray, Warrington. OXFORD UNIVERSIEY EXAMINATIONS FOR WOMEN.— Distinctions—The following obtained distinction, in order of merit : Religious Knowledge (60 candidates) —J. B. Hay, Carlisle, and M. J. Burgess, Warrington, seventh, bracketed equal with three others; R. Kennon, Warrington, 22nd, bracketed equal with thirty-nine others.</p>
        <p>JUNIOR CANDIDATES.—Honours List.— Containing the names of those who satisfied the examiners and obtained honours. First Class—ln order of merit (131) candidates —R. Wilcox (Warrington Grammar School, 43rd, bracketed equal with seven others ; A. Wright, Warrington Grammar School, 76th, bracketed equal with 24 others- Second Class—ln order of merit (292 candidates),—R. Lee, Warrington Grammar School, Ist Ibracketed equal with twenty-eight others ; G. F. Greaves and W. M. Watkins, Warrington Grammar School. 205th, bracketed equal with over eighty others. Pass List—Containing the names of the candidates born on or after July Ist, 1889, who satisfied the Examiners. First Division—Arranged in numerical order under the several centres(2,4sB candidates). Warrington A. Bennie, N. ft Dunn, M. Forshaw and E. B. Partington, High School ; M. D. Hall and A. Isherwood, Priory School. Second Division—containing the names of the candidates who having exceeded the age of sixteen years on July Ist 1905, satisfied the examiners (1,020 candi- dates),R. M. Carnson, V. C. Roberts, and J. Whitehouse, Warrington Grammar School ; G. C. Hall, Priory School :F. Warburton,High School. Distinctions : The undermentioned candidates, arranged in order of merit, obtained distinction in—English Language and Litera- ture, 06 candidates—R. Wilcox (Warrington), bracketed equal 30th with ten others- Greek, 25 candidates—R. tWilcox, bracketed 14th with two others ; A. Wright Warrington), bracketed equal 14th with seven others. PRELIMINARY CANDIDATES.—Pass listafirst division: Warrington, E. Bennett, D. Bracecamp, and E. Broome, High school. Second Division : A. Kenyon, L. Percival and H. G. Wilson, High school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS HINT.</head>
        <p>A NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS HINT.</p>
        <p>Here is a hint that will prove helpful to many War- rington men and women. It is given by one of our neighbours—a Newton-le-Willows woman. We publish the following letter from Mrs. R Cook, of 45, Go'borne Dale-road, Newton-le-W 110 ws,beca use we feel sure it will interest our readers, and benefit not a few of them. Mrs. Cook writes :—" Dear Sirs,—l shall recommend your Doan's backache kidney pills whenever I get an opportunity, because my experience with the medicine has been so splendid. I was taken suddenly some time ago with a sharp, cutting pain in my back. It seemed to affect my stomach, and I suffered terribly. " I went on in this way for two years, and at last my health got reduced to a very low state. I used to feel tired and worn out on rising in the mornings. The pain spread to my side, and the heart's action was irregular. Every cold seemed to settle in my back and kicine3 s." "Hearing that Doan's backache kidney pills were a special medicine for troubles like mine, I got some to try, and they did me good from the tirit. lam thank- ful now that I tried these pills, for I feel quite a different woman. Yours truly, Signed (Mrs.) R. Cook." Doan's backache kidney pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shiilings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores' or post free, on receipt of price, direct from Foster, McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get the same kind of pills as Mrs. Cook had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. Yonge and the Cotton Crisis.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday, the Rev. John Yonge referred to the question of peace, speaking from the text," Now the Lord of peace Himself give you peace always, by all means. The Lord be with you all !" They remembered he said that the characteristic of peace was always associated with the promise and with the advent of Jesus Christ. The Hebrew prophets,when testifying beforehand of the Messiah's coming,were wont to speak of Hire as "Prince of Peace," and they added the assurance that "of the increase of his government and peace there should be no end." "They shall beat their swords into plough- shares, and their spears into pruning hooks ; nation shall not lift up sword against nation,neither shall they learn war any more." "He maketh wars to cease unto the ends of the earth." These were predictions which it might seem as though the history of the centuries had falsified. But we might not so judge. We had been living all our lives in the midst of manifold and more or less continual strifes and conflicts. At home we had known very bitter conflicts between Capital and Labour, which had entailed sore trouble and grievous loss and suffering upon all classes. We had had, also, our foreign and colonial we rs,and theirconsequentsacri- fief s and miseries, and further we had been—and we now were—witnesses of tremendous wars between other nations. At the present time we were in the very midst of a deeply interesting series of events, all of which apyeared to be of peaceful promise. The county in which they lived, through the mercy of God and the good sense and humanity of men, had been spared the wretched experience of a great strike in its premier , industry. There was a deep thankfulness in multitudes of working class homes, and also in the dwellings of thr manufacturers, on account of the peaceful settle- ment arrived at in Manchester last 'Thursday night. Had that happy termination not been reached, that Sunday would have been for Lancashire a very dark and cheerless day. But both sides saw the higher wisdom of mutual concession, and consequently there was peace. Were we not to recognize that, consciously or unconsciously, the Spirit of Christianity had been at work on both sides of that controversy ? Had not " the Lord of Peace " been influencing the , thought and disposition even of men who may have been ignorant or sceptical or uncon- scious of His grace? Had not high Christian principle and Christian temper been influencing men on both sides to remember the great Master's pure and peaceful will, and to strive their best to give effect to it? There had also been quite recently yet other events in the country which were contributory to peace and goodwill. The establishment of cordial and friendly relations between this country and France, and also , Spain, Portugal and Italy, might be regraded by superficial and perhaps cynical minds as a merely ! diplomatic achievement. The constant endeavonrs 'of our Sovereign to promote European peace might also be discounted by the same kind of selfish opinion. But should they not be rather cln strued, by such as ourselues at any rats, as encouraging I evidences of the development of the Christian p3licy of international peace as the truest means of general i Welfare? Were they not the rays of the dawning of the I better thy, heralding the coming' of the predicted ; supreamy of" the Lord of Peace" ? Christian faith, at any rate, should hail them with thankfulness and ,deli;ht. Most supremely of all, however, however, , were the anxieties and the hopes of men concentrating upon that conference in America between Russia and Japan. No one could yet confidently forecast the result. In a very intense and supreme sense "the lot is being cast into the lap and the whole disposing of it is with the Lord." Surely, a ground of hopeful expectancy was discoverable in that un- questionable fact. He with Whom disposal rested—and Who would over-rule the issue even if to them it might seem most adverse—was " the Lord of Peace." He knew how to cause the wraths and conflicts of men to praise Him, and the re.naieder of their angers he could restrain. It had been the concomitance of all those great events that had helped to draw his (Mr. Yongels) thoughts,in the direction of his text. They seemed to make one feel that " Peace is in the air." And they prompted them to pray that what was in the air might speedily descend and dwell upon the earth ; that men might sit at the feet of the great Master of all" King of kings and Lord of lords," and learn of Him to adjust their differences, settle their qualrels, and realize their universal brotherhood. If the churches and the pulpits of Christian lands w mld ever advocate and promote the policy of peace and gobdwill, they would prove them- selves more loyal to their Lord and truer servants of he race which He came to redeem and save.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Long Stratton (Norfolk) Gaardians, it was reported that inmates of the work- honse had been allowed to go into the harvest field, receiving a glass of beer for what they did, but no wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>The. Examiner, Saturday August 26, 1905.</head>
        <p>The. Examiner, Saturday August 26, 1905.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>--- Proposed Resident Medical Officer.</p>
        <p>DR. BRAME'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. J. Billinge pre- siding. There were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs' Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. Dr. Mercer Dr. Patterson, Messrs. W. Hickman (vice-chairman); A. J. Jolley, It. Forshaw, J. W. Knowles, .1. Payton, R. Bolton, T. H. Ashcroft, P. Longton, G. Thompson, W. Houghton, A. Brookes. R. Flanagan, T. Ritchie, G. Mather, J. C. Sutton (clerk), and J. F. Awbery (master) PROPOSED RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER. LETTER FROM DR, BRAIDE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee on Angust 11th considerable discussion took place with reference to the nursing arrangements generally and a consensus of opinion prevailed that the time had arrived when the Guardians should appoint a resident medical officer, but before suggesting this change it was resolved that a committee consisting of Miss 13roadhent, Messrs. Forshaw, Jolley, and the Master be appointed to visit similar institutions at their discretion where resident medicil men are employed, to obtain the fullest details and information and to lay the same before the com- mittee at their neat meeting prior to definite action being resolved upon. At a further meeting on August 19th the question of appointing a resident medical officer was again considered. The report of the special committee appointed to collect information was read and received. After prolonged discussion it was agreed unanimously "that owing to the growth of ium des, it is no longer practic- able for a doctor in general practice to give sufficient time for the proper treatment of the sick indoor poor, and that the Board be recommended to proceed to the appointment of a resident medical officer for the Workhouse." To give effect to this the follow- ing three resolutions were adopted:— (1) That the Guardians appoint a resident medical officer to be responsible for the medical and surgical treatment of the indoor poor, of the indoor staff, and for the training of the nurses.</p>
        <p>(2) That the Local Government. Board be requested to issue an order, empowering the Guardians to appoint such an officer.</p>
        <p>(3) That the Clerk be instructed to communicate these resolutions to the Local Government Board, together with the statement of the terms and condi- tions upon which Dr. Braide holds the position of Workhouse medical officsr, and ask the Board whether they will approve of those suggestions; on the termination of Dr. Braide's appointment as Workhouse medical officer, and the amount of compensation if any to which be may be entitled. The CLERK announced that he had received a letter on the subject from Dr. Braide. Mr. FORLITA.W said that as this was a rather delicate matter, he thought it would be better to discuss it in camera.</p>
        <p>Mr. KNOWLES : I don't know that there is anything delicate.</p>
        <p>After further discussion, Mr. HICKMAN said he did not think it would be wise for the letter to be read and publicly discussed. He therefore moved that it be dis- cussed in camera. Mr. JOLLEY seconded. Mr. Asticnorr said the question was a public one, and ought to be publicly discussed. On a vote being taken, Mr. Hickman's proposal was defeated and the CLERK read Dr. Braide's letter which was as follows : Bold-street, Warrington, August 20th. Dear sir,—On Saturday Mr. Awbery gave me a copy of the minutes of the House Committee held on the previous day. One part of the minutes read as ' follows : " After prolonged discussion it was atlength unanimously agreed that owing to the growth of in- mates it is no longer pacticable fora doctor in general practice to give sufficient time for the proper treat- ment of the sick poor and that the Board be recom- mended to proceed to the appointment of a resident medical officer for the Workhouse." Now this state- ment is distinctly misleading and calculated, if printed in the Warrington papers, to give a wrong impression to the public ; also because on the same page in the minute book the Chairman writes that the treatment of the indoor patients is satisfactory and that regular attendance is given to them. At the discussion on the same subject on the previous Friday at which Mr. Sutton and I were present it was generally agreed that the proposed change was princi- pally due to the desire of the Comrhittee to train nurses in the Workhouse, and to make the ‘Varrington Union Workhouse a recognised training school for nurses under the Local Government Board, for which a resident medical officer is necessary. Of course, I can quite understand that a resident medical officer is desirable for the training of the nurses, and I think the matter should be put before the Local Government Board and the public on these grounds, and not inferring that the sick poor have in any way been neglected. Trusting you and other members of the Board will seethe reason- ahleness of this request, I remain, yours obediently, GEORGE BRAIDE."</p>
        <p>Mr. KNOWLES (to the Chairman) : Perhaps you can tell the Board what length of time it has taken Dr. Braide on an average during the last month to attend to all the infirmities of the sick here.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN : Probably the Chairman of the House Committee will answer that.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSHAW moved the adoption of the minutes of the House Committee. He thought Dr. Braide was under a misapprehension on the matter. The minutes were framed most carefully with a view of giving him no annoyance, and he did not think the doctor was v-ell advised in interfering with them. He (Mr. Forshaw) could not for the life of him see where there was any reflection upon Dr. Braide's work there. Rev. Dr. MERCER : In the preamble to the resolution. Mr. FORSHAW, continuing. said Dr. Braide had certainly attended to his duties regularly and he bad always come to the House when he bad been summoned in special cases. In every way be bad tried to do his duty as far as it was morally posssble. When he was appointed in 1894 the number of inmates averaged about 350. Now it was about 550. He (Mr. Forshaw) considered it was impossible for a medical man to attend to a practice and attend to that house and see every patient that should be seen. He was not saying there was any reflection on Dr. Braide. He had done all that was morally possible. Mr. KNoWLEs : He nas done all that mortal man could do? Mr. FORSHAW : Yes. Continuing, he said. it was the nursing question that really open6cl up the suggested appointment of a resident medical officer. They had at the Workhouse twelve probationers, so-called, but he thought they would be better called nurse girls. Mr. Knowles : lam afraid you are insulting them. (Laughter.) That ought to be withdrawn. Mr. FORSHAW : They are better called nurse girls than prolontioners. Mr. AstiCROFT : You engage them as probationers. Mr. FORSHAW : But we don't train them as proba- tioners. They take the places under a misconception. They think they are going to be trained as probationers as they would in any ordinary hospital, but they after- wards find they are not.</p>
        <p>Rev. Dr. MERCER: You are charging your own com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSIIAW explained that the probationers did get experience in surgical cases without which they could not pass their examination. Then again the Local Government Board would not agree to the appointment as superintendent of any nurse who had not been trained under a resident medical officer, and she would be unable to rise in her profession. When these facts became publicly known the Board would not be able to recruit their nurses from as good a class as hitherto. In that event they would have to employ properly trained nurses at a cost of £4OO a year in salaries. Mr. A. J. JOLLY seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. THOMPSON moved an amendment that the whole question be referred back to the House Committee. The matter had been brought forward in two great a hurry and he thought the committee acted wrongly in appointing a deputation to visit other unions without the sanction of the Board (Hear, hear.) If the minute had been brought before the Board he would certainly have objected to it, as he did not think there was any necessity for the deputation. He for one had been in favour of the appointment of a resident medical officer for some time but he thought the matter might with advantage be further discussed by thecommittee. , Mr. KNOWLES seconded. Mr. Forshaw had stated ' that Dr. Braids had done all that mortal man could for the patients. Hs (Mr. Knowles) was CaSU Illy informed—Mr. Forshawhad not answered him on that point—that Dr. Braide was able to attend to the duties in less than an hour per day. If that was so he could quite understand that the preamble was not quite right when it said that the duties were so numerous and required so much time that it was impossible for anyone other than a resident medical officer to attend to them.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. BOLTON supported the amendment. He con- tended that any doctor could go through the hospital in an hour as the nurses gave him all information as to patients requiring attention. Mr. IficKNIAN said he agreed with the principle of electing a resident medical officer but one of the objec- tions to the resolution was the"growth"of the inmates. Mr. KNOWLES: That's another " Burtonwooder." (Laughter). Mr. FORSILAW : I protest against those sort of speeches. Mr. PAYTON : The Board room will be as bad as the Council Chamber soon. __ _</p>
        <p>Mr. FORST" A.W : Let Mr. Knowles try to realize where he is. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN contended that the resolution would be suitable if the clause as to t`e impracticability of giving proper treatment to the patients were deleted. He was in favour of having the matter thoroughly discussed in Committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. MERCER said the Rouse Committee were unanimous in their desire to have a resident medical ' officer, but they were not desirous of having one as an intolerable expense, and without giving him any work to do. It was considered that it might be possible to allocate to the new officer the work at Padgate. and at some future date to get a district for him. In that case</p>
        <p>the ratepayers would receive value for their money, but in the present form of the proposal he did not consider there was sufficient work for a resident medical officer to do.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY expressed surprise at the speech of Dr. Mercer because in committee the rev, gentleman strenuously supported the appointment. He (Mr. Jolley) failed to recognize any ground for such a bold farce as that seemed to be. No attempt had been made to rush the thing or burke discussion. Some of the points raised would have to be settled by theLocalGovern- meat Board. He was also surprised at the amendment moved by Mr. Thompson who wrote to the chairman of the House Committee expressing himself in absolute and full accord withl the proposal. With regard to the deputation he (Mr. Jolley) objected most strongly to it, but on the advice of the Clerk and ultimately with the unanimous consent of the members, it was agreed that the deputation should be sent. I He would be sorry to say anything that would be regarded as a personal reflection on Dr. Braide, but he said most emphatically that the wording of the preamble to the resolution was absolutely true and was accepted as absolutely true by the whole members of the committee present at the meeting. The responsibility for the wording of the minute was accepted by the committee. The number of sick inmates in the House had increased of late years. Not only was the hospital full, but numbers of people who ought to be receiving hospital treatment had had to be removed into the body of the house because there was no room for them in the wards. The committee did not intend any reflection on Dr. Braide. it was distinctly stated that when he was summoned in cases of urgent need— day or night—he came at once. The conditions, how- ever, had been gradually changing for several years. The numbers had been increasing and necessarily the amount of time to be devoted ought to be greater if adequate attention was to be given to the sick poor. Every member of the house committee who had been in the habit of attending regularly agreed that the time had come for a change. It was not the time for recrimina- tions or futile discussions as to the exact apportionment of the blame to Dr. Braide or tne members of the House Committee. If they said any blame was attached to anyone they might say that the committee was casting blame on itself for not arriving at is decision earlier. Mr. Jolley emphasised the necessity of the new appoint- ment for the training of probationers. The salaries they would have to pay for trained nurses, in the event of being unable to obtain suitable probationers, would more than provide for the salary of a resident medical officer.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. MERCEa said it was not very pleasant to be accused of being a turn-coat by a man who might use his clearness of perception a little more. There was no question of turning one's coat when one desired the whole thing the committee had in view—the appointment of a resident medical officer, but his point was that they wanted to give, value to the ratepayers if possible. He protested most forcibly against Nlr. Jolley's accusations. It was nbt the first time Mr.Jolley he had made them and he would be better advised to teep them to himself. After further discussion the amendment was carried</p>
        <p>and Development" ; the philatelic editor contributes a topical article entitled "The Stamps of Norway"; and the natural history editor gives invaluable advice in his "Corner." "Stories in London Stone " is an interesting article telling the history of famous memorials in the great metropolis. The number is illustrated throughout with that care and superiority which has alwitys been a feature of this magazine.</p>
        <p>A FEAT OF THE CAMERA. A fine outdoor flavour pervades the September number of" Fry's Magazine," and the reader who cannot find something to grip his interest in this issue must be very difficult indeed to please. Perhaps the most attrac- tive feature from a pictorial point of view is the series of action-photographs of famous batsmen at the wicket, by G. W. Beldam, the accompanying letterpress being written by C. 13. Fry. In this article we do not find the ordinary cricket pictures rendered monotonous by constant repetition, but new and striking poses showing such eminent batsmen as Victor Trumper, F. S. Jackson, K. S. Ranjitsinhj i. A. C. Maclaren, and others actually engaged in executing their characteristic strokes Nothing quite like these action-pictures have ever. appeared in a magazine before, and they cannot fail to appeal at once to every lover of cricket, and every student of expert photography. .kitiong other articles of special note in the September issue of" Fry's" is a capital character sketch of the Hon. F. S. Jackson, facing a fine original portrait of the English captain; a short article by Miss Bertha Thompson on Why Golf is Good for Girls " ; a topical paper on" The Mountain Guide in the Making," fully illustrated " Sailing : the Real Thing," by an enthusiast, besides generous items for voluateers, cyclists, seaside visitors, and others. No one should miss the September " Fry 's."</p>
        <p>C</p>
