      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS AND REVIEWS.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS AND REVIEWS.</p>
        <p>A MODERN ROBINSON CRUSOE. It might be thought that in these days of telegraphs, railways, and submarine cables, experiences similar to those of the immemorial Robinson Crusce would be impossible, yet the September" Wild World Magazine" contains a story entitled " A Tasmanian Robinson Crusce," setting forth' the extraordinary adventures of an unfortunate sailor, who was cast away during January of this year on the coast of Tasmania. For over three months this poor fellow was marooned in a veritable No Man's Land, and his narrative makes enthralling reading. Incidentally it clears up an ocean mystery which had baffled the experts. Yet another "scoop" for this most enterprising magazine is "Round the World on Foot," the record of a seven years' journey. A daring American made this stupendous walk, starting out in a paper suit, and without a penny in his pockets. His adventures, as may be supposed, were many, and his narrative is a fascinating story of dogged pluck. "Trapped," is a stirring story of smuggling days in Norfolk; and half a dozen other striking tales will be found of equal interest with the foregoing. Among the articles, all profusely illustrated with photographs, we may mention • Some Adventures with Sharks " ; " Across the Great Snow Desert," and "Some East African Experiences." by Lord Hindlip. Altogether the September " Wide- World " is a very fine number, literally packed with absorbing material from every corner of the earth. The September "Captain" concludes the thirteenth volume of this excellent publication "for boys and,old boys," all of whom will be sorry to say good-bye to "John Baywood," whose adventures are ended this month. Other fiction items include : " Tank-Sok end Ook-Jok," a story of two brave Eskimos; "The Baboon that went to School," a humorous and exciting story of the Transvaal; "An International Affair," and tale of Wrykyn School ; "The Sniper," an up-to-date naval yarn ; and" Wanted—A Python." a story of the Indian jungle. The articles, too, are as useful as they are varied. Mr. P. F. Warner, tho athletic editor, writes on "Great Bowlers of To-day "; the cycling editor tells us tke besOtray of "Taking it Easy" on the the</p>
      </div>
