      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ICED WATER.</head>
        <p>ICED WATER.</p>
        <p>The" British Medical Journal" quotes a little verse from a paper read by Dr. H. W. Wiley to the American Medical Association. It is quoted by way of solemn warning, because while America's national ailment, dyspepsia, is ascribed to the almost universal habit of drinking qaantities of iced water, the stream of tendency is beginning to flow in the same direction in this country. The lines are these : Full many a man both young and old Has gone to his sarcophagus, By pouring water icy cold Adown his hot oesophagus. We would not be understood, says the" Daily News," as making ourselves responsible for the stet...meat that people in this country are beginning to drink too much ice-water. We scarcely know where they would get it if they wanted it ; and we hardly see what they would want with it if they could get it. Our climate is not so fiercely tropical as all that, and if we have had some unusually hot days this year, they have come in the midst of so many others of a very different character that we hardly think the ice-water habit has had time to form. Just at present August is as cald as it very well could be, and even that insatiable thing, the country," must be getting almost as much ram as it wants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WHAT IS AN " OILETTE " POSTCARD?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS AN " OILETTE " POSTCARD?</p>
        <p>An " Oilette " Postcard is a miniature fac-simile oil- painting produced by Raphael Tuck and Son, direct from the original. It is therefore always a work of Art, and the postcard par excellence, welcomed and appreciated all the world over. The most picturesque views of practically all the tonns. cities, and noted places of interest throughout the United Kingdom are now incorporated in the Oilette "series of postcards, while an immense range of figure subjects, animals, birds, flowers, as well as humorous designs by the lead- ing exponents in this domain of Art, will be found in the splendid range of" Oi tette " postcards, the latest addition to which is the " Wide-Wide World " series, already famed as the most artistic postcards nf the most picturesque spots in the world. Other famous series among the 35,000 different Tuck's postcards are the aquarette, real photograph, silverette, humorous, write away, photochrome, raphotype, &amp;c. THH QUEST OF THE ARMADA'S TREASURE., The Doke of Argyll cnntributes to the September number of the" Pall Mall Magazine " an extremely interesting account of the efforts that have been made for generations past to recover relics from the Armada, ship in the depths of Toberrnory Bay, which are again in progress. "About twenty-six years ago," says the writer, " I sent down a Crinan Canal diver from a yacSt. He at once brought up a brass stanchion, show- ing I was on the spot. He expressed full confidence in his tackle ; but when again lowered, an ominous noise like a pistol report showed the air tube had given way, and we pulled him up as fast as possible for dear life, and the recupemture qualities of whisky were finally proved by the survival of that fresh- water and over-confident diver. He required a good deal; but he wanted to venture again on the same terms, which wore held to be too heavy a draft on the virtues or the" water of life," and again there was a pause of years. Then the present enter- prisi-.g gentleman, Mr. Burns. who is now engaged, tried his hand with good results. It is a curious thing that, at the very considerable depth at which he is working, his man can see everything on the ground. He can vvalk about, picking up small things without using any arti- ficial light : and last year's artificial light was not so good as is that of the gorgeous summer we are now enjoying. But 1901 showed the sea at that spot paved with bones and fragments and scattered coins. The money seems to have been only what the seamen and troops had in their pockets, and almost all were silver dollars. There was no Italian money, so it is probable that though this ship was furnished by the Italian State, the crew came from Spain. Mr. 3urns had nothing that could reveal what lay below the surface of the sea bottom. This year he has had better appliances. But even with these disadvantages there was much of interest recovered. There was part of a brass gun. There wean perfect bronze breechloading piece, looking as though it had but lately left the foundry arid had been painted green, as guns may be nowadays for the sake of invisibility. And in the breech-piece were the ancient charge ana shot, perhaps put in after the last charge fired at Elizabeth's squadron. Then a bronze mortar came up—a pierced cover for a dish—a glazed wire bottle, a sword, and other small objects. Much the most curious of all of these were the very com- passes—two pairs of them—Pereira must have used ! An accretion of lime or calcarions stuff was round them. and had preserved them, as a delicate fossil is preserved in friable stone. When the stone was chipped off, the well-fashioned little compasses emerged perfect, and ready for to-day's measurements. In each case the points had been carefully curved back at the extreme ends, so that the charts should not be injured by their sharp points. A lateral pressure at the sides of the hinged points caused their 'fingers' to open. Their workmanship was perfect." . _</p>
        <p>My</p>
        <p>CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>As I have been away visiting during the present week, I am afraid that I cannot write a letter this time.</p>
        <p>I have had a large number of new member&amp; during the last few days; you will find the list of names in these columns. The prize for the 150th member is awarded to Harry Nuttall, and to John Higson, being the 200th joining our C. C.</p>
        <p>Goodbye for this week, Your affectionate</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>MICE AND MUSIC.</p>
        <p>mommik</p>
        <p>T. is a flict that mice, in common with almost every oilier species of animals, are attracted by music. 'Mice have been known to regularly coMe out of their holes and run about a school- room whenever boys were singing psalms. An offlcei.cruifined in the Bastile, at Paris, begged to be allowed to play on his flute, to soften lij coanoement by its harmonies. • Shortly afterwa ids, when playing on his instrument, he wtis immh astonished to see a number of mice frisking out of their holes, and many spiders (Ics.euding from their webs, and congregating r ri;l,3! him, while he continued his music. \l'litecier he ceased, they dispersed; when- ever he played again, they reappeared.</p>
        <p>James Hadfield'. 219 Harry Thorn.</p>
        <p>- 220 Richard Parkes. 221 Harry Barlow. 222 Dick Spencer.</p>
        <p>223 Alex Robinson. 224 Horrace Hallam. 225 James Nuttal. 226 James Stott.</p>
        <p>227 John Walmsley. 228 Harry Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>BREAD AND JAM. (Continued.) Marie was not willing to leave the luncheon on the ground and then run, as her sister wished. She had listened to many wonderful stories, and wished that something wonderful might happen through her. Then, she thought, perhaps there might some day be another Saint Marie, and other little children would be told the story of this saint, and of her charities when a child. But it was not all vanity with this peasant child, for Marie's nature was kind and charitable. So, clinging to one another, back they went to feed the hungry. "Did you say you were hungry?" asked Marie, when they had come nearer, but were still at a safe distance from the stranger.</p>
        <p>"He is asleep," whispered Louise, for the man took no notice of the- question</p>
        <p>"Here is something to eat," persisted Marie, thrusting her lunch almost into the man's face. The man suddenly startled the children. With a low cry, he snatched the food, which. he instantly began to devour like a wild animal. The children stood watching the hungry man, and as he stuffed the last morsel of Marie's bread and jam into his mouth, Louise held out her portion: "Now, mine." _</p>
        <p>The man, whose hunger, was somewhat ap- peased, and whose mouth was too full to speak, shook his head. "Now, mine," insisted Louise, looking disap- pointed at the refusal. "No," said the man, as soon as he could, still refusing, for now he was no longer terribll hungry, he was somewhat ashamed of having taken the child's luncheon.</p>
        <p>"Now, mine," insisted Louise, thrusting her offering into the man's hands, and, as one child's luncheon was not much for a hungry tramp, and she would not be denied, he took a large bite through both slices of the bread and jam. It almost brought the tears to the eyes of Louise as she saw them going,—still, Marie had said " Surel" But suppose Marie should be mistaken?</p>
        <p>When recess came, the Sister told her pupils they could get their baskets and eat their luncheon in the school-yard, under the trees. Standing at the school-room door, the teacher watched' over the children. Soon she noticed Marie and Louise sitting at the foot, of olle of the trees, their heads close together, Marie looking very sad, and Louise crying. They had made themselves comfortable on the ground before opening their baskets, confident they should find a good luncheon—and both baskets were empty! To be continued.)</p>
        <p>PUZZLES FOR PLAYTIME. Jumbled Flowers. Mariegmu—Srtes—Aniboeg—Raancnoti— Wsnreulof—Dyais. Charade. You'll find my first a wild, shrill cry; My whole is often called a hue. My last is never loud or high, And yet it is to bellow, too. Do my whole you never could; Be my whole yod never should; Wcar my whole you often would. Easy Riddle. I am a little word composed of five letters. My 1-2-3 make about half of the human race; my 4-2-3 make so small a number that it can be represented by a single letter; nil 3-2-4 make an article very useful to maul persons; my 1-2-4 means encountered; and my 1-2-3 4 5 names a city noted for its fortress and as being the place where printing was invented.</p>
