        <p>On the top of Mulberry Cliff we stood to take 111 the air and the view. Both were -cold and ()leen, and scornful of ibut quality of atmospheric bhuldisl uit that prevails in the Western Coml. ties. Item, in the Eastern, Istid, mem and aloud were not interfused, but sharply differentiated. The line of watery horizon was ruled, the outliee' of sandstone cut, upon n slaley sky. N...dltto f;')1 her effects not by " washing," hut lay rigid dray/no,. Sonic; from us the long low swell of cliff tailed owny in gentle maaliCatiloos, till it wits merged in the salt niers'', lands that exteualed baled.: to the horizon. North werilm, it mired into a moderate Weir, mama the snarl) of which 'one cutild see little far purple longues if coast thrust through the white froth of the slue line. Ail acre of tlaktle serail—the royal waste of nature, em- broidered alit)! vivid poppies mad ragwl rt. anal the nielherry spires of sorrel —eras epie.ad up the elope before us : iii, crowniattr this slope, its feet filed ill the waste. the keens of its old ' lulltresses swAtleal in ivy, sal, slowly ecilepsing Upon itself, the radii of an ataleiciat alatarch. It stood up there in its (loony anal 1111101ille4M, tile most nauereigia monument to human vanity : stood, with its siglatlcse ('Yes 111111 ed 110111 WI to I e Hey hamlet that ley in the valley on its fur- ther eide. For did not ibis score of cot higom !beamed V,.14, even' little filmrais dwellings sprouted from fallen walls anal flints-es long hurled —eland for all that once' WILY Of a tansy seaport tom; : a town of picturesque industries and glowing pas- eiinam,—a plOll il)Wit, too, with its seven churches mid vain endowed motetet pries 1 Xeres a,,,,d fiat ante. samn. Bite by bite, the Nem hail devoured - nay ,wits still devouring —it all. Ages ago the last of its monks and shipwrights had tileked up their skirts mid splashed nwaay out of its do ~,,, ed streets. Ages ago, behind the sue 1111 l ry of living man, desolation lied 8,-Itled up- on the place. Now, as we visited it, it 0/11,,, a lapful of ruins, and the little ami-laroona hamlet at their feet ; and we had climbed up from the latter to take our impression of the fernier, first, to satisfy an antiquarian sentiment ; second, to Itheorb the anise en scene of a legendary. And, it,' to that, the story is to come. Now, as we stood looking, through the still sorrowful air came to WI faint sounds of whisper- inge and Ir:impinge. These were at 01100 it fear anal a warn hag ; for while they betokened no- thing more than little periodic avalanches if mortar that flaked and slid from the perilous walls, there was ill their meereey— bees i too there wee nu breath of wind to displace so /MIA its It leaf—a suggestion of the weAry rustling of ghosts about the dead gaping vaults that hail once been their living home and shelter. Yet, if it was so, they were met, poor spectres, upon miserably effacing their own memorials' inas- much as under . this corroding tramp of time, the ruin, we were told, WtIN threetening swifterr veer by year to fall into indistinguishable rub- bish. i We could foresee now, indeed, the incliner of this end. From the tower, a mere glue wed shell of stone, one whole side, or near it, wa4 col- lapsed, and ran in a spill of wreckage tuom the foot. There was tiolluing else wood in n pretence of shape, but, the wall of one aisle, that, green with ivy, went. in It trip of arches to the cliff hue. Thence, below, from the shorn sandstone one 'night pick out lames like tanagets ; and beyond, over overwhelmed streets and yards and MOlllll terima, rorel in the devouring sea. ' You have visited the eovpse, gentlemen of the jury," said the Traveller ; " And so now fur the evidence 118 10 What Miltio It one." fie alluded fautetiously to the ruin oar tho cliff. It wan ma his recottintendittion ibuit we had gone to vieit the locale of the legend lie wits 11hOilt to relate to US. We had lighted on him in the Pear 7.1,8 : a strange, gnarled body of It man, with bright brown jellies of eyes, as unfathom- able as it. ritbhit'eu. He -was strung with hardi- hood and shrewd intelligence ; sot f-cducated, ob. , viounly, end never the sort to miss his way in the dark. Dianberry had borne him ; tuttl no w, to ease off steam in a rare interval between work mud work, he was revisiting his native place, thet he hail not set foot in for thirty years. " I've been to see it once since I came," he said (referring to the ruin), " and I don't go • otteite It neele me melancholy. It wasn't that l'ime's been busy there with blasting-powder, sine() I saw it lust; it was the sight of paper begs and broken but this that did me. I'm not tt reinutionist, gentlemen, that's a fact ; but I will "my that if anyone's entitled to the name, Wm It ghost ; ilPil how is Ityliost to stand ngainst those Picnic parties I' Not Iltuntlet's father could have done it : lunch less Caleb Darkin, who was ordy 'ilk ignorant, evil-minded sexton, and, didn't know Ids Shakespeare at all. " Ile lived—and died —did Caleb in the days . Lerore the light ; before the police—by the name of Peelers—swept out the houses of darkness from ktie to kitchen, and asked nothing of the cook in exchange but a mutton-bone. My grandfather knew Caleb ; and you can take the story—lit ale- fandt of official records, whieli the sea hes got with the rest—from him through dad, and from ded through me. "At that time there were still some retnnanta of ties old order about Mulberry. The ocean We." taking its toll, true enough ; but the church—the "Illy one of all that remained—kept a margin of eonte two or three hundred yards between itself end destruction. It wasn't to last long ; and in the meantime the folks—more, by scores than they are now—climbed up to worship there to the tame of as handsome it peal of bells as ever set it line tower rocking. And the ringers were a eon- meientione body of men : and, of them all, Jake Vlerrydew was at once the youngest and the most vigorous. " Iddy had measured I • , with her eye, at twice her own height, which was overdoing it. but there's a female passion that inagnifies ai ! 111 l les inches, and that passion isn't hate. Any. how she was the smallest wench in the village—ti ,thlog with a waist like a watch-spring, anal her "iim as sweet as a kissing•comfit ; and, when she °tan rising eighteen. there wasn't a man of Man- trY worth his salt but what was set to putt off Metriniony till all chance of Iddy was lost. " She was Cromwell, the blacktenith's daugh- ter ; and, from the time she was it motherless !itite, mhe'd sit and sing like it bird to the music of leer father's anvil, while the hot iron, glowing through her eyes, made her strong to bear the women's part. Tilsit was the story ; and I'll ""t ioly but that there may be more ways then "lie of taking in a tonic, though Blaud is in fav- our with most. But, however that may lie, Idaly 7114 tough metal, and no mistake, for all that in 'oaks there was never a fairy in the starlight to euille near her for frailness and delicacy. „ ' Well, gentlemen, you'll understand it was ',..;", twenty-third of November, whit+ is St. `-,,',letnent'e, the blacksmiths' feast day. The man, t.;rotnwell—a cheery soul, and himself a ringer stout one—was just over at the itiack Bey 1/ , treating Ja rind ake friend or two, to a glass of --rt'-in ; aud Iddy, during his absence, was sit- ; watch on the anvil—where ten minutes he- , they'd been firing salvoes of gunpowder to -, "o Saint—when in comes Caleb Darkin, slink- log end mweating, like a man set on a desperate 4 Verature. i t "'lily stopped hitmining, and sat swinging her °ea- She was a little frightened, because the TH‘t had en uncomfortable reputatioll-110111ing eefinite, you know, but. the sort of shadow one ,haet peer; by without turning one's head to rtnike 1`":”e it ain't following. He was It wry, powerful 1 "yr, with arms like steel, from . their play, 'Own and up and over, with a loaded spade ; niel •_;._ wits it smuggler (lint that all were, though of ' lferent complexioits ; end Caleb's was !middy) ; 2r.,10+40 man ; lancl—vvorst of ell —ii close Admirer. ILtl,tvir knew thet —what girl wouldn't F But it g • I.er no gratifientioai. She looked lot him, will. her little nose tipped. up, in spite of n load. 41 ell feeling that her clays were gene too big for „, ' Are ye enunn In be 41.1., CJl'eb: She says, ""ll ye wa!lc .0 haltinn ? Bide a bit, till fettle:</p>
        <p>' Fore cloven hoof,' snym He looked up at her and lowered his eyes in flash. ' Maybe I've somethiug of the devil in me, he said. ' You could cure it ifWQUId you " She laughed out and thanked him. Pin not to be used for beast-leech at my time of life,' she said. "It was snowing, and a dusk yellow forenoon. Moreover the smith', was at the tail end of the village. Caleb, with a spring like a tiger's, had the girl in his clutch, and, with his hand on her !nolith, had borne her to the dark end of the shop before she could kick. Hark to me,' he said, deep and hurried. ' Where folks won't listen, folks must be com- 4)elied- More thau that, where compelled fella</p>
