      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>toTi tale*, the con4onitptwee will be like to 101 OIL other heads thau their own. Do you under- *tend</p>
        <p>_ "It wan plain she did. He took his hand from ner month. _ _</p>
        <p>" • I'll not tell tales ; I'll not cry out, Cilob,' sbe said, swallowing at her fear, poor thing. ' Don't burl me. What is it you went ?' " ' Want !' the man gasped. Yon to be sure You won't tell, won't you ? You're afraid for somebody' other than yourself, eh ? Well, you've need to be. I'll .BWing in a halter for you; I'm lying for you : this ain't my world without you. You needn't lie ; you've •guessed that already. Better take a devil than a psalm-singer, I tell you : they love hottest in hell ; and besides you could win me pardon. Look here, I could crush von ; I could ruin you—there's not a soul nigh ; hut I won't. I won't do that, because my love is stronger than my 'desire. But this I tell you : If you defy me, through your own desires of another, you'll be a blood-guilty woman.' " He lowered her gently to the ground, and was gone in a moment. She sat huddled there, trying to think it was all a bad dream. She knew more than she would confess, even to herself, of this man's passion. She had seen it smoulder ; had seen the heart of it break into deep red fire in the breath of some recent gosairinvg. She shuddered, and moaned, and hugged herself. . . .</p>
        <p>" Iddy !'cried a jolly voice. "It was Jake's. He had run over from Ilic Black Boy—from bread and cheese to kisso., bo- fore he returned to his work, which was carpen- tering. " • Iddy !' he cried ' Why, what the thun- der, girl! " She had come up to him. Her face was like a alone.</p>
        <p>..... " ' Hush' she said. l You shouldn't. You have no right.' " No right !' he cried amazed. " It was to be a secret,' she said. • You bare broken it; and so we must go back to the time before you asked and I oonsented.' " • Broken it r he said. How, in God's name</p>
        <p>" coming and laiming me like this, will all the village to guess the reason.' "He stood back from her, bewildered.</p>
        <p>" Didn't I do the like before—often and often 2 Iddy, you haven't left off loving me F' " She struggled with herself, poor weneh, knot tin,, her bands and. striving to outface him. Then she broke down.</p>
        <p>I can't, I can't she cried ; ' aud, Jake, 1. want to.'</p>
        <p>"lie made it lens possible than ever, holding her to him with his lips and arms, like a blessed ciittlefish, till she was near stifled. Tie was 11 nature that mastered by confidence in himself ; snd he had no scruples but that she had been on. iine.titte• with him. She wits drained of till will when he left her, going whistling to his work. Mid then her time began. . . . .</p>
        <p>" For there was a little sooty window in the end wall of the smithy ; and Caleb, hidden under a lean-to, had stood glaring through it mid marking everything. " The man didn't let himself go at once I. was too strong for that. die went straight to Cromwell, and, making nothing of rep ads and rumours, asked slap out for Miss May's hand. The fn titer, of course, threw his character in his teeth, and, for all CAW) could prove himself t, warmer Titan than Jake, told hurt he preferred honesty to riehes, and showed hint the doer. " Caleb went, and went his way, and kept it. lie was never seen after that, but in the church- yard: •• avoiding her helped May to a little re- spite from her palpitations. She feared this mall more than she could say ; or could dare te say, for the matter of that. She lived in terror of where his vengeance would fall, if she let out. Even when, to finish off other claimants, her Either insisted upon making public her betrothal to Jake, she wouldn't venture to tell parent or lover of his threat to her. But the secret fret- ted her like moth, burrowing into the dark places of her mind.</p>
        <p>" Now, however, as the days passed, and he kept close and quiet, she grew to hope th.d; I. had meant TIO harm, by his talk of 1)100(1.. guilt. to anyone but himself. She was a good girl ;liii love itud desperation warped her so far. Perhaps it was for this little sin of selfishness she was made to suffer what she did. " It Was the fifth dny following St. Clement'. •—the morning of the First Sunday in Advent. Tddy's heart felt a bit less loadeds,thatt of late Perhaps it couldn't conceive of the devil heinstlet have his way on the Sabbath. The Sabbath was tiod's own Court day, as it were ; and i$ Was more likely that the wicked would think to defy the divine law in its own presence,# than thee would the earthly. So, when soon after bteak- fast, she found herself wanting her psalter, which she needed for a tough bit of practice before set.- VIOP, she made no bones about it, but trippof SI cay to the old. church up on the hill, in the choir of which she made certain of findung what she sought. To be sure this was, in a manner of *peaking. to fly in the face of Providence. as there was no place more likely to meet the sexton in than up there. Tint the morning looked WI inno- cent as sun on quiet snow could make it ; nobadv was handy to keep her company—not even Jake. or her father, who would be sernbbing and pol 'siting themselves to ring in the Lord's coming, as usual.; and, perhaps, when all's said, the little dare-devil in her was beginning to chafe nadir its own self-repression—at least by daylight. Bi. she pulled her hood, over her pink ears and started. "It was very windless and peaceful. Not H sound broke the stillness but that of her own footsteps, that crunched in the snow as regular as the working of a cow's jaws. She reached Elm church, and hurried in. The tall arches yawned with cold and emptiness at her. Butt she didn't mind that : it Was Bound, not silence, that site reared.</p>
        <p>" She snatched her pghlter, and wag runninir ont again, when she noticed that the little belfry door under the tower wan open. " Now what moved her, gentlemen, who but God shall say ? He had his scheme of retribu• tion and, maybe, to play on this girl's fondness, er cariosity, or anythingelme that makes the per- versity of her sex, Was pert of it. Cm not sorp, mind you, thnt there isn't something delightful' to a bird in the faseinatim. of the snake that wants to bolt it, and woman, after a like holden, TilaV he drawn to hor_ondoor._ _ -</p>
        <p>" In any case, Illy suddenly turned out of her way, and went into th,. tower. She climbed up and up, breathing it little hard, perhaps, until she reached the ringers' chamber. Here she stole about on tip. Foe, with it little cerise of naughtiness, examining the charts and the looped up ropes, and wondering which was Jake's. Whichever it W/114, it stood for the heavie‘t, the treble belt situ he oms proud Of lds strength. While she was peering about, a little aflutter of white powder, lying under a rope that wits near- it Ow wall, caught liar eye. She looked up. The same powder was sprinkled on (lie rope it- self where it came through its hole in the ceiling. She stooped, and took it pinch of the stuff in her hand. Fresh sawdust : there Was no question of it ! But bow could such filter through from the old beams above ?</p>
        <p>" Now, did He, who had directed her foot- step., enlighteu her ? Tn aftet days she'd never venture an opinion. Only she could say that mhe was curious, curious, curious, and that her heart beat as if it would burnt and fly.. .</p>
        <p>" She went swiftly from the chamber, not down but up again : np, from dank oppression into a senee of chill airiness. Awl then, of a sudden, a huller of green light grew from the wall high before her, and the rays that were its rune,--f i!" qnliv,lu won., inff -110,1raq. • say—teu upon a web or ;huddled beams, and wheels' and domes, dusty and brazen, that were caught amongst them. " She had never been in the belfry before; and now, fearful of taking a false step and of slip- ping into some unknown pit, or, perhaps, sheer through the ceiling into the room underneath. she stood panting. Not for long, though. Close by where she'd come in, one of the mighty joists mitered the tower wall ; mud, all hi a moment, glancing that way, she gave a cry, and was down trembling on her knees. " Illy God, gentlemen ! Would you believe it ? The beam had been sawed three.- quarters through, as had every other beam of the cage in which the bells hung. At the first peal, what with the fall and the rocking, the whole mass of wood and metal would tear away, and go thundering down the tower, crushing to pulp all it encountered by the road " The devil that designed it was a madman of course. His revenge, aimed at Jake and Crom- well, was out of all proportion with the disap- pointment he'd suffered. But worse was to mine.</p>
        <p>' lady, when she realised the truth, turned to run—to step away on tiptoe rather. She wouldn't sob, she'd hardly breathe, for fear it might be the bust straw. ' Jake she whispered ; 0, Jake !' asshn fluttered towards the door and then, on the in• stuout, she was down, and Caleb's hand at her mouth.</p>
        <p>"He'd been hiding in the dark all the while. laughing end gloating at her. - - -</p>
        <p>"The creature's strength WRA fesrful. Lying ovr.• ter. he waggea her with his handkerchief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Examiner,</head>
        <p>The Examiner,</p>
        <p>Then, atretohing for a eon of rope that my hs trussed her, lege and arms, like it tionom.% He never breathed a word the while : and it's a wonder who didn't die under his hands. But. Ow was tough mend, as 1 told you, and .he'd not give out of her own will, so long as there was a •bane° to save joke and her father. " When he was done the man rose and clod over her.</p>
        <p>" 6 Now, he sail, low and hoarse, ''you've got to learn what it meant to fly in my face. I warned ye, didn't I, woman P I hadn't designe.l this here, I confess it ; hut the Lord prov. k.- me • with His favours. Do you know what. going to do with ye P Lash ye to Jeke.,, Is‘r, that's all, so as he may brain ye will. I. own bell, before he rings that down on his silly head. Come, you mad woman, and add your voice to the merry peal.' " The treble bell, by foul luck, hung highest of all, clean away from the platform. It was it big bell, two or three tons in weight. Caleb took and tied the girl to the clapper, swinging her under tho arms in such way that her head would just crack nicely on the rim. Then he stied nit, and regarded. his work. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>' I must be going.' he says. The ringers will ho down there soon, and I don't want to get involved in this mess. lint I shall be wait- ing for ye, below—' says he, and, with the words, left her. "To what P On my soul, gentlemen, I've scarce got the stomach—for all the tittles Fyn - heard and related the tale—to picture it. As a matter of "choice, I'd like to put a line of stars hers, like the newspapers, to signify what I can't explain. Think of her, that sweet frail body, strung up in the ghastly loft of echoes, and shadows, and wicked unfamiliar shapes ; and nil the time the most awful of deaths waiting Ler end her loved ones in the reeling pit under- neath ! And she.dumb and helpless, too ! " She must,have gone raving —must have, lint for one thing. From the moneeit Caleb left her, she set to working her jaws, like a mad woman, to try and loosen the gag. It wasn't Innen ; but it was just the little active business that kept her tension of horror front tightening more than she could endure. So she. spoke of it afterwards. " All of is sudden she heard the men in the room below : could distinguish the very voices of Jake and her father. They were merry and laughing. They had no thought - but that she was with her friends, and on the road to take her place in the choir. She- had not been missed, then. Her last hope was slipping from her. " Now there struck, on the instant, into her hearing a sharp creak and flutter. One of the misty domes moved, slowly turned itself horizon- tal, poised, with its tongue lolling, and its black ‘hroat open to vomit. thunder: then another, end another. They were ringing up the bells down selow. It was terrible to see, one by one, the silent monsters rear, at the instigation of no visi- ble human agent. In another moment, she too, would feel herself lifted and swung at poise, and then-0, God of Mercy r the sickening swoop, end her last scream passing unheeded in the shattering peal that was to make the tower for evermore an accursed and haunted ruin.</p>
        <p>" Withi dying effort. she sought to cry ont --drew in her breath—and the bandage 'dipped from her mouth. •</p>
        <p>" It was a way with .Take to hold on to hia rope, with his head leaned to his hands, before he took a sudden breath and heave, and tightened himself to ring-up. Now, standing like this, lie was just duwing-in for the tug, when, clear and sharp as agony, a cry came down the rope straight into his ear : ' Jake, Run ! , The bells are falling !' " My God he faltered out, and dropped the rope and l.ant for the door. ' Run, father ! Run all !' he serepelied—' here, for your lives r They had heard the strange cry, but not to understand its import. Those who had rung-up let go all and bolted. The released ropes whipped up to the ceiling; the slash of them was answered by a discordant clang, and there fol- lowed. on the thought, a crash 90 sudden, so deafening, so enormous, that the senses of the group, standing crushed together just outside the door of the ringers' chamber, seemed to scatter into chaos. They might have seen, smelt, tasted, once upon a time. All these faculties were merged now in the one of dreadful hearing. "A. cry from Jake pierced the chaos. God of Mercy ! It was Iddy screeched out, and from overhead there ! What's come to us, and to her !`</p>
        <p>" The dust cleared from his blinded eyes, as he spoke. He Wal4 standing nighest the door ; and, standing, lie gave a heart-killing Yell. Be. fore him was it thumping tangle of wreckage : beams, bolls, lath, plaster and ropes. The mass laid jammed in its fall, because (and Caleb hadn't foreseen that) the walls of the tower thickened and shut closer towards the base. But there wit something else, and that something made mani- fest the designs of Providence. For—you'll find it hard to believo me, gentlemen,—the girl her- self, in a dead swoon' but unhurt, hung within reach of rescue by her lover.</p>
        <p>" There : the marvel's out. How it was, I can't say ; hut a beam, I tell you, bad fallen in such a manner as to protect her ; and within a minute she was in Jake's arms. And thou rotrttightway—RB if the Lord had but halted His doom to secure its instrument from hurt—no sooner was the girl safe, but the load of F tuff broke through the floor, and spread-eagled CAW) who was just hurrying underneath to the ii tie door in the tower, to run up and 800 what had stayei him vengeance half-way." The traveller paused, and pulled at his pipe. " Rummy story, isn't it, gentlemen F" said be ; " but true, rn answer for it. It was the end of the church anyway. The bells had rung doan the curtain on raid' a tragedy as toads the folkn shy- of worshipping there ; and it wasn't very long before the sea cattle with an order to wils; out what was left. You may see Caleb's mink still, they tell me, when you oan catch it broad- ways on ; for he was flattened to a leaf by what fell on him, stud can't PO much as ChNt a shadow right or left."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ALIAGED INGENIOUS FRAUD.</head>
        <p>ALIAGED INGENIOUS FRAUD.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, at Runcorn, Margaret Worsley (63), of no settled abode, was charged with obtaining ss. from Miss Hankinson, lady visitor to Runcorn Parish Church. The evidence showed that on the 7th inst. the prisoner called upon Miss Hankinson, and said she had been sent by the Sister of Chester Rescue HOMO to find the parents of Mary Ann Mea, who haa run away from the home, and been locked up at Wrexham and fined 10s. The Sister wished Miss Hankinsen to send the amount of the fine. At first Miss Ilankinson refused, but eventually gave her ss. on the prisoner stating she would pay the other ss. It was afterwards found that the prisoner's story was quite untrue, and a warrant was issued for her apprehension, Police-constable Holland arresting her at Stockport on Monday. The prisoner, who had a" record," was committed for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>A great comfort these days to be able to do the family wash without steamy suds, without undue exertion, with nicer, sweeter clothes.</p>
        <p>. All who use it rightly know that Fels-Naptha soap takes only about half usual time, with about half usual labour to do the work.</p>
        <p>And Fels-Naptha takes away the most disagreeable part of the work.</p>
        <p>When using Fels-Naptha divest yourself of all old ideas and try the modern, the easy way of washing clothes • and cleaning house. At once it will be seen what great value Fels- Naptha is in domestic economy. 21c1 a bar.</p>
        <p>0016-Naptha 39 Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
