      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MOTOR SIREN.</head>
        <p>THE MOTOR SIREN.</p>
        <p>A new terror has been added to life. It is a motor siren whose voice is so raucous and terrible that it is said it can almost put the foghorn of a Transatlantic liner to shame.</p>
        <p>It is stated that its brazen throat will warn pedestrians of the approach of a motorcar many hundreds of yards away, and as mJny of these fearsome instruments have, so it is said, been sold to owners of fast motor-cars, the prospect for the peace-loving public is anything but agreeable. It simply means that to Miss Marie Coreli's estimate of men,' rushing about on motor-cars,smelling horribly and looking like the worst devil's in Dante's Inferno," the voice of the Inferno itself is now added. The siren gathers the power for its voice from the fly-wheel of the car, with which it is connected by pressing a lever. Its note is more or less awful accord- ing to the speed at which the car is travelling. The noise starts with an ominous ascending whirr. Up, up, up, it swells, in an agonising, ear-splitting crescendo, till it reaches its topmost note—a shriek of 'demoniac fury. Then it comes down throuph a gamut 11 of the most prolonged and hideous wails imaginable, and dies away in an angry burr-r-r-r. I It is a new terror to the highways, which bids fair to I eclipse every other grievance for which the motor-ear and the motorist are held responsible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ST. HELENS CHRISTENING,</head>
        <p>A ST. HELENS CHRISTENING,</p>
        <p>'Nicholas Keighley, daywageman, of 287, Derbyshire Hill-road, who was represented by Mr. P. C. Kelly, denied, when charged on remand at St. Helens on Monday, that he wounded his lodger, a man named Murphy, with an intention of doing him any harm. Chief-Inspector Goodall called Dennis Murphy, who said that on Sunday, the 13th inst.. there was a christen- ing at Keigkley's house, with a good deal of drinking during the day. In the early hours of Monday morning the two men made a wager as to the measurement of their chests, and Murphy won. When the guests had departed Keighley suddenly took up the poker and said, `• Look here, big nose, you are in your last set." He then struck him a severe blow on the head, which felled him. In consequence he had been in the Providence Hospital with the injury. Mtrphy said that be worked at -the same colliery as Keighley, and had lodged with him for about five months. The magistrates said they did not think there was any intent to do bodily harm, and let him off easily by ordering him to pay, after being bound over, the costs, amounting to 17s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. W. Dixon, of Bowmere Lodge, Tarporley met with an acciden t on Monday afternoon while cycling in High-street, Tarporley. A motor-car and a mail van were passing, and to avoid being caught between them Mrs. Dixon turned to the side of the road, and the pedal of her bicycle caught the kerbstone. She fell, and her left arm was broken. She was driven home in the motor -car.</p>
      </div>
