      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE'S ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE'S ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Elective Auditors Refuse to Sign.</p>
        <p>PAPERS WITHHELD.</p>
        <p>The two elective auditors for the Borough, Messrs. Edwin Bradshaw and Lawrence Bolton, have refused to sign the accounts of the Water Department and in the published Corporation abstract of accounts for the year completed 31st March last their signatures are conspicuously omitted. The accounts of all other committees of the Corporation are signed by Ur. John Fairhurst, the Borough Treasurer and the two elective auditors, but those of the Water Committee are signed only by Mr. Fairhurst. We understand that the two elective auditors refused to sign the Water Committee's accounts because certain documents relating to Mr. Lawson's contract were not produced for inspection on request.. In other respects the accounts are correct;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CHARGES AGAINST TWO COUNCILLORS</head>
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST TWO COUNCILLORS</p>
        <p>Investigation Committee takes Counsel's Opinion.</p>
        <p>A PROSECUTION RECOMMENDED.</p>
        <p>At the May Council meeting, Alderman Pierpoint, Alderman Smethurst and Councillors Ashcroft, Clegg, Craik, Lord and Stott, were appointed a Committee "to investigate the charges made against a member in accordance with the resolution passed at the last meeting of the Council, and to investigate a charge made against a member of the Council in relation to the Water Department, and any other matter effecting the honour or integrity of the members of the Council."</p>
        <p>The Committee has had numerous sittings since that date and heard a large amount of evidence con- cerning the charges made against the tvr'o Councillors. This evidence has been since printed and will be submitted to the General Parpobes Committee on Monday. _ .</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the Inquiry, the Committee decided to take Counsel's opinion on the two cases of alleged corrupt practice, and the evidence was laid before Mr. Eldon Bankes, K.C., who has given the 'opinion that both cases warrant an application to the Attorney General for the right to institute proceed- ings under the Public Bodies Corrupt Practices Act, 1889.</p>
        <p>Each member of the Investigation Committee has since signed the report of the inquiry which con- cludes by recommending the Town Council to take proceedings as advised by Counsel.</p>
        <p>The matter will, as indicated above, come before the General Purposes Committee on Monday, and the prosecution wilt receive the confirmation of the Council, or otherwise, at the monthly meeting on September sth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in nit cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Cornwall : Britain's Reviera, and Getting There.</p>
        <p>_ Sir,—Such is the heading to-day on the Great Western excursion bills to be seen at all the principal stations in the North where the Great Western Railway have running powers. As a Cornishman I was a pleased to see that some effort was at last being made to do justice to the land of my nativity and to place Cornwall in the foreground as a resort of pleasure and health. I have travelled much in England, Scotland and Wales, have visited the resorts to which the public flock in their thousands, but for real rustic beauty, and for sure last- ing enjoyment which produces health and vigour, com- mend me to Cornwall. especially the West. The bills referred to announce excursions for land 14 days,leaving Manchester at 7.15 p.m. on Friday, returning from Penzance on the following Thursday at 6.30 p,m. By , what stretch of imagination this can be construed into 7 days I fail to imagine. Previous to booking at War- i rington I went to the Great Western office and askedl for the probable time of our arrival at Penzance, the booking clerk did not know, had no instructions on the matter:and only knew time of departure. I then went to ,1 the London and North-Western office, where they were I running an excursion the same night at 10.25, the result was the same. Then I paid a visit to the Great Central' office,they too were:running an excursion at 6.25 p.m.but ' were equally ignorant as to the time the train was due I at Penzance, so that it was impossible to advise your friends as to what time you were likely to reach your destination. Having travelled by the Great Westernl excursion last Witsun twelve months and having had to change flve times between Warrington and Penzance and that in crowded trains and then on the I setnrn journey, being compelled to stay at Bristol all I night, the last train for the North having been dis- patched more than half an hour before our arrival there. I therefore decided to travel by the London and North- Western via Crewe and Severn tunnel. I left Warring- ton at 10.30 p.m. Friday ;the coach in which I travelled belonging to the Great Western Company, arrived at Penzance at 130 the following afternoon (Saturday), the journey occupying exactly 15 hours. This without any convenience was bad enough for men,but for women and children to undertake such a journey at night with- out a corridor is an insult to civilization, demands the I serious attention of the travelling public,and is a matterl that might well receive attention from our legislators at St. Stephen's To me it seems inconsiderate and cruel, and t doubt whether such a thing would be tolera- ted only where there is a monopoly such as the Great Western command in this case. The journey from Plymouth to Penzance is a caution, and was calculated to give one the impression that we were nearing the Land's End in more than one sense of the word. Four hours for about 70 miles is certainly not fast travelling. If the Great Western Railway Company are really serious in advertising Cornwall mid wish to make it popular something must be done to meet the convenience of the travelling public. A mornings excursion was run on the same day to Exeter and other places leaving Manchester at 9.40 a.m. but to the "Riviera "a night excursion when the refreshment rooms are closed the beauty of the country hid from view, and when few would travel if they had the choice of a day train must suffice. The complaints which I heard were many and great and I fear that the time taken up by the journeyand the inconsiderate treatment meted out to them will prevent rainy from repeating their visit on recommending it to their friends at home That something could be done, ought to be done and must be done beyond mere .advertising if Cornwall is to have justice dealt to it is the view not only of the writer but of many others with whom I have come into contact, and the sooner that something is done the better will it be for Cornwall and for all c.mcerned.- 17 ours obediently, STEPHEN H. JENKINS, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ALLEGED MALICIOUS PROSECUTION. Unsuccessful Action at Warrington.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED MALICIOUS PROSECUTION. Unsuccessful Action at Warrington.</p>
        <p>- - Beiore his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, at the Warrington County Omit on Thursday, Ellen Stevens, a widow, hying in Haydeck, brought an action to recover £l5 damages for alleged malicious prosecu- tion from John Picton, farmer, New Boston, Havdock. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. J. V. Knowles, Widnes, defended. Plaintiff's case was that on June 23th, she was sum: monad by the defendant at Newton Petty Sessions for atealina coal. Defendant only called his son as his witness, but plaintiff had several witnesses proving that she was in bed at the time it was alleged she was at the coaltip; and the Bench dismissed the case. Prior to the hearing of the case plaintiff's son saw the defendant and told him that his mother was in bed at the time of the alleged theft and warned him that if he proceeded with the prosecution the consequences might be serious. Defendant, however, took the case into court. Four months ago the plaintiff was seen by the defendant with a bag and he warned her not to take any coal, although she had no intention of doing so. Mr. Browne con- tended that these facts constituted malice. Plaintiff's son gave evidence and then defendant was called and said he had no malice or ill-feeling against the plaintiff. They had always been good friends and at the time of the summons he believed the evidence he had got. He was sorry if he had made a mistake. His Honour said there was no malice and he wished it to be understood that there was no suggestion whatever that the magistrates' decision was wrong, He gave Pidgment for the defendant without costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>"THE HAVEN UNDER THEi . HILL." I</head>
        <p>"THE HAVEN UNDER THEi . HILL." I</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY IMPRESSIONS OF WHITBY.</p>
        <p>Cycling by Moor and Coast.</p>
        <p>When one's holiday experiences have been of the happiest there is always an additional pleasure in recounting them in the hope that they may prove of interest to others. And how happy are the remem- brances which continually well up of days spent "between the heather and the northern sea" at dear old Whitby—as the late Mr. Gladstone used to call it. For two years in succession it has been my privilege to seek change of scene in Whitby and its romantic surroundings. A complete change, truly, to be transported from this ' smoky centre of industry to the rocky Yorkshire coast with its inland setting of wild moorland mad richly- wooded vales. I have often marvelled that 30 few Warringtonians find their way to Whitby,and for that reason this short sketch may not be out of place. It was under the simple title of "The haven under the hill" that the late Mary Linskill, Whitby's gifted authoress, in one of her most beautifully touching works, pictured the quaint old town of which she was so devoted a daughter. And it is as "The haven under the hill" that Whitby still possesses the greatest charm. Whitby, the growingly-popular resort, without doubt awakens fragrant recollections, but Whitby" The haven under the hill " conjures up the truest and most enduring picture of the little port which looks out on the Northern Sea. For old Whitby—that part of the town where modernity has not intruded—is indeed well described in the one brief phrase. Its red-roofed houses are clustered on the side of a protecting cliff, while just below are the placid waters of the harbour —a welcome haven to many a storm-tossed vessel. But this is anticipating somewhat. Although War- ringtonians have a way of distributing themselves to the far ends of the• earth at holiday times, Whitby remains " undiscovered " by the majority of them. Of course, there are holidays aid holidays, and those to whom the ideal Change is one long round of excitement and a multitude of artificial attractions had better not make Whitby their centre— unless they are open to conversion to a quieter and, shall I say, more healthful form of ' recreation.To the lover of nature in some of its wildest ' aspects, however, Whitby and the moors on which it borders offer a splendid prospect. No doubt Warring- ton people shrink from undertaking the long and tedious journey across the county of broad actes to the North Eastern coast. True there is nothing strikingly picturesque about the greater part of the route which lies through a chain of cotton and woollen manufacturing towns, and I found it a welcome break in the monotony of railway travelling to leave the train at Leeds and visit the far-famed Kirkstall Abbey, so pleasantly situated on the outskirts of the city as to be a very -favoured haunt. On wandering through the well-preserved ruins of what must once have been a magnificent pile, one could not help feeling how deep a debt Leeds owes to the public-spirited citizen whose generosity secured the abbey to this progressive community for all time. The last twenty miles of the journey to Whitby embrace grand moorland scenery and the pure air has an exhilarating effect on the jaded traveller. As the train winds in and out among the heather-clad hills, ever-changing beauties are i unfolded and when at last Whitby s reached the visitor is feeling in a bright frame of mind. But how much more delighted he is when taking in a full view of the town!</p>
        <p>It is here that the river Esk after a winding course through sylvan scenery enters the sea. What Whitby would be without the Esk one dare not even imagine. It divides the town into two parts—the old and the new—but the old is the real Whitby, forming as it does a connecting link with the past and carrying back the mind to the days when Whitby was little more than a fishing village. The old town nestles on the side of the great dark east cliff which rises steeply from the turbulent sea, while a narrow swing bridge, : spanning the harbour, is the only means of reaching the modern side of Whitby which lies on the opposite cliff. The streets are quaint and narrow, and some of the fishermen's houses look perilously near the edge of the cliff. There are little alleys through which one gets glimpses of the harbour, and the whole place breathes,as it were,an air of antiquity and tranquillity quite refreshing in these days of stress and strain. Crowning the cliff is the Abbey—the great glory of Whitby—while hard be is the ancient Parish Church. To attend their Parish Church the good people of Whitby have to climb 199 steps carved out in stone—an exer- tion which should surely bring its blessing! Romantic indeed is the situation of the •Abbey at the highest point of the cliff, exposed to all the winds that blow. It is a veritable paradise for the artist and photo- grapher, and many choice scenes amid the ruins are reproduced by pencil and camera. Tim grace- ful ruins now existing are those of the mag- nificent church erected soon after the Conquest, but the Monastery of Streonshalh, from which arose Whitby Abbey, was founded in the year 658 by Oswy, King of Northumbria, in fulfil- ment of a vow made before a great battle. Warrington has a mission Church dedicated to St. Hilda, the first abbess of Whitby, who was held in great veneration and of whom many remarkable legends were told. So peaceful are the suiroundings of the Abbey that the visitor feels quite cut off from the busy world, and the imagination goes back to those far-off days when Lady Hilda presided over the little monastic com- munity, and the bells of the Al.bey continually pealed forth from the tower which formerly crowned the ruins.</p>
        <p>Whitby is indeed rich in history and traditions. At the top of the church steps, and not far from the abbey, a tall Anglican Cross,beautiftilly-figured,stands out conspicuous. It forms a fitting memorial to Credmon, the fath&lt;tr of English sacred song, who, in tact, wrote the first 'true English poem. The Abbey was the scene of his labours. It was here that he was employed as a humble servant before the gift of verse 'came to him in a remarkable way, and 1 he wrote the sacred poems, telling of the Creation, which were the beginnings of English poetry. The Englishman instinctively feels a reverence for a spot possessing such associations, and 1 cannot do better than quote the words of Stopford A. Brooke who in his well-known work on English literature says : " Above the small and land-locked harbour of Whitby, rises and juts out towards the sea the dark cliff where Mid's mmastery stood, look- ing out over the German ocean. It is a wild, wind- swept upland, above the furious sea ; and standing there we feel that it is a fitting birth-place for th; poetry of the sea-ruling nation." Whitby people are proud of their Parish Church which boasts consider- ably antiquity. It is one of the few surviving churches of the " old-fashioned " type with high square pews, a roof resembling a ship's cabin, and a " three- decker." In the churchyard are some unique epitaphs, • hut the following is so remarkable as to warrant repro- duction :</p>
        <p>IHere lies the bodies of Francis Htuftrodds and Mary his Wife who were both bwn on the same Day of the I Week Month and Year (viz.) Septr ye 10th 1600 Marry'd on the day of their Birth and after having had 12 Children born to them died Aged 80 Years on the same day of the year they were born September yelOth 1680 the one not above five hours before ye other. Husband, and Wife that did twelve Children bear, Dy'd the same day: alike both aged were; Bout eighty years they lived, five hours did part, (Ev'n on the marriage day) each tender heart. So fit a match, surely, conld never be, Both, in their lives, and in their deaths agree. There is a hardy, sea-faring community at Whitby —bronzed and bearded old salts of the genuine type. Their's is, indeed, an an active, open life Towards evening one sees the brown-sailed fishing boats glid- ing slowly out of the harbour to the fishing grounds. Earls- next morning the barb mr is all animation, for the toilers have returned. The trim little craft are laden with shining fish, and for a few hours the fishermen are busy salting and packing them in barrels ready for consignment to our great towns. During the day the boats float lazily in the harbour, while the crews are busy mending the nets or enjoy- ing a whiff of the fragrant weed on shore. To me the most impressive of sights at Whitby is when the tide comes in with all its grandeur on a breezy day. Then it is eKhilarating to walk along one of the two stone piers which project far into the sea and watch the seething waves as they dash against the wall, some- • times giving an unexpected baptism to unwary spectators who have ventured too near. On the piers one gets the full force of the salt-laden breezes which blow in from the sea. Little wonder is it that with such a rock-bound coast there are some magnifi- cent storm scenes in NV; nter. But in the past there have been heart-rending sights too. The gallant life-boatmen of Whitby are ever ready to respond to the cry of distress, and old inhabitants tell</p>
        <p>E. I.</p>
        <p>A negro, Corporal J. C. Smith, has proved himsel the best shot in the United States Army. In slow firing he made 181 out of a possible 200. and In rapid firing 97 out of a possible 100,</p>
        <p>of a memorable and fateful night when after going WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE just as many times to wrecks, the lifeboat capsized, as it was nearing home, and all but one of the crew , OVERCROWDING. were drowned. Does history record truer heroism! that that which in a single night plunged a whole ' _-- town into mourning? i EXTENSION SCHEME AGIIIN BROUGHT I have said 'little about new Whitby because it is , the ancient part—" The haven under the hill "—that I FORWARD BUT DEFEATED. appeals to me most. On the west cliff, where palatial --- hotels and boarding houses have sprung up, there isl The Administration of Out-Relief. modernity without its boisterousness and, leaving the i The proposal to meet the accommodation difficulty at town behind, one can walk for miles on the high cliff the Warrington Workhouse by the erection of a new tops or along the sands. The Whitby legislators are infirm block at a cost of £8,430 was again before the not great advertisers, and there is none of the dexter- Board of Guardians on Wednesday, but after a long ous angling for the day trifSper which tends to spoil discussion it was decided to again defer the scheme. many places. So the town still retains that peaceful- The meeting was presided over by Mr. J. Billin,ge and there. were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, ness which inspired Mary Linskill when she wrote • Miss Broadbent, the Rev. Dr. Mercer, Dr. Patterson, its praises. That it may long do so, is my sincere ' Messrs. W. Hickman (vice-chairman), A. J. Jolla s', 11. wish. Forshaw, J. W. Knowles, T. El. Ashcroft, J. Payton, P. Whitby, however, is only the key to a wide district Longtone G. Thompson, R. Bolton R. Flannagan, W which abounds in natural beauties. I had often been Houghton, A. Brooks, G. Mather, ,T. Ritchie, J. C. warned not to attempt to explore the country per Sutton (clerk), and J. F. Awbery (master). bicycle, and friends who were "in the know ' drew l Notice hal been given that the following resolution