d as soon as the new block was built it would bees crowded as the present buildings. It would trinity of visiting the grey old city of York and its stately Minster. I have tried to convey to my readers be a mistake and a blunder to erect the buildings at a picture of some of the most favoured spots in our present, and they would not serve the desired purposea little island, and if more Warringtoniane are inducsd He moved that the scheme be not proceeded with at present, to visit "The haven under the hill" lam sure they Mr. KNOWLF,S seconded. He felt that if a little will not be disappointed, more out-relief were granted to aged persons, there would not be so many people in the Infirmary to-nay. They were told that the patients could be kept very cheaply in the Infirmary but when they took into account all the expenses of the nurses, medical treat- ment, buildings, machinery, itc„ they would find that the cost He head would he a good share of £1 per week. He felt very strongly against such arguments as Mr. Forshaw appeared to put forth:—" Give us plenty of house accommodation and we can either starve the people outside or force them to come into the house.' The Guardians hed a duty to perform—not to look at the scheme. entirely from the point of view of saving money to the detriment of the poor, but to use discrete kindliness. It was a terrible thing for aged men who were willing to work but could not obtain employment to be told that they could come into the House. Out-relief judiciously administered would, he believed, avoid the filling of the Infirmary. If they spent £40,000 on a building he ventured to say that in twelvemonths time it would be full. Provide what accommodation they would and they could always fill it. They should try to do with the existing accommodation, and not go into extravagant expenditure. Mr. JOLLEY said he regretted that Mr. Hickman should have failed to carry out the promise he made a fortnight ago to bring forward another alternative con- structive scheme. He, however, cheerfully recognized the perfectly straightforward tone in which Mr. Hick- man had moved the amendment, carefully abstaining from any insinuations against the motives of those who differed from him. Mr. Hickman said that recent legis- lation would stop the influx of able-bodied men to the Workhouse. Even if that were true, it, did not in the least degree affect the point at issue. It was not a question of able-bodied men. He defied Mr. Hickman or anyone else to find able-bodied men in the house. The whole policy of the Board for years had been to hear constantly in mind that the house was not for able-bodied M 33, who could maintain themselves, and it was not true to sly that th,e.o wets able-bodied mm there. Mr. Hickman had stated that the-e were more empty beds and rooms than there was in March. "het ought to be so, because if there was a slack time in the year it should be in July and August. He also said there were 51 patients eligible to be removed from the hospital as they were not receiv- ing medical treatment. That was surely an argument in favour of the provision of the new block, as at present there was no suitable building to which they might be removed. The rooms in the old Infirmary, except a few on the female side, were absolutely full an 1 over crowded last winter. It should be remembered that those who advocated the extension were ratepayers and were as much interested in keeping down the rates art anyone else. The scheme was the result of careful and prolonged deliberation, and was recommended, after considering various alternatives, as the cheapest and I most effective way of meeting the difficulty. The only alternative left was that which Mr. Knowles had ad- SERIOUS ACCIDENTS AT Sr. HELENS, i voci.tecl—the granting of out-relief. To administr John Maddison, collier, aged forty, of 101, Herbert- that relief properly they must he able to apply the street, Sutton, was on Tuesday admitted to the St, house test to thos