        <p>out-relief was going to he economical. Oat-relief was not cheap; iewas not effective ; it was demoralizing ; and if freely administered tended to lower wages. The best friends of the working classes, the leaders of thought among workingmen,were as stronglyjopposed to the free granting of out-relief as the most strict political economist. bemuse they knew what it roe mt—they knew the evils it had wrought in the past. Those who posed as economists and friends of the working classes and</p>
