      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MESSRS, A. F. AND S. A. PEACOCK.</head>
        <p>MESSRS, A. F. AND S. A. PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>Meeting of Creditors.</p>
        <p>The "Drapers' Record "of August 10th, publishes the following On the 10thinst. the following circular was issued by Mr. F. R. Roberts, solicitor, Egypt-street Chambers, Warrington : I have been consulted by Messrs. A. F. and S. A. Peacock, trading in West-street, Warrington, as whole- sale drapers, as to their financial position. On examina- tion of the figures supplied by my clients, lam satis- fied that they ought to take their creditors into their confid3nce and ask for their opinion as to the future conduct of the business. To this they have agre3d. It is proposed in the first instance to confer with creditors whose debts are not less than £lOO, and I therefore beg to invite you to attend a meeting of such creditors to ,be held at the Home Trade Association Rooms, 15, High-street, Manchester, on Monday next, the 11th inst. I at 3 p.m. I have instructed Messrs. Joshua Jones and Company, accountants, of York-screet, Manchester, to , prepare and lay before the meeting such a statement as to the debtors' position as it is possible in the time at their disposal. At the meeting an approximate statement of affairs was, we understand, submitted, which disclosed liabili- ties amounting to about £15,800. Of this total £8,350 was claimed-by trade creditors, £6,000 by the bank, and , £4OO by cash creditors. The assets were stated to com- I prise stock. £7,440 ; fixtures, &amp;c., £BO ; plant, &amp;c., £275; I horses and carts, etc., £5O ; book debts, £703 ; cash in , hand, £8 ; and the warehouse property, £l,OOO, er a total of £9,382, showing a deficiency of £8,220. Ps stated in the circular, the meeting was only attended by creditors whose claims amounted to over £lOO. It was, we understand, resolved that Mr. r. W. Moss, chartered accountant, Manchester. should undertake an investigation with the assistance of a committee consisting of the representatives of Messrs, S. M.Emmanuels and Co., Messrs. J. N. Phillips and Co., Ltd., Messrs. A. and S. Henry and Co„ Ltd., Messrs. Fred W. Millington, Ltd., and Messrs. T. , Cooper and Co. The investigation will, it is expected, take some little while to complete. It will be possible, it is anticipated, to convene a general meeting of the whole of the creditors—who are very numerous—in about a fortnight's time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>advocated the free granting of out-relief advocated that which was against the best interests of the community and against all social principles. Lancashire had for many years stood nearly at the top of the tree in the adminis- tration of the Poor Law but Warrington was not up to the average of thelcouutrv. There was,however,no reason why it should not be. The real extravagance rested with those who opposed the scheme and who, quite unconsciously were wasting hundreds of pounds annually of the ratepayers' money. In three years the out-relief had gone up by £8.50. How was it that their , expenditure was equal to 2d. in the .£ more than it was three years ago ? Mr. KNOWLES : Everything is rising. Mr. JOLLEY admitted that a portion of the increase was due to changes of legislation, but he asked the Board to consider the question seriously, and remem- ber that their half-yearly call was higher than the , average for the county. Flu had not the least hesitation in saying that the expenditure was higher than it needed to be, or than it would be if the administration inside could be made efficient by the provision of the accom- modation which it was proposed to supply for the infirm inmates. Hr. Elocc,a rox said the inmates were entitled to humane treatment at the hands of the Board. Was it a humane idea to convert potato stores, a paint shop and 1 other outhouses into an infirm block? He did not know whether or not the piggeries had been inspected with a view to conversion. They could not, he contended, conscientiously ask people to Coale into the hospital, , and then house them in such a building. He also sup- ' ported the scheme for the erection of a new building as</p>
        <p>Laing the most economical one. Mr. THOMPSON said he was surprised that Mr. Houghton should compare the old Inarmiry_ with the piggeries as that Infirmary was built under the sanction of the Local Government Baird. When the new Infirmary was opened a lot of people who might have remained in the old building were rushed over into the new premises. On Jane 30,,h six years ago the hospital was stated to be overcrowded. If it had been over- crowded so long it would not take any harm for another year or two.He was against the extension chiefly for the reason that they did not know what the new legislation might work out for the': Able-bodied men were, he c tended, taken into the House. They had men working there who would be taken out of the place if the [Them- plowed Bill were put into operation. Mr. ASHCROFT said they had tradesmen such as joiners, who were able-bodied man, in the House to-day. Mr. JOLLEV: Of what age? Mr. Asurcnorr:_. About fifty.</p>
        <p>Mr. ;JOLLEY': That is untrue. The CHAIRMAN said that in the interests of the sick poor and of better administration, and in the larger interests of the ratepayers, he believed the scheme was the best and cheapest. On a vote being taken the amendment to defer the scheme was carried the following 10 voting for it : Mrs. Wilkinson, Rev. Dr. Mercer, Mesars. W. Hickman, J. W. Knowles, J. Payton, R. Bolton. T. H. Ashcroft, P. Longton, G. Thompson, and M. W. Seton. The members who did not vote in support were The Chairman. Mrs. Clay, :arks Broadbent. Dr. Patter- son, Messrs. Flannagaa, W. Houghton, R. Fershaw, A. J. Jolley, and A. Brooks. Messrs. 0. Mather acdT.Ritchie had left the meeting,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-08-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Wells —Spann. Holloway—Spann. On Saturday a large gathering assembled in Bold- 1 street Church, Warrington, to witness a double wedding, 1 the two daughters of Mr. Richard Spann, carriage builder, being married. Both brides were well-known, having been connectud with the choir and Sunday , school for a number of years. Miss Mary Hannah Spann was married to Mr. William Henry Wells, 1 youngest son of Mr. John Wells, Froghall-lane. She I was given away by her father, and wore a pale blue eolienne dress, trimmed with Paris lace and insertion. 1 She wore a white hat with ostrich tips, and carried a 1 shower bouquet of lilies of the valley and roses, the • gift of the bridegroom. She also wore a gold brooch ! set with pearls, the gift of the bridegroom. Her bridesmaid was Miss Maud Wells, sister of the bridegroom, who was dressed in a biscuit ; coloured voile, trimmed with Brussels lace and ' silk. She wore a black picture hat, and carried a shower bouquet of corn flowers, the gift of the bridegroom. She also wore a gold lace pin, the gift of the bridegroom. The best man was Mr. Gamble, 1 brother-in-law of the bridegroom. Miss Jessie Spann was married to Mr. Wilfred Holloway, third son of Mr. Samuel Holloway. Folly-lane, Warrington, She was given away by her father and was attired in.a fawn silk 1 taffetta dress, trimmed with chiffon and silk lace, and wore a hat to match trimmed with pink roses. She ' carried a bouquet of lilirs of the valley, and wore a gold 1 bsngle, set with diamonds and rubies, the gift of the ' bridegroom. Her bridesmaids were Miss Fanny Spann ; (sister) and Miss Ruth Mary Spann (three-year-old ! niece of the bride). The former wore a dress of grey silk voile, trimmed with pink silk and Brussels lace, and white felt hat, trimmed with chiffon , and lace to match, and carried a shower bouquet of 1 pink carnations, the gift of the bridegroom. The?other 1 bridesmaid was daintily attired in a pleated silk chiffon dress, trimmed with Parisian lace, and wore a Victorian ' bonnet. She carried a bouquet of sweet peas, carna- tions and maidenhair fern. Both bridesmaids wore 1 gold heart pendants, the gifts of the bridegroom. The 1 best man was Mr. S. Holloway, brother of the bride- ' groom. The Rev. Joseph Manning performed the cere- monies. The reception was held in Toft's Assembly Room, about forty guests being present. The happy couples afterwards left for Blackpool, where thehoney- moon is being spent. Mrs. Wells's travelling dress was a dove-coloured costume with fawn hat, trimmed with lace and poppies. Mrs. Flolloway travelled in a dark grey tweed costume with black hat, trimmed with ostrich tips. Numerous handsome presents were received. Haviour. —Bosehier. An interesting wed ling W 1.3 sol.unrd at WyCliffe Church on Thursday, ths contracting parties being Miss Bertha Boticnier. d nighter of Mr. Joseph Wallace Bauchier, " Wellfield," Stockton Heath, and Mr. Harry Haviour, Warrington. There was a large congregation to witness the ceremony which was performed by the Rev. John Yonge. The bride, who was given away by her father was becomingly attired in a dress of white lawn and a lace hat to match. She carried a bouquet of marguerites. The bridesmaids were Miss E. Haviour (sister of the bridegroom) and the Misses May and Bessie 13ottchier (sisters of the bride). The first two wore dresses of creme voids and hats to match, and carried bouquets of choice flowers. Miss Bessie Bouchier wore a dress of white silk. The bridesmaids ornaments were gold signet rings, gifts of the bride- , groom. Mr. fo, Porbuore ably discharged the duties of bast man. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., was at the organ, rani played suitable selections of music, including Mendelssohn's " Wedding March." The reception was held at" Meld." after which the happy couple left for Llanfairfechan, where the honeymoon is being spent. 1 The following is a list of presents : Father of bride, cheque and Georgian silver spoons Mother of bride, cheque Father of bridegroom, oak sideboard Mother of bridegroors, cheque Bridegroom to bridesmaids, signet rings Mr. F. Podmore, silver claret jug Miss May Bonchier, silver afternoon tea spoons Miss Bessie Bouchier, Chippendale chair Mr. W. Wallace Bouchier, cake stand Miss Edith Haviour, secretaire Mr. and Miss Clarke, drawiir, room chair Miss May and Aida Clarke, barometer Mr. and Mrs. and the Misses Percival (Liverpool), afternoon tea kettle Mrs. Percival (Bolton), cushion Mr. and Mrs. H. Wallington, oak tea tray Miss Lily Wallington, brass pan Mrs. Grey and Cousin Polly, jardiniere . Miss Emily Grey, tray cloth Mr. and Mrs. Ed. Wallington and family, silver fruit knives and forks , Mr. Harold Wallington and Miss Kershaw, bed spread Mr. and Mrs. England, preserving pan Misses Mabel and Marion England, table Mr. W. England, Brigilian shell Mr. and Mrs. acid Miss Conley, silver fish eaters Dr. and Mrs. Howard, silver sugar bowl and sifter Mrs W. B. Roberts, copper flower pot and stand Mrs. C. J. Holmes violin stand Mrs. A. Willett, bread board and knife Mrs. Sutton (Manchester), embroidered handkerchief Mr. F. H. Crossley, embroidered cushion I Mr. Oswald Garside, picture Mdlle. Helene Tharratt (Paris), pewter cake dish Mr. apd Mrs. T. J. Lee, afternoon tea cloth Mr. and Mrs. A. Landon, gas fire Mrs. Hunt (Hull), d'oyley Mrs. Batman (Leeds), embroidered tea cloth Mr. and Mrs, Deakin, silver butter knives Mr. and Mrs. Caldwell, copper kettle Miss V. Taylor, cushion covers Mrs. Chritchlev, cushion Mrs. Davies, silver butter knife Miss Louie Davies, clock Rev. J. Yong.e. Wordsworth 's poems Miss F. Beswick, breakfast tray Mrs. W. Wallington (Latchford), d'oyleys -Miss Julie Dreher. silver butter dish Mrs. Wilson, silver photo frames Miss Wilson, afternoon tea cloth Miss Thompson, table centre Miss E. Howard and Mr. Davenport, chair Mrs. and the Misses Robinson, silver cake dish Mr. Robinson, silver spoons and sugar tongs Mr. and Mrs. Farrar, toilet service Ma. J. W. Scott, eiderdown quilt Miss Miles (Leeds), copper bowl Miss Beatrice Lees, tea and coffee stand Mr. It. H. Pearson, violin stand Mr. and Mrs. E. Lloyd-Jones, cheese dish Mr. and Mrs. Edwin Bradshaw, Limoges fruit dishes Miss Minnie Thornton, d'oyleys Misses Pigot, music stool Mr. and Mrs. W. Haig, photo frames Mr. and Mrs. T. Williams, antique Staffordshire hand- painted china', glass flower basket and dioyleys Mr. and Mrs. W. Ford, china fruit dish Itev..T. S. and Mrs. Hughes damask table cloth Mr. A. Miles (Leeds), art books Miss Annie Wallington, afternoon tea table</p>
