: 1, A. Broady; 2, W. Downs. One geranium : 1, J. Owen ; 2, John Cart- wright. One lily : 1, J. Fairhurst; 2, John Cartwright. One gladiolus : 1, A. Broady ; 2, H. Lyons. Dish of shallots : 1, T. Brindley ; 2, A. Broady ' • 3,J. Astall. Six spring onions : 1, A. Broady •, 2, H. Lyons ;3, J. Moore. Six autumn onions : 1, J. Moore; 2, H. Lyons; 3, E. Vernon. Twelve pods of peas : 2. H. Hallard. Twelve pods runner beans: 1, H. Lyons ; 2,H. Brockle- hurst ; 3, H. Hallard. Twelve pods of dwarf beans: 1, A. Broady ;2, H. J. Hopkins : 3, A. Rathbone. Six ' pods broad beans: 1, H. Brocklehurst ; 2. H. Hallard. One red cabbage : 1, H. Lyons ; 2, F. Timperley ; 3, John Miller. One white cab- bage : 1, T. Brindley ; 2, H. J. Hopkins • 3, ! John Miller. Two cos lettuce : I, H. J. Hopkins; ,2. J. Whitley. Two cabbage lettuce: 1, H. Lyons. Three sticks celery : 1, A. Broady ; 2, James Owen ;3, J. Astall. Tweke potatoes : 1, J. Astall ;2, E.Vernon; 3. T. Brindley. One marrow : 1, H. Brocklehurst ;2, John Miller :3, W, Worrall. Two cauliflowers : 1, H. Lyons; 2, E. Vernon. Six white turnips : 1, T. Brindley :2, J. Astall ; 3, H. Hallard. Six leeks : 1, John Miller. Three beetroots : 1, E. Vernon ; 2, H. Lyons ;3, A. Rathbone. Six parsnips : 2. H. Hallard. Collection of six vegetables : 1, E. Vernon •, 2, J. ! Moore. Group of plants and flowers : 1 ,Frank Baker; 2, John Isherwood ;3. George Moss. Group of plants: 1, James Owen ; 2, E. Vernon ; 3, W. Downs ; special ! prize, John Isherwood. Praise is due to the .officials of the society for the admirable, arrang.ements for the show. They are :- ' President, Mr. Vera B. Davies ; vice-presidents, Rev. H. V. Pigot, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, and Mr. Leonard G. 1 Davies • committee, Messrs. C. Plinston, A. Broad, F. Timperley, F. Anslow, T. Edden, W. A. Timperley, W.H. ! Woodcock, J. Moore, E. Vernon, S. Booth, J.Ryan' and ! A. J. Willett ; hon. treasurer, Mr. C. Plinston and hon. secretary, Mr. A. Fawcett.</p>
        <p>Penketh Flower and Bird Show. The members of the Penketh and Sankey Window Cottage Garden, and Cage Bird Society held their four- teenth annual show at Penketh on Saturday. The exhibits were arranged in a large marquee and were of a high quality, especially in the cut flower classes. The show was made additionally attractive by the excellent arrangement of plants, fruit. vegetables and flowers, which were sent by Messrs. C. E. Parker, J. Gerrard, J. B. Aitken, and Miss Bolton. Flowers, fruit, vegetable, &amp;c., were also sent for exhibition by Messrs. William Byrom and Sons, Liverpool-road Nurseries, Bold: C. W. Higgs, Joseph Carman,l James Waine Caird, Mrs. R. Garnett, Mrs. Higgs, and Miss Warburton. Special prizes had been given by Messrs. J. B. Aitken, Eustance anrl Co., J. B. Baker. J. Irwin, W. H. Podmore, A. Seed, H. Whitfield, P. Heath, H. Cawley. C. Houlton, Carter and Sons, J. Fisher, Garnett, Caldwell, G. Starkey, G. Davies, G. Bates and Mrs. J. Stanley and Miss B. 11roxton.</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray presided over the opening ceremony which was performed by Mr. D. Houghton,Grandmaster of the Order of Druids. In declaring the show open Mr. Houghton said he- considered that the influence of the show, had a wonder ful training and educational effect behind it, and immensely improved the tastes and desires of the people. (Applause.) It would be easy to enlarge upon the usefulness of the show, but he would merely add that it was bound to have an effect upon their children that would inculcate in them healthy tastes and desires. How heartily, then, could they offer to the founder and officers and committees their congratulations upon the success attained, and hope that the experience of the future might be met with the same cheerful result. (Applause.) Mr. Houghton was heartily thanked for his services as was also Dr. Murray for presiding. During the afternoon and evening the Liverpool Leyland Pierrot Troop° rendered art excellent pro- gramme. The Penketh Tannery Prize Brass Band, under the conductorship of Mr. Harper, played selections and also played for dancing. PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>Gardens.-Best kept large cottage flower garden : 1, and two specials for best garden and best variety, W. Dutton; 2, S. Moss. Best kept small cottage garden with greenhouse : 1, J. W. Walters ; 2, A. Birch. Best kept small cottage garden without greenhouse: 1, J. Hall ; 2, R. Aldcroft. Best arranged and cultivated kitchen garden : 1, J. Goulden, sen. ; 2. J. Turner. Best arranged and cultivated allotments, Sankey : 1, J. Turner ; 2, R. Talbot. (Prizes in last class given by Mr. George Higgs.) Best arranged and cultivated allot- ments, Penketh : 1, J. Carman ; 2, F. Goulden. (Prizes given by Penketh Tannery Company). Plants.-Three window plants in pots or baskets : 1, Joseph Leigh; 2, J. Davies. One window plant (foliage) 1, J. Davies ; 2, A, Goulden. One window plant (flowering) : 1, W. Jones ; 2, J. Davies. (Prizes in above two classes given by Miss Bolton). Four cottage greenhouse plants : 1, I. Hampson ;2, D. Bonehill. Best cottage garden greenhouse : 1, J. W. Walters ;2, S. Moss. Best stand of plaasts and cut flowers from cottage garden and greenhouse : 1, A. Seed. Cut Flowers.-Six cottage greenhouse roses : 1, J. Waine ;2, W. Dutton. Six roses (hybrid perpetuals) : 1, J. Carrington ; 2, W. Dutton. Six roses (hybrid teas) : 1, W. Dutton; 2, Joseph Carrington. (Prizes in above three classes given by Miss Bolton). Three roses (hybrid perpetuals) 1, Joseph Carrington ;2, J. Goulden. Three roses (hybrid teas) : 1, J. Davies ;2, A. Seed. Six Asters: 1, S. Moss ; 2, W Dutton. , Three spikes of stocks: I, J. Hall: 2, T. Hampson. Six dahlias, show or fancy: 1, W. Dutton; 2, .1. Goulden. Six dahlias, cactus: 1, J. Goulden; 2, R. Talbot. Six dahlias, potnpone : 1, J. Carrington ; 2, S. Moss. Six dahlias, single: 1, J. Goulden. (Prizes in above four classes given by Mr. J. B. Aitken.) Three dahlias, show: 1, J. Goulden; 2, W. Dutton. Three dahlias, cactus : 1, S. Moss; 2, R. Talbot. Three Idahlias, pompons 1, J. Goulden; 2, S. Moss. Three</p>
        <p>carnations: 1, J. Goulden; Z H. Bingham. Three' picotees : 1, W. Dutton. Three gladioli: 1, A. Skellhorn; 2, A. Seed. Three spikes of phlox.: 1, J• , Carrington; 2 J. Davies. Three hollyhocks : 1, NV- Dutton ; 2, J. Carrington. Three distinct sweet</p>
