        <p>Eggs.-Twelve eggs, white: 1, A. Seed; 2, A. T. Clay. Twelve eggs, brown : 1, A. T. Clay. Breadmaking.-Loaf (home made), not exceeding 21b. : 1, Mrs., Midwood ; 2, Mrs. Dutton. Loaf (home made), baked in 21b. tins, from Messrs. J. Fairclough and Sons' " Dreadnought" flour : 1, Mrs. Hobson ; 2, Mrs. Dutton ;3. Mrs. Cheetham. (Prizes in last class pre- seated by Messrs. J. Fairclough and Sous, Ltd. Honey.-One pound jar of this season's honey: 1, J. Richardson. Cage Birds Section.-Canary, Yorkshire yellow, clear ticked or unevenly marked, nest feather (seven entries): 1 and 2, H. Cawley, Runcorn; 3, Houghton, Warrington ; , iv h c, J. Davies, Penketh ; hc, E. Peck, Runcorn. , Canary, Yorkshire buff, clear ticked or unevenly marked nest feather (six entries) : 1, A. Abbott, Farnworth ; 1 2 and 3, H. Cawley; 4, E. Peck; hc, J. Davies ; c, E, Houghton. Canary, Norwich yellow, clear ticked or I unevenly marked, nest feather (six entries) : 1,2 and vl3 c, R. Barker, Cuerdley ; 3, S. Andrews, ‘Vidnes ; hc, J• i Davies ;c, T. Fenny, Farnvvorth. Canary, Norwich buff. . clear ticked or unevenly marked, nezt feather (ten eateries) : 1, R. Barker ;2, and hc, J. Davies; 3 and , c, T. Houghton, Ditten ; vh c, F. Fenny. Canary, Norwich ) green, yellow or buff, self or foul, nest feather (seven entries): 1 and 3, S. Andrews ; 2 and hc, T.. Houghton ; v h c, J. Davies; c, I. Hampson, Penketh, Canary. crest or crest-bred, nest feather (five entries) • . 1, S. Andrews ; 2, 3 and vh c, R. Jenkins, Widnes Canary, any variety of old•bird (four entries) ; 1, J. fl, Leigh, Warrington ; 2, E. Houghton ; 3, G. Billington; Warrington; vh c; I. Hampson. Canary, mule any variety, any age (six entries) : 1 and hn, L. Houghton ; 2, 3 and c, I. Hampson ; vh c, G. Bi Ili ngton. British bird, any variety, hard bills, except birds of prey (five entries): 1 and 2, I Hampson ; 3, E. Houghton; v hc, J. Davies; c, G• Billington. British bird, any variety, soft bills, except birds of prey (one entry): 1, S. Andrews. Foreign birds, any variety, single or in pairs (three entries) W. Hall, Widnes ; 2, I. Lloyd, Penketh ; 3, J. Davies. Selling class, 10s., bird and cage' paid on sliding scale (six entries): 1, vh c, and c, S. Andrews; 2,R. Barker, , 3, J. Davies; la c. I. Hampson. A Scotch illustrated ki prize card was given to every first bird in the above classes. Novice Classes.-Yorkshire clear ticked or unevenls" marked, nest feather (three entries) : 1, and 2, J.- Potter, Warrington. Norwich clear ticked or unevenly marked, nest feather (three entries): 1 and 2, A. Young, Farnworth ; 3, J. Dodson, Penketh. i</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES &amp; ATHLETICS. Football. Next Saturday the football season opens at Warring" ton with Leigh as visitors, and seeing the resources et Warrington's command, there should be no doubt about the gain of two points. The team which won the cup. last April is again available, with a capable lot of reserves and promising juniors. Iklost of the first team players have already been seen out at practice, while some of the absentees in the persons of Shugars. D• Davies, and .T. Davies have now returned from Wales looking fit and well.</p>
        <p>Among the new players already signed on are Janls: Davies, of Penygraig, a three-quarter namesake of the reserve half back, Garside, a half back of Salford Adelphi, and Bibby, a three-quarter from Woolston• Garside has already been seen at practice, and ha&amp; created a very favourable irnpressio.i. Another half back from South Shields is expected, and Marshall], a young Salford three-quarter, is to have another trial.</p>
        <p>Dai Davies has been placed on the transfer list.</p>
        <p>There will be a practice match on the Wilderspool- road ground this (Saturday) evening at 6 o'clock, when most of the new men, including J. Davies, threequarterr will turn out.</p>
        <p>The "Athletic News" Football Annual is, as usual,ae admirable three-pennysvorth, consisting of over 200, pages. Rules, fixtures, club directory and secretaries addresses, records, Sze., under both codes are give together with a number of excellent photos of "cracks. An attractive football guide for the season 1905-G has been issued by Messrs. Hulton and Company. The fixtures both for the Association code and the Norther?, Union matches are admirably arranged. Excelleol cartoons are given and in addition the Oldham sew Warrington Rugby teams are reproduced,</p>
        <p>Clubs making preparations for the coming season would do well to consult a catalogue of accessoriea issued by Mr. Frank Stagg, Liverpool, the well-known athletic outfitter. Footballs of all descriptions all offered to the public at prices to suit all pockets all; from the top to the bottom all are guaranteed to sten; an unusual amount of wear and tear. The " Football King "also offers a large variety of jerseys and °the.; requisites at reasonable, if not to say low prices, aa' one could not do better than inspect Mr. Sages articlea before making a purchase.</p>
        <p>Cricket. The batting averages up-to-date are : Times Most not in Inns. , out. inns, runs. average; E. A. Connor Mason A. Thewlis .1. W. Bibby l5 1 ... 55* ... 214 P. B. Hawtin J. Thompson W. Richardson l9 ... 0 ... 41 ... 215 ... 134 Bowling. Sankey to-day have the stiffest, match of the swot:°. as they entertain the Bull's Head team on the Sanke.7 green. The probability is that they will win as it necessary that they should do so if they are to occupY the second position in the League table. The Church' street men are, however, determined to record another victory and thereby eclipse all previous league records. With two such determined teams it is difficult tct forecast the result. Penketh Park entertain the Cemetery men and have little hope of winning.</p>
        <p>The Mayor has granted permission for the Warrington and District Championship for the prize of £5 given Mr. E. England to he decided over the Corporation. green in Victoria Park. The contest is open to fon' representatives from every club in town and also frogl the Stag Club, Walton, and for the credit of the clubs who send representatives it follows that the best Men will be selected. An excellent competition is promisee! and some exciting games should be witnessed on SeP tember 16th between gentlemen who can be describe as the cream of Warrington trundlers.</p>
        <p>Cycling. Twenty n th members beorpsenoifntg run n her St. Hoelens Saturday Cable f a t cyclinge rnoclos joined,l7: Starting from Arpley at two o'clock, the party Dr°,,, ceeded to Mouldsworth, via Acton Bridge and Delans Forest. arriving at the Station Hotel about five. excellent tea had been provided by the President, 3"b F. Kenyon, who, owing to slight indisposition and muct, to the regret of the party was unable to be prese,r'e, The catering by Mrs. Fairhurst was thoroughly aPP`of ciated. The return home, was made by way e, Frodsham, Warrington being reached at half-past U 0 - The boisterous weather on Saturday deterred locs cyclists from turning out with the result that only 5,, put in an appearance for the Warrington Club run Crana,ge, but those who did brave the elements greaTt, enjoyed themselves. The club run on Tuesday Dig had to be cancelled on account of the rain.</p>
        <p>To-day the run is to Hunt's Cross, the correspondil, outing earlier in the season having been so enjos's" that a repetition has been decided upon.</p>
