        <p>• RUNCORN Boy's REMARKABLE FIND. A reralrkable find has baea mAde by a boy, named Janes Henry Moores, son of Mr. J. 11. Moores, a canal l watch man,o f 40,E gerto o-st reet,R uncorn,who has recently I been staying, in company with hii p treats. at the Gros- mill Cottage, C Awyn Bry, the home of some relations. It appe trs that ha and his cousin, Edward Ne wall, and a boy named Stanley Hugh,s (son of the heldmaster of the African Institute "),.were in the vicinity of the "black school" grounds, where tha students were ennaged in a game of tennis. One of the balls happened to be played over the wall, and fell into what is known as the Dingle." The temptation of a penny reward for the discovery of the ball resulted in the three boys ! climbing over a wall, and going down to the banks of a small stream in the wood. It was at the place they went to, hidden under a fallen bush, that Newell espied a casket. He secured it and took it to his home, where it was found to contain about £5O worth of jewellery, £5 10s. in gold, and two notes entitlin,, the owner to £lOO in each of two banks. A visiting lady afterwards identified the property which had been stolen from a house in the town. It is expected that the lucky boys will each rec.ive a substantial reward. It is stated that an arrest on suspicion of theft has been made.</p>
