        <p>THE EXAMINER. AUGUST 26th 1905.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. ... . Next Saturday cricket gives place to football. * * * : The Social Service Lealeue re-commences its meet- logs next month. ,4 Looking back upon the six months of the past .`• session, I, for one, cannot regard it as otherwise than , largely a waste of time.—Mr. Henry Norman in "The • World's Work." * * * r . During the past year the Parr Hall, has made the , handsome profit of g,I 1 7s. 11d. ! It is remarkable that with an income of nearly £7OO only such an in- ' '-,eignificant margin is left for the local exchequer.. - ' If the tram-rails over Warrington Bridge rise much .•-• higher above the pavement people will be obliged to • agitate for a" level " crossing; already the rails stand nearly as high as the metals of a railway in some . places. * e. • Congratulations to " our men in blue " on the -,, complete success of their sports! A venture with , such a worthy object certainly deserved well of the • public, and we are glad that the expectations of the e Promoters were fully realized *s* ' Will Liberals who have been served with notices of g objection to their votes by the Conservative agent Please forward them to Mr. J. Howes, jun., Liberal - agent,Liberal club, Bewsey-street ? By so doing many '' laims may be established without taking them into . Court, ,* 5 * The nuisance at the corner of Milner-street and ' ,Liverpool-road is not abated. "Beer bottoms" cannot - by any stretch of the imagination be likened unto the . roma from" otter of roses." We would like those .` 4 /*11c) are really fond of something out of the ordinary to get a sniff. Phew! r* * * The care of the poor is becoming an increasingly , . , burdensome charge on the thrifty. Workhouse exten- e sioos seem to be the order of the day. The Runcorn ' Guardians have sanctioned plans for extensions to fair large workhouse at Dutton, and it takes the 'economists all their time to prevent additional bricks, l, mortar—and rates—at Warrington. ' . 'Vegetarianism is becoming more and more popular. - ." Scorched corpse," as Mr. George Bernard Shaw has :called meat foods, is giving way to what is increas- ingly becoming considered to be a more healthy, , helpful and sustaining diet. Living on fruit and ; vegetables could well form a plank in the platform of f, the "Beautiful Warriugton Society." • 5, * 5 , The monopolists are in Parliament —all the influences ' that sustain idleness, wealth, and pride are there. The ~ anti-monopolists, the Socialists, all who believe in -'plifting the people and destroying the means of sus- lining the rich in their evil power, must go there also. . They must drive the supporters of monopoly out of ; Parliament, as Christ drove the money changers from .; the Temple. " For the Kingdom of Heaven suffereth violence, and the violent take it by force,"—" Labour Leader." ~_,. *5 * , An enthusiastic open-air meeting in support of the ve‘ candidature of Councillor Stott, was held in Orford- avenue last (Thursday) evening. Councillor Stott •: explaieed some of the work which he had accomplished during his brief term of office, and emphasized the . fact that he was a Socialist end believed in . kunicipalization. During the past year he had been Present at every meeting of the Council and Com- mittees which he had the privilege to attend— a record, certainly, which must be gratifying not only to himself • e hut also to the members of the I.L.P. * * * ~ 1 e I don't know bow you can find your way about nere, I'm lost," grumbled a fine strapping fellow, as ; he shuffled along Warrington's narrow Cheapside : with both hands in his pockets, about noon one Satur- ' - (lay recently. He was talking to his wife—a weary, f avorn-out looking woman with a basket on one arm, al ' 'lldeeping babe on the other, and a little girl hanging to her dress behind. Yes, " lost " he was in danger • of being. What a tangle it all seemed, and what had the future in store for that poor woman ? * * * . - We understand that an effort is being made to - introduce" young blood" as well as publicans into the ' Town Council. We know that one young gentleman t. who has for some years interested himself in sociol- , ogieal matters, has been approached and that he is seriously coosidering the request. There is plenty of 'eoPe for all those who are in earnest and are desirous O f lifting the town's affairs to a higher and nobler etandard. The Ist of November will soon be round and it behoves the "progressives "to gird their swords their loins. They must notleave things until the. iast minute. . * ~ * The documents relating to Lawson's contract with - „a Water Committee, which for three years Conn- 'nor Pembertm has striven to obtain a sight of, have en also refused the borough elective auditors,Messrs adshaw and Bolton. Consequently these two gentlemen have declined to sign the Water Committee's tr accounts, and very bald they look without- the anditorial signature. We shall probably hear more of t.,11,0 matter at the September Council meeting when 'l_,e abstract of accounts is" on the minutes.' When ,t'ane precious documents are eventually disclosed ''-uleV had better be sent to the museum ! * 5 * "• - Salford Northern Union Club has been, granted el&gt;e, rmission by the Northern Union to make a collec- at the next practice match, the proceeds of which . re to be divided between the Salford Royal Hospital .'- *at d the Warrington Infirmary. It will be remembered that Lambert, one of the Salford players, was an tElate at the latter institution for some weeks last • ,on, owing to an accident he sustained during the .; • ' eeatch between Warrington and Salford. The club is . %sirens of showing its appreciation of the careful -, 'attention of and services rendered to one of the players . and We congratulate it on adopting such a practical laethod. •.-- * * * - - Beautiful Warrington, forsooth ! While some of are dreaming and seeing visions of a beautiful '.. . e L-ringtert in the future, others of us would be thank- _fet have it as worthy of the name as it was, say a r years ago. For what with dense clouds of ~ 0 ack smoke turned out by steamers going along the ...apaalres, and dense clouds of white dust turned up by .to going along the roads just outside the borough ndarY, one almost despairs of the possibility of anyl ▪ Provement. As a housekeeper said the other day— • 1 : I.Lurtains require cleaning four times a year now in 1 nough:''"gehforil, and time was when twice was quite often' *5 * , unusual type of witness created some merriment Iv the Warrington County Court on Thursday. He „eaas apparently over-awed at the surroundings, . 'fe his evidence in very halting fashion, and quite , ehl'efle to appreciate the humour of a question which 'Judge put to him. He was a travelling round- ...,:out proprietor and,asked what he did with a certain _TY, he explained that he " put it in the van." "In part of the van ?" was the next query, and • ena promptly answered "In the shafts." " Oh• " - the blB Honour, " You don't put it in the bedroom „ it „;1, ? " No," replied the witness quite seriously, 'le could not get it in there ! " a *** Loyal Warringtonians have a duty before them and , re •,. th_ cord to equal. A correspondent states that during .. iti,e, I'., eek which ended on Saturday, August 5, not a . ;"-Rfe death was registered as having occurred in the ~ ' eirough of Ramsgate. This record is a remarkable the population at the present time must exceed persons. We exhort the 70,000 people in rleierrington to go one better and carefully refrain from for a period of seven days. Then perhaps we ~•'einei_oeeppe for a week being reminded that War- • telle4t n s death-rate was the highest of the 76 large ufilie &amp;c. We are sure that the Medical Officer iii eelth must be heartily tired of seeing such a jibe Print. It is not his fault, poor man! * * * o t,T.lieeelvetering of tram-rails is a subject on which the , ,s4a.u.,,,taii.rilrliycal Times " has lust published an article. the subject is of as much interest to cyclists 1 , Pleas tectr.ical engineers, and they will not be at all je , _. t nerve-destroying twist of a wheel which has .- ~with ther s somewhat ow.f.,,ethewwonder wet-rail deet contemptuous-rra ilf tohethe eh has ever "—says 1 ,'D n tile dangers , .raternit , „ . ~ the _ y are alluded to. Accidents y .allitnit°r " are so easily avoided by a trifling felt tlnrtt a care." Nru rianee of care, fallen foul of the wet rail. If not trie dnorie he shortly will to good purpose. Warrington , the PS might give him an opportunity of seeing what . .. Ilicevri et rail can do when at its worst. Here it is kept i_ pry watered for some mysterious reason, and there _nohably no place on earth with a tram-rail crossing Skid. which it is easier to suddenly and ingloriously</p>
